Parliament did not support an amendment liberating Tymoshenko
Ukraine's parliament today rejected the proposal to repeal, as part of an amendment to the Criminal Code, on the basis of which opposition leader Yulia Tymoshenko was sentenced.
The amendment, which would open the way to the liberation of the imprisoned ex-prime minister, was rejected by MPs at the second reading of a proposal to alleviate sentences for economic offences.
Tymoshenko was sentenced to seven years in prison in October for concluding an allegedly unfavourable treaty with Russia on the purchase of natural gas.
The verdict is not final, the appeal of the convict is due to be heard by the court in December.
Tymoshenko calls the verdict a political revenge of the regime, the suspicion of bias of the court provoked a trial even in the West.
The proposal to remove paragraph 365 from the Criminal Code, on the basis of which the ex-prime minister was sentenced, was supported by 147 deputies.
226 votes were needed to approve it.
Libyan Victory
The story of Libyan liberation, or the Libyan rebellion, which has its losers.
Muammar Gaddafi is buried in an undisclosed place in the desert and is after the war without him.
It remains to define the winner.
As is customary in the county, Islamists will prevail in the elections, but it is a question of whether moderate or radical.
The Transitional National Council has subscribed to Sharia's customs law, and we already have an idea of it.
Libye will become a crime-free country, because the theft is chopped by the hand.
Women can forget emancipation, possible apostates from the faith awaits execution and so on.
Instead of being a dictator, a society consisting of rival tribes will unify the Koran.
There's going to be order in Libya that we can't have imagined and that we certainly wouldn't want.
However, our way of life is not the only one, or objectively the best, and it probably wouldn’t suit the aforementioned Libyans for a change.
In fact, it is surprising that Islamic fighters took thanks from unbelieving dogs.
The only excuse is that, from their point of view, they were driven not by a general from America, but by Allah, whose greatness they celebrated with each successful intervention.
Therefore, when winners are sought in Libya, the West is not among them.
We took part in gunfights, served Islam, and our politicians got rid of a political ally in the person of the dictator without guaranteeing any profit.
After a fresh bad experience from Afghanistan and Iraq, we don't militarily occupy Libya.
As a result, there is no problem with expulsioning "crusaders".
But without the occupation of the oil company, they'll have Libya's black gold?
There may not be enough, and the West will finally be able to boast the selfless protection of human rights.
So, if those followers of the Sharia law don't thwart us.
A new era of crisis begins.
France and the rest of Europe came to the attention of investors
On Tuesday, a statistic whirlwind swept through Europe.
Individual states published GDP data for 3Q.
The Czech economy has stagnated quarter-on-year, Germany and France are growing.
Today, however, the bond market breaks records.
Investors are no longer looking only at Italy, but also Spain, France, Austria and others.
The statistical authorities of the euro area, Germany, the Czech Republic and some other countries of Europe published preliminary estimates of the development of the economy for the third quarter.
Germany's GDP grew by 0.5% expected, France by 0.4%.
At the same time, both countries adjusted the data for the second quarter, in which Germany, in addition to better than expected (a growth of 0.3% instead of the original 0.1%), France stated that it appeared in a slight decrease of 0.1% (the original data spoke of stagnation).
The Czech economy grew 1.5 percent year-on-year in the third quarter.
On a quarter-on-quarter basis, however, gross domestic product (GDP) stagnated, confirming the trend of a gradual slowdown in economic performance growth.
The results are worse than analysts' estimates.
The most important events:
The Czech economy grew 1.5 percent year-on-year in the third quarter of this year, but stagnated quarter-on-quarter.
Germany's third-quarter economy, according to preliminary and seasonally recalculated data, grew 0.5 percent.
Between July and September compared to the previous quarter, the economy of both the eurozone and the European Union grew by 0.2 percent, in line with analysts' expectations.
Greece's economy contracted 5.2 percent year-on-year.
Yield on its 10-year bonds is at 28.45%.
Yields on Italian and Spanish bonds are rising to critical borders.
Austria, the Netherlands, France, Belgium, etc. are also starting to feel the problems...
Yields on Czech 10-year-old bonds are below 4%, although the situation in the region is deteriorating.
There are voices from Germany that the ECB should be the creditor of last resort.
The Czechs qualified for the EURO.
In Montenegro they won 1:0 and celebrate the advance for 200 million
The football team has mastered the toughest task of the season!
In Montenegro, thanks to a great Czech and a beautiful goalie Petr Jirácek won 1:0.
Czech football, even with a great portion of luck in the second half, defended a luxurious lead from the home rematch and will again not miss the European Championship.
IHNED.cz watched the progress match in detailed reports.
Almost no excitement in the first half and a big portion of happiness in the second.
Thanks to the domestic destruction in the finish, the great Czech and hero Jiracek won in Montenegro happily 1:0 and celebrates progressing to the European Championship.
Due to the precise defense of the Czech team, the Montenegrins came under pressure only in the second half.
The underdogs of the match became Damjanovich with Vucinic, who burned two great chances.
Thanks to the great Petr Čech, the Czech national team will not miss the European Championship next year, in addition, a reward of about 200 million crowns will be added to the cash register of domestic football.
Since the opening minutes, there has been a lot of combative football.
Both teams were difficult to get involved in the combination and were played mostly around the halving line.
Therefore, the pressure of domestic footballers did not appear in the first half, because long kicks for the defense were precisely averted by the Czech defense.
Iranian students are prepared to die for their country's nuclear program.
They created a vivid shield
Several hundred undergraduates formed a living shield around a nuclear facility in Iran.
With their gesture, they want to express support for their country's nuclear program against a possible Israeli attack.
Students prayed at noon on Tuesday in front of the property and then proclaimed U.S. and Israel deaths.
Several hundred students of universities in Issahan, Iran, created a living shield on Tuesday around Isfahan's nuclear technology center, in which uranium is processed.
Thus, they indicate that they are ready to defend their country’s nuclear program with their own lives.
The International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) noted in a recent report that Iran did not appear to have stopped pursuing a nuclear weapon.
A possible attack on Iranian nuclear facilities is being talked about in Israel.
At noon at noon, students prayed and then proclaimed death to America and Israel at noon.
In the event of an attack designed to cripple Iran's nuclear program as much as possible, according to the Israeli press, in addition to the Isfahan Center, a facility in Natanz would also be aimed at enriching uranium.
Obese child's diet: doesn't eat and dinner sausages
More than a third of children between the ages of 9 and 13 have overweight problems, with 9 percent of schoolchildren having an excessive weight, and 5 percent of children are obese.
Obese children usually come from unhealthy families, according to the current survey.
Once again, it shows how important it is for a person's healthy development to the environment in which he grows up, and the patterns by which he is surrounded.
For example, sausages appear on tables in obese children by up to half more often than in families of children with normal weight.
Fried fries had for dinner the day before research associated with an awareness-raising event called Obesity is not a coincidence more than 19 percent obese.
It is also not customary to play sports in families, but this applies to most families of today's schoolchildren - only 15 percent of children replied that their parents find time for active movement at least once a week.
Exploration among 900 children
The survey took place at the end of last school year and was attended by almost 900 children from primary schools from all over the Czech Republic.
It was already the second year of the Obesity event is not a coincidence supported by the General Health Insurance Company and Unilever.
So far, more than 12,000 schoolchildren from grades 4 to 8 have been involved in the project.
Proper eating and plenty of exercise in the family is one of the determining factors, which causes a dramatically increasing number of children who are overweight or even obese.
"Only half of respondents, for example, said they eat five times a day in breakfast-moon-lunch-snack-dinner mode, which is one of the basic prerequisites for proper nutrition," reads one of the results of the survey.
Without breakfast and in the evening sausage
Experts point out that for healthy development are also essential relative details, such as a regular time of family breakfast.
In the Czech Republic at the same time, only 23 percent of schoolchildren’s families are having breakfast.
In most families, everyone eats breakfast on their own.
And the worst thing was to find that there's not having eat in a tenth of households at all.
Breakfast is the basis of the right daily diet.
The questionnaire showed that unhealthy fries are much more often consumed in obese children's families.
Almost everyone - regardless of weight - drinks sweet lemonades (70 percent) every day.
More than 40 percent of the children surveyed regularly play sports, but for their parents, the result was less than half - 15 percent.
Assemble your diet
Another part of the survey allowed selected children (204 boys and 172 girls out of 13 schools) to assemble their own diet according to their own taste and own opinion.
"The dietary preferences of Czech children between the ages of 12 and 15 are far from the ideal in terms of healthy nutrition."
Among their favorite dishes are a lot of floury, meaty and sweet dishes.
"In particular, the great popularity of sweet lemonades is alarming," according to the final report on the survey.
For lunch as the main meal, pasta won just above poultry.
Children, however, practically did not want legumes at all, and the popularity of fish and middial vegetable dishes is also low.
Inclination to health
At the same time, the survey showed that children are willing to adjust their eating habits if they get the right information.
"There has been a significant shift of preferences towards the principles of a healthy diet in a number of cases for the control group of survey participants who completed a questionnaire after the completion of the Obesity awareness-raising lecture," the project results said.
It was significant, for example, for sweet lemonades, where more children would begin to prefer ordinary water.
Children also ticked poultry and fish as the main meal of the day in the questionnaire much more often and would limit sweet foods and dumplings.
A stylist must also be a good psychologist, the pros say
In the introductory article about how stylists learn, I kind of complained that there was already a lot at the opening hours.
I didn't know what would happen next.
Lectures by three professionals in the field, dressing problematic characters and a seminar on how it goes on set.
And it'll get worse.
The closer to the final exam, the more information and practical tasks awaits us.
And even though the course takes place every Saturday and Sunday, it's really "trampled."
This time we were discussing (there is not a single boy in the course) how to dress a woman who does not have a perfectly symmetrical figure.
Such is the vast majority.
Someone has a short neck, someone has a boy's figure without proper curves and someone is overweight again.
The stylist needs to know how to optically soften the wide shoulders, lengthen the neck or add the necessary curves.
We were given tips on how to put the figure nicely on a woman like Jennifer Lopez, i.e. bigger breasts and hips and a narrow waist, and at the same time don't dress the woman too provocatively.
If, for example, the difference between the waist and hips is such that skirts or pants really do not fit, then it is worth having at least one such piece tailored.
You won't regret it, because perfectly fitting clothes are priceless.
You will feel much better in a tailor-made skirt than in one that is constantly shifting, rolling up, standing at the waist and the like.
In turn, the boy's figure can afford to wear clothes with sewing, applications, embroidery or pick-up.
Of course, there are many such information and types of characters, so I am glad that I have a whole week to sort out all the information in my head.
And I'm still afraid that, given the assigned homework, it's all the way.
Each of the students received an assigned celebrity, to whom she is supposed to invent two outfits: daily and evening.
We need to take into account the current appearance, the fashionable style of the personality and the type of figure.
I'll "dress" Miranda Kerr, you may know her from Victoria's Secret campaigns.
In my mind I'm cheering, for me personally definitely better than Britney Spears, Christina Aguilera, who's gained perhaps a hundred pounds recently (please take into account the exaggeration), or the younger Selena Gomez.
We have to cut the suits out of magazines or search the Internet and print them out, and then process them graphically.
Moodboard, as the whole result is called, will then also express our personal style and relationship to the graphical rendition.
Thinking about the graphical side of styling is also important.
The stylist does not only dress physically, but also creates, for example, so-called product pages in magazines (on a specific assignment, for example, trendy handbags, coats, etc.).
That's what stylist and designer Mario Kameník told us about.
But it wasn't until Sunday.
The stylist must have a general overview
Saturday's lecture packed with information about the types of characters was diversified by three guests in the field.
Stylist Honza Pokorný, fashion designer Jakub Polanka and fashion photographer Ben Renč.
Jakub reminded us that fashion is an illusion and the stylist should be able to take advantage of it.
And a handy stylist can work with one piece of clothing differently.
Variability is crucial in this case.
For example, such a trench coat (or if you originally want a trenchcoat) can be worn classically over jeans and a T-shirt as a cloak, but you can also put it on the bare body, put on pumps and take pictures like a dress.
Or you just need to roll up your sleeves or collars and immediately the whole look looks different.
"Play with it," he emphasized.
However, I most appreciated the following advice: "You need to know the rules perfectly for work."
"Then you'll start violating them," Jakub said.
Finally, it is clear to me why we are crushing all those rules, what to combine with, for what, why, when even in fashion magazines we sometimes see something else.
Not to mention the shows.
Thanks to the photographer Ben, I know that the stylist must have prepared a lot more variants of clothes than how many can be photographed.
So for the fashion story, which will have ten pages (i.e. ten outfits), I have to be ready to "stumble" at least fifteen.
And it also looks and works in combination with other pieces in a picture of clothes that is sometimes completely different than in reality.
So we have to take this into account when creating an outfit.
Honza Pokorný confirmed that the image and style sometimes makes up to half of the success.
Just look at Madonna, who is not exactly a miraculous singer, but image and marketing have made her a world star.
In the Czech Republic, the approach to the use of stylists' services is still rather cold.
Even well-known faces sometimes consider it unnecessary (it is enough that I can do something, what I look like is secondary).
And if someone gets styling from us, then we should count on the fact that it is very likely to dictate a completely different (smaller) size than it really has.
Then we have to google and find out if the tabloid did not write about the fact that the celebrity has grown/lost and so on.
Honza and Mario on Sunday told us that it is not enough for a stylist to orient himself in fashion and be able to assemble an outfit.
In his work he primarily communicates with the people he wears, so it is important to be a good psychologist.
To be able to guess people, not to pander, to stand up for your opinion.
"If you're ordered by a manager to sort through her wardrobe and complement it with nice pieces, you can't throw out her whole closet and say, for God's sake, that's terrible."
"Find at least a few things there you'll commend and recommend what they're worn for," Mario says.
He also specifically showed us what the so-called "plaque", or plaque bag, must contain.
It's the one you're going to fasten around your waist and you've got all the necessary proprieties with you.
In addition to bright things such as scissors, hair rollers or closing pins, there is also a nylon thread (with which the stylist assists in shooting products such as handbags or hats), special clips, which we fasten at the back of clothes, if it is larger (pin or other clamp are not permissible, they would damage the garment), or a sewer.
In order not only not to listen, but also to practically try something, we learned how to stick the shoes correctly.
Even when taking pictures in the studio, where there is a flat surface, the sole could be damaged, let alone outdoors.
And so we glued and glued.
We can't even touch our shoes so they don't get damaged.
We really need skill.
Mario delighted me when he praised my creation without reservation.
Mentioned skill is really not my friend.
This is just a small fraction of what we heard about the second weekend session.
It’s becoming more and more clear to me that it’s really not a rosé orchard to do styling on a professional level, no matter who says what they want.
In Need for Speed: The Run, they race across America.
Reviews
Originally a carefree ride, due to a series of completely unnecessary sloppy mistakes, turns into a routine morning trip to a shift.
The ambition was largely fulfilled, but it is of no use when no one apparently wanted to do anything extra.
The new episode, subtitled The Run, aroused high expectations before the release.
He promised that the age-old racing series Need for Speed would once again move a bit in a new direction, and from the playable demonstrations he appeared to be a game that might not only be in number in the genre of action racing.
We were also curious to see how the inserted passages will turn out, in which the main character gets out of the car and takes place on his own.
However, as is customary with the NFS series, many are a little different than we expected.
They're potentially decent arcade races, but the whole drags down a few unnecessary mistakes that have been appearing more or less in the series for years.
So it's actually not even mistakes, but rather a manifestation of some incomprehensible laziness of developers.
The adventure begins very well.
You are introduced to a story whose main character Jack has certain problems of a personal nature and as a solution offers participation in an illegal race across America.
You get behind the wheel on the west coast in San Francisco and your task is to arrive first in New York.
The idea brings with it several interesting elements.
The route is divided into ten stages, followed by more than fifty races.
It follows that each race takes place on a different track and the stages are set in different, sufficiently different and interesting environments.
It doesn't feel like the game offers the same thing all the time.
In addition, some of the environments are very nice to look at, although the technical level of graphics corresponds to the previous parts of the series.
So it's nothing world-class.
But driving across deserts, autumn landscapes or high in the snowy mountains seems visually very pleasant.
In addition, the races are diversified from time to time by some unusual element.
In the desert, for example, you get involved in a sandy storm, which greatly reduces visibility.
In contrast, in the mountains, you need to make sure that you do not have an avalanche.
In addition, individual races are quite well behind.
There are only a few, but that's enough.
For a while you try to overtake the required number of opponents (basically a classic race), then you catch up with time (a classic duel with stopwatch) and then a duel with a single opponent is waiting for you.
From time to time, the police, which makes your entire efforts even more difficult.
New are the passages out of the car.
It's more about interactive animations, when Jack usually flees from someone and you have to help him by printing the right buttons at the right moments.
It's nothing complicated.
However, it is surprising that there are far fewer of these passages in the game than you would expect (specifically, perhaps only three).
As a result, however, it is for the benefit of the matter, because this element has no chance to become annoying, and in the context of the above, it ranks among the elements that diversify the game.
The driving model follows in the footsteps of previous episodes.
This means that it is definitely not who knows what nowadays, but it can not be said that it is negative.
If you’re looking for a realistic simulation, you’ll have to look elsewhere.
But a more action-packed racing model still suits quite well, although we would be able to find a better in the competition.
Until then, everything is in perfect order.
However, problems are beginning to appear after some time.
In contrast to the driving model, it’s no longer a collision model.
We don’t expect to calculate complex deformations and collisions, but if you don’t have the slightest idea how your car behaves before any impact on any object, then probably everything is fine.
Typically, when overtaking or passing cars in normal traffic, sometimes you only lick each other easily, but at other times in an almost identical situation, your car ends up in wild pirouettes.
Or even attack.
Thanks to the restart system, it doesn't really matter, but it will upset you reliably.
The restarts are handled in such a way that if you smash or drive far off the track, the game will throw you back to the last clue on the track.
There are only five restarts in each race.
When you exhaust them, you have to start the race again from the beginning.
So it's more such an aid in case something goes wrong, rather that you can explicitly rely on them.
However, sometimes even restarts do not help, they will be solved by the need to learn the given section of the race by memory.
The big illusion of the game is that the same things often happen in the same places.
For example, you need to go through some corners in a specific way, otherwise you just can’t fit into them.
At other times you will meet "civilian" cars in the same configuration, whether you arrive there first, fifth, five seconds earlier or a minute later.
It annoys, especially in situations where you can see how between two oncoming cars the opponent drives in front of you easily, while when you get there, the cars are already in such a position that you have to go around or brake them.
It doesn't matter when you reach them, because it's "counted" so that the situation always happens just before your nose.
Opponents also have another advantage.
In the whole period of playing, you will come across several places where, again, regardless of time, the same opponent in the same place will overtake you.
It hits the most at the end of the game on a long bridge, where you're driving in the opposite direction.
The adversary has to get there in front of you, apparently at any cost.
When driving in front of you, it makes your overview of oncoming cars worse, so at first you are about a few times nicely greased.
It is possible to understand the intention to make the game more exciting and adrenaline, but the execution is so obvious that it quickly disgusts you.
And it's a shame, because despite the simple driving model, playing is fun and sometimes even so actionable that sometimes you can't literally keep track of where it flies.
And it doesn’t really matter whether it merits the concept with fifty unique tracks, shifting races so that boredom doesn’t have a chance or anything else.
An unconventional idea is to change cars during races.
You have to pay attention to gas stations by your travels, because that's where you can change the car, although in the vast majority of cases you can do with what you just have.
Cars are divided into three categories.
Sports cars are suitable for urban areas, strong U.S. crashes for straight highways and exotic supercars for technical riding, such as winding mountain paths.
The main story mode goes hand in hand with the mode of challenges, which are races in individual locations that you know from the story.
Here you get not only medals, but also experiential points to your profile, which is common for challenges and stories.
For obtaining a higher level of the driver, various items are unlocked: starting with cars and ending with backgrounds for the profile.
At the same time, it is reconnected with the online Autolog feature, which, among other things, compares your achievements with other players and provides additional community features.
One of the other hard-to-understand kicks is the situation from the last race in the story.
With your car you can get to the subway tunnel (so be it), where you drive it in the dark on the tracks (so be it).
On the tachometer, the numbers between 150 and 200 km/h are flashing (so be it) and a subway train rushes from behind and uncompromisingly scrambles you.
In the editorial office, we will probably still have a discussion for a few days about whether no one really thoughted about the creation of the game, or whether the authors think of us as such a big jerk.
The technical side of the game is nothing special, but at least the overall visual impression is in most cases very pleasant and in a few cases even great.
It freezes that in some more tense moments the imaging decreases, in some cases quite tragically.
However, this applies especially to crashes with a larger number of participants, when smooth imaging would no longer be of any use to you.
The sound side is fine and the soundtrack is a matter of taste.
In an effort to please the widest possible range of rather younger audiences, a soundtrack was created from the category "every dog different village", but it can be assumed that due to listening to the music, probably just someone will not buy the game.
The overall impression of Need for Speed: The Run is quite embarrassing.
If the game avoids completely unnecessary mistakes, then we would not be afraid to say that The Run is the best work of NFS in the last few years.
However, this is how the game is unfinished.
It’s fun, but be prepared for the fact that mistakes will smash into your eyes with the power of airport reflectors.
Singing choirs UK will be heard at a concert Students of Velvet
For the third time, the Festa Academica Festival offers a combination of the celebrations of the International Student Day and the anniversary of the Velvet Revolution with a musical experience.
During it, he will present himself at twenty high school and college choirs.
The main focus of the festival is two concerts held on 17 November.
"Charles University has a large stake in the festival."
"Among the participating choirs there is the Choir of the Pedagogical Faculty and the Faculty of Humanitive Studies, the festival committee will include Rector Vaclav Hampl," said Jakub Čaloun, the head of the organizing team.
The highlight of the whole festival is a free concert of high school choirs, which will take place on November 17 from 2 p.m. in front of the National Technical Library in Dejvice.
The programme includes the composition of the prominent Czech jazzman Karel Růžička Celebration Jazz Mass.
In the evening there will be a concert "Studenti of Velvet" in the ceremonial premises of the National House in Vinohrady, where university choirs, including choirs of Charles University, will perform.
Festa Academica aims not only with dignity to celebrate International Student Day and commemorate the 22nd anniversary of the Velvet Revolution, but also to present to the public choral music at the highest level and to develop the creative cooperation of young people.
The main organizer is the Union of Czech Choirs in cooperation with Jan Kepler Gymnasium.
Bohuslav Svoboda, Mayor of the City of Prague, and Václav Hampl, Rector of Charles University, took over the entire event.
The festival will take place from November 16 to 20 in Prague and Pardubice.
In addition to ceremonial concerts, the schedule also offers a variety of musical gatherings, student happenings, matiné and other programmes.
Detailed information can be found by those interested on the festival website www.festaacademica.cz
Ambassador to the EU Vicen:
The Czechs have a reputation as negativists and Eurosceptics
"The Treaty of Lisbon has greatly disturbed the balance between individual institutions."
"I didn't expect it to be such a tough war."
"It will take five, six years for the situation to stabilize a little, calm down and for all players to get used to their new roles," Milena Vicenová, the Czech ambassador to the European Union, said in a statement.
To the extent that she takes her motto "Comprehensively and openly about Europe", she showed already in the first part of the lecture, when she managed to describe individual EU institutions and their relations in such a clear and comprehensible way that a number of lecturers from the Faculty of Law of the Charles University could envy her.
Vicern also praised the work of the new Permanent President of the European Council, Herman Van Rompuy:
"At first there was criticism that it was bland, that it was a gray official, that he could not play any role, and I have to say that it took everyone's breath, that he performed the role excellently."
"He is a person who can listen to twenty-seven Member States, communicate well with them and has a clear vision of where the European Union should go."
She then explained the new post of High Representative for Foreign Affairs and Security Policy in response to the question: "Who is this European Union? Which phone number should I call?", that is, as an important step towards unification and greater legibility of EU policy towards states such as China or India.
No one wants to ban our cheese
Vicenová also devoted herself to the agenda, which the European Council lived, lives and will live in the near future.
Last year's Czech Presidency was a success.
The Czechs dealt with the oncoming financial crisis, solved the gas crisis or held a summit for the Eastern Partnership.
The fall of the government at this time was described as a big negative, which greatly damaged the Czech Republic.
Czech representation in the EU is very busy at the moment.
Speaking in the words of the ambassador, the "really frmol" was not only the presidency, but also the subsequent financial crisis, which is being dealt with to this day.
In addition, a new seven-year Union budget has yet to be approved, which is very complicated precisely because of the current economic situation.
Whether the budget will be slim and austere, which sectors will take precedence, which countries will be supported more and which less, it is all on the agenda for future negotiations.
The ambassador also recalled her main slogans, with which she came to Brussels: "About Europe clearly."
'I really don't like nasty acronyms, other people's words, a mischieve that no one understands.
How is a citizen supposed to understand that there really are no curved or round cucumbers invented in Brussels, bananas of various sizes or that someone really has to ban us."
It also wants to make better use of the EU environment and get more Czechs into European institutions.
We have a multi-speed Europe
According to the ambassador, the Czechs have a reputation as Eurosceptics and people who notice the negative than positive aspects of things.
Moreover, she regrets the low representation of Czechs in European institutions.
According to her, a lot of factors are to blame, for example the modesty of our candidates, their inability to write motivational letters, but also low support from the Czech side.
In addition to the successful Czech Presidency, it highlighted the strength and cooperation of the Visegrad Four.
"Believe me, when a Pole and a Hungarian come to me and a Slovak approach, the Frenchman next to me will say, "Hey, what are you meeting there, what about your Visegrad Four?" and he doesn't look enthusiastic about it at all."
In the future, according to Vicenová, the Union will face mainly new tough rules on the budgetary policies of the Member States.
He perceives the concept of a two-speed, if not multi-speed, Europe as very real, rather than a new "European federation."
"We've got eurozone countries and out-of-euro countries."
"We have a Schengen area, we already have an institute of enhanced cooperation."
"For the first time it has been said, let only the countries they want cooperate, and those who do not want, let them stand nearby."
"There will be more of these institutes, and it's going to be a strongly two-speed Europe," Vicen said.
In this regard, it has also praised the role of the Czech Republic and Herman Van Rompuy in enforcing a principle under which all topics that have an impact on the whole Union are to be discussed among all Member States, not only between euro area countries.
Brand story:
Innovator IBM begins the second century of its existence
If someone were looking for a word that most accurately expresses IBM's strategy, the closest thing would probably be "long-term."
For the general public, IBM is a symbol of the technological revolution, it is a company perceived as very contemporary, and yet a hundred years have passed since it was founded.
The basis of this long-term was laid by the company's first boss, visionary Thomas J. Watson Sr.
He's already coined the motto that a number of the world's problems can be easily solved if people are willing to think.
His motto "Think!" has become an imprint, around which the life of the future giant will unfold (and also the name of an internal magazine, which was published at IBM as early as 1935).
It was also the basis of Watson's carefully constructed corporate culture.
“For us from IBM, long-term thinking means a continuous movement towards the future.
IBM survived and prospered for a hundred years because it remained true to its core values and was not afraid to change everything else around it.
This has allowed us to transform technology, business and society during our first century.
We hope that in the second hundred we will be able to achieve even more,” said Samuel J. Palmisano, the company’s current director.
A sophisticated system
In the 1930s, Watson approached caring for his employees similarly to Bat'a in the Czech Republic - people from IBM were one of the first U.S. employees to have paid leave, employer insurance plus an elegant employee uniform and an elaborate system to reward the best inventors and merchants.
IBM has also had a long-term relationship with a customer.
From the beginning, the company was brilliant at mining talents - as one of the world's first companies, it abolished any discrimination, so it was able to choose.
In line with the anti-discrimination strategy, the current plan is that Palmisana will be replaced next year by a woman, Virginia Rometty.
She has been working at IBM for thirty years, so far she holds the position of head of sales, marketing and strategy.
In recent years, IBM has been making so-called jams, on-line brainstormings, from which many future strategies and innovations arise.
Not surprisingly, there are enough ideas - almost half a million people work in the company.
An institution with a good name
A firm position in the business is ensured by the company's strong internal and external image.
"From the beginning, IBM had the concept of an institution rather than just a technology firm," a Harvard Business School professor and the author of the book on IBM Rosabeth Moss Kanter told the Economist.
"IBM is not a technology firm, but a company that helps solve business problems using technology," added George Colony of consulting firm Forrester Research.
As the company itself says, there is a difference between entering the market and creating the market.
IBM, or "Big Blue," as it's dubbellious because of its logo in the United States - has grown into one of the world's biggest companies in a hundred years.
It is probably the most versatile technology company - developing hardware and software, infrastructure, hosting and consulting services in a range of products from servers to nanotechnology.
It holds the most patents of any U.S. technology firm, and five of its employees have won the Nobel Prize.
Its inventions include an ATM, a payment card, a financial swap, a barcode or a floppy disk.
IBM, with roughly $220bn, became the second-largest technology firm this year according to market capitalization (the first is Apple) when it overtook Microsoft for the first time since 1996.
Looking Forward
Not all products and innovations hit the tastes of customers and times - IBM, for example, did not make a bet on its own operating system OS/2, on-line services Prodigy or IBM PCjr computers.
"If your business is positioned to move forward, you can't emotionally attach yourself to the past," Samuel J. Palmisano notes.
That's why IBM doesn't have a hard time building its foundations on many platforms - and when they don't work, leave them.
Unlike the rival Microsoft, which in principle stands and falls on the Windows operating system.
Perspective Technology
IBM's revenues account for the largest share of services, after about a fifth they are made up of software and hardware.
In 1990, with more than halve, it was the strongest hardware representative.
Since 2000, IBM has sold $15-billion-dollar-dollar low-professional assets, investing $58bn in buying promising technology over the same period.
It has continued to invest in 2008, when the modern financial crisis swept through the world for the first time.
IBM is acting according to the legacy of its first boss, Thomas J. Watson, who also increased the volume of investment during the Great Depression.
So far, IBM seems to be going through the current crisis unaffected.
IBM's share price has risened 120 percent since the beginning of 2008, the firm's net profit is continuously rising.
IBM's corporate motto is to change everything, but not its values.
It can therefore be expected that even if another crisis comes, this flexible giant can handle it.
IBM's History: From Cheese Slicer to Space Flights
IBM (International Business Machines) was founded in New York City a century ago, originally called Computing Tabulating Recording Corporation.
IBM did not take the name until 1924 - it was originally a brand used for the company's offices in Canada and South America.
The basis for the later IBM was the fusion of four companies whose history dates back to the 1980s.
They owned technologies such as the famous "pricks", i.e. the clock recording the employee's arrival and departure.
The architect of the new company was businessman Charles Ranlett Flint, who also chaired the company's supervisory board until 1930.
Flint hired the head of a rival firm, Thomas J. Watson, to assist him with the company's management.
The leader then overshadowed IBM's founders - an invincible corporate culture and identity is still built on Watson's thoughts.
Watson led the company until 1952.
IBM, as its name suggests, started with the production and sale of all sorts of machines for the business - from meat and cheese slicers to cash registers.
In the first four years that Watson was the head of the company, the turnover was doubled to nine million dollars, and the extensive expansion of the company into all continents began.
Watson was so indispensable to the company that after almost forty years in the boss's chair he was replaced by his son, Thomas Watson Junior.
Under his leadership, IBM began working on the development of artificial intelligence, developing the first programming language.
In the 1960s, people and computers from IBM helped NASA drive flights to space - to Mercury, Saturn, and in 1969 the famous flight of man to the moon.
Meanwhile, in 1964, IBM developed IBM's first family of computers - the IBM System/360 series.
The classic "care" that established the computer standard of that time, called the IBM 5150, came on the market in 1981.
Twenty years later, IBM sold the production of computers to the Chinese company Lenovo, on the contrary, it bought the consulting part of PricewaterhouseCoopers and began to fully devote itself to consulting and services with high added value.
The strategy that has paid off for IBM over the past three years is the Smarter Planet concept, the "smarter planet."
Its aim is the smart use of technologies in unplanned areas and improving the situation, for example, in transport or urban administration.
For business, IBM recently introduced the "Smarter Commerce" platform, which helps traders manage the entire commercial cycle.
IBM has a strong history in the country.
She established her branch in Czechoslovakia in 1932 as the first country in Central and Eastern Europe.
Today, in addition to IBM ČR, it also operates a strategic outsourcing center in Brno.
The Headquarters for Central and Eastern Europe is located in Prague and there is also a world-class research team dealing with voice recognition.
Rychtářová about unfaithful husband:
Iveta, save the donkey, I don't want it anymore!
Darina Rychtářová (53) said the end!
After her husband Josef Rychtář (53) became not only the protector of Iveta Bartošová (45), but also a lover, Darina still hoped that Joseph would wake up from the pink dream and return home.
But now she doesn't want to wait and sets clear rules for the next life.
"Pepa can come home, but no longer as my partner," Rychtářová explained.
When Darina Rychtářová gave asylum to Bartošová in her house in the summer, she probably didn’t dream how her marriage would end.
Although Iveta moved out of Říčany after 30 days, Pepa did not leave the singer.
He's even publicly admitted that he loves her.
Over time Darina resigned, she reconciled with her husband's relationship, and now she even said goodbye to him forever.
Rychy doesn't care who his wife sleeps with
If Rychtář and Bartošová broke up one day, he would not be waiting at home for the open arms of his wife.
"He's the biological father of my sons, and if he wants to live his life in our house together, no one's going to expel him."
"However, he will live not like my beloved, stray old donkey, but as a person with whom I have certain ties," Rychtářová revealed, adding that she had already detached herself from her husband and insisted only on mutual decency.
Joseph doesn't mind that Darina closed his back door yet.
"I respect my wife's decision."
"I'll be with Iveta, and I'm not going to change it."
"My wife has set up a paper marriage, and I respect that."
"The company's moving on, I'm taking care of repairs, the garden, the property, I'm shopping."
"Darina and I are having a very normal conversation, except we don't share a bed with each other," Rychtář told Blesku.
If Rychtářová also finds a new love, she will not prevent her from doing so.
"We don't sleep together and I don't care where he's going, who he's going with and if he has a boyfriend.
I know it's my fault, I fell in love with Iveta and I take all the responsibility," Josef concluded.
Worst Female Habits: Do They Concern You?
When you ask men if they would be able to list some typical feminine bad habits, as a rule, they all put them very promptly from their sleeve.
Dear women, do you get to know each other in the following lines, where there are several feminine bad habits that can perfectly pump blood in men's veins?
Shopping
Does your other half blame you that you're addicted to shopping?
Do you have to buy at least one cloth a week?
Do you like to shop and in bulk in discounts?
Then, in your case, you can talk about shopping as a bad habit.
Some women are aware that they should at least tame their purchasing passion.
But say, who would resist just walking around clothing stores, looking for well-fitting uppers and then trying them all (ideally in different colors) in the booth?
Even if shopping is a hobby for most women, it can literally become a ready-made hell for men.
Just remember how many times you've told yours to wait for you at the entrance...
Constant discontent
“Aren’t I too fat?”
"I'd need to lose at least five pounds - should I start exercising?"
"Do you even like me?"
Don't these sentences remind you of anything?
Oh, yes?
Then you're one of those women who's obsessed with how they look.
It's not that you should stop taking care of yourself, but a lot of men are directly allergic to how their partners are constantly complaining about their appearance.
The stronger sex also coincides that the more the frequency of this complaining increases, the more annoying women are.
Order and purity in the first place
It's like an endless merry-go-round.
Washing windows, vacuuming, mopping tiles, wiping dust, scrubbing the tub - at least twice a week.
Do you blame your partner for stepping on a clean pavement with his dirty foot, for getting dirty with a scruffy sink and covering the glazed living wall, with whose polishing you had so much work?
In case you suffer from just this "deviation", try to realize that nothing should be exaggerated, and order and purity should definitely not stand within the family in the first place.
This harmless desire for purity can jeopardize your mutual relationships and family well-being.
Screeching
"Throw the used shorts in the basket."
"Are you going to wear those old socks tomorrow?"
"A wet towel doesn't belong on the ground."
If you dig into your partner several times a day, know that you are able to dial him reliably.
Of course, you don't mean anything wrong, you want to make sure that everything's okay.
Maybe if men knew, women wouldn't have to dig so much into them...
Gossip, gossip, gossip
For some women, gossip, gossip, as well as offending and conspiring against someone.
Women are focused on relationships, so they like to discuss things like who, where, with whom, how and why.
Perhaps each of us releases some small harmless gossip or gossip from time to time.
Hand on heart, it's inherent in all women.
The opposite, however, are gossip of the coarsest grains, who like and often gossip, perhaps despise everyone and are especially dangerous for their surroundings - especially within the women's working team.
Jagr and Voracek helped with assists to Philadelphia's win
Hockey striker Jaromír Jágr participated in Monday's NHL game with two assists to Philadelphia's 5:3 victory on Carolina's ice and is again at the forefront of the productivity of Czech players in the competition.
