We've had gaps in that," added one defender, P. K. Subban.
Czech goalie Ondřej Pavelec contributed thirty Winnipeg's hits to a 5:2 win over Tampa Bay.
Five different scorers shared the Jets' goals, interrupting a five-match losing streak.
How the prices of tours have evolved since 1989
Until 1989, only five state travel agencies were allowed in Czechoslovakia at that time: Čedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autoturist.
At that time, travel agencies were overwhelmingly only selling tours to socialist bloc countries, as travel to the west was very limited.
Czech tourists thus traveled on holiday to the sea to the GDR (German Democratic Republic), to the Soviet Union (Sochi in Crimea by the Black Sea), to Bulgaria, Romania (both to the Black Sea) and to Hungary (Balathon).
A bright exception was the period from 1962 to 1972, when it was also possible to travel to Yugoslavia, but then the Yugoslav regime leaned towards the West and the trips of Czech tourists to Yugoslavia were subsequently very limited.
The great paradox of the period of socialism was that the citizens of the socialist bloc (i.e. also Czechoslovaks) were not allowed to travel freely even within socialist countries.
In the event that a citizen of Czechoslovakia wanted to travel individually, for example, to Poland or the Soviet Union, he needed an invitation letter from this country, otherwise an individual trip was not possible.
At that time, travel agencies also offered tours to the West, but it was necessary to stand the queue overnight with a sleeping bag or have "good acquaintances" in the travel agency.
However, it has not been won.
The so-called foreign exchange promise, which was a condition for trips to the West, was not given to "politically unreliable people" who could not travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency was not a freely convertible currency, so it was not possible to raise funds (western currency) for the trip to the West.
However, Czechs most often spent holidays in the Czech Republic, resulting in a large number of cottages and cottages that Czechs bought and this custom survives to this day.
The travel of Czechs has experienced a real revolution since 1989.
While until 1989, holidays in the GDR or Bulgaria were our maximum, last year Czechs spent 4.5 million holidays abroad in countries around the world.
A huge paradox, which we are no longer aware of today, is that it was not possible to travel freely until 1989, because it was part of Yugoslavia.
At the very beginning of the 1990s, the visa was abolished to neighboring Western European countries, which started a huge wave of short-term visits, especially Austrian Vienna or Germany.
For a long time the Czechoslovaks could not get enough trips to the West, everyone wanted to look there at least for a moment.
In Paris, for example, tours of Czechs slept in parks in tents, because our income at that time did not allow us to pay for a hotel or guesthouse.
A typical feature of the travel of Czechs at this time was driving their own food, preserves, sausages, etc. to save money.
Another important moment was the introduction of a freely exchangeable Czech crown, so our tourists were finally able to freely buy Western currency after many decades.
However, our standard of living, and with that, average salaries were rising, which resulted in another important moment around 1997.
Thanks to the increased demand for travel, travel agencies have regularly started preparing tours with charter aerial services since 1997, thanks to the ability to contract quantity discounts compared to the past.
At the end of the 90's of the last century, Czechs already had visa-free travel to virtually all developed countries of the world, except the USA, which continued to facilitate our travel.
Another interesting moment in the history of post-revolutionary travel occurred in 2000, when, thanks to considerable demand, it was from the Czech Republic the first ever direct charter flight into exotics, it was to Thailand.
From this year onwards, direct charters fly with Czech tourists to warm distant countries, this winter will fly directly from the Czech Republic to twelve exotic destinations.
Exotic countries are currently visited by around 100 thousand Czechs a year!
Another interesting moment in this period, which symbolizes our post-revolutionary development, came in 2008.
On November 17, 2008, the U.S
In 2008, for the first time in history, Czechs also spent more than half of their holidays abroad (50.3%) and for the first time in history spent more than 15 thousand CZK per person for one foreign holiday, including the cost of their whereabouts.
The general meeting is supposed to find the chairman, but there is a threat of stalemate again
For almost five months, Czech football has been without a chairman and there is a risk that Ivan Hašek’s successor at the head of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR) will not be known even after another extraordinary general meeting, which will take place in Nymburk on Thursday.
There is still no agreement according to which electoral rules will be pursued.
Without it, there will be no vote on which of the three candidates will be elected.
Jindřich Rajchl, Miroslav Pelta and Tomáš Paclík are interested in leading Czech football.
Hašek resigned in June and called on colleagues to unite and find a strong personality to lead football in the future.
But in the era after Hašek, football slid back to the quarrels between the two union chambers, which cannot agree on a joint candidate.
This became apparent on September 16, when the new president was to be selected.
The delegates of the general meeting came unnecessarily to the National House in Prague in Smíchov, because the negotiations remained blocked and it was not just a method of choice.
There was also no agreement on who actually has the right to vote at the pile - whether only the statutory representatives, or even the functionaries entrusted with the power of attorney.
At this single point since then there has been a partial agreement, which was also helped by the recommendation of the Institute for the State and the law, according to which representation on the basis of a power of attorney is not possible.
However, there are still disputes over the electoral order.
The executive committee did not bring much clarity into the situation either, which no longer solves basically anything else at its meetings, whether proper or extraordinary.
The view of the choice has changed several times, but the neuralgic point is still the same.
The question is: how to proceed in Round 3 in order to exclude the possibility of a heel?
"The dispute is whether to proceed in accordance with the statutes or to find another model," said Dušan Svoboda, one of the three vice-chairmen of the FACR, who represents professional football.
According to the statute-proposed model in the third round, the candidate who will have more percentage support in the chamber for which he is running will win.
Moravia is reportedly willing to make a concession to be won by a candidate who wins a higher sum of the percentage of votes from both chambers.
And the Czech side asserts the condition that the winner must gain at least a two-thirds advantage during this percentage census.
"But it would lose the meaning of the two previous rounds, it would completely deny the two-chamber concept," understands the objections of the Moravian Svoboda party, according to which it would be best not to bend the order and vote according to the valid statutes.
Tomáš Paclík agrees somewhat surprisingly, although it is estimated that the statute-determined electoral order suits Pelt the most of all candidates.
"I wish you to vote according to the statutes at the most."
"So as not to make up any proxy nonsense as at the last general meeting," said the owner of Viktoria Plzeň, who is running for the first time and in his remarks defined himself primarily against Pelt.
Pelta remains optimistic.
According to him, in Nymburk, the new president will be known and believes that the candidates will cooperate constructively together in the future.
"All passions will be reassured by today's progress of the national team at the European Championship."
"Football will be on a happy wave again, there will be a greater willingness to work together," says Jablonec official, who has the greatest experience in managing football at both professional and regional levels.
Rajchl is more restrained.
"I'm hoping for a deal, but unfortunately the situation is very divided."
"It's not between the people of Bohemia and Moravia, but there are efforts to adapt the regulations on purpose."
"This may cause the general meeting to be blocked again."
"But I hope that common sense will prevail," Rajchl said.
There's not much time left for the agreement of all parties.
Some negotiations were supposed to take place directly in Montenegro, but who knows whether the football generality will be complete until the match at all.
The aerial special, in which they were supposed to travel to Podgorica, among others, the vice-chairmen Dalibor Kučera and Rajchl, did not leave Prague due to a technical malfunction in the morning, looking for an alternative option how to transport part of the executive committee to the venue of the barrage retaliation.
In the end, a ministerial-level meeting, which could reportedly take place on Wednesday, may be crucial.
The minister of education, youth and sports, Josef Dobeš, would like to bring to an agreement of the divided parties within the strongest sports association in the country.
There is a lot in the game: state subsidies, the credibility of football and also the upcoming general meeting of ČSTV, which will solve the new direction of Czech sport after the financial problems of Sazka.
Paclík goes into the fight for chairman because he doesn't see a suitable candidate
If Tomáš Paclík, the head of football players in Pilsen, had seen a strong candidate before an extraordinary general meeting, who would have fulfilled all his ideas about the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic (FAČR), he would not have signed up to the fight for the top position.
"If there is a quality adept, I'm fighting for him and I'm not going to the election."
"But in the current situation, no one has been able to find such," Paclík told ČTK.
Into the fight with Miroslav Pelta and Jindřich Rajchl drives Paclík’s motivation to try to prevent Pelta, the alleged favorite of the elections, in the way of the FAČR leadership.
Paclík also points out that in the event of his victory, he would last only until 2013, when a regular general meeting is on the agenda.
In office, he would like to see an experienced and top manager who is not very connected with football events.
"I've spoken to some of the people who would come into consideration."
"It was crazy for me when some said they didn't even want to identify with Czech football at the moment."
"I'd like to change that view," he said.
Paclík is said to be most annoyed by Pelt’s connection with the chairman of the Pilsen Regional Football Association, Roman Berbre, a man with a controversial reputation known behind the scenes as a powerful lobbyist with the ability to influence delegate votes at the general meeting.
It's so unacceptable for Paclík to put Pelta in the presiding position.
"If he had won, the current state of affairs would have been preserved and nothing would have moved forward in football."
"In combination with the support of Berbro, who is a proficient trader, it wouldn't have benefited football any more," Paclík said.
It also alludes to Pelt's name years ago heard in interceptions during an investigation into the corruption affair, from which, however, Pelta came unpunished.
"I don't know what prestige football would bring to Pelto's victory."
"And what would we look like in the eyes of UEFA and FIFA," Paclík said, but he is also already taking his previous statement that he would "sell the Pilsen club if Pelta became chairman".
In the event that Paclík triumphs in Thursday's election, however, he would be able to imagine future cooperation with Pelta.
In the same way, another candidate Rajchl, whom Paclík supported before the September general meeting, which ended in a fiasco, is also thinking.
"He (Pelta) would be the first one I'd go after."
"He's got great powers."
"I could think of him as the head of representation."
"But he couldn't manage the association managerially."
"I don't stick my nose where I can't," said Paclík, who attracted a lot of attention in September by dismissing a committee of referees led by Luďek Macela, who was replaced by Dagmar Damková.
Paclik says he'd dare to lead the association.
He is referring to his skills from the management of private companies, since last summer he also heads Pilsen, which with his support in May celebrated its first league triumph.
"I have the strength to make a difference in the football environment, to contribute to its certain cultivation."
"To limit the influences of some interest groups, which, for example, tend to influence the disciplinary and appellate committee, which goes into disgusting situations," Paclík, who does not hide his antipathies, for example, to the Spartan boss Daniel Křetínský.
He would also consider one of the priorities that the FAČR’s relationship with the Czech Olympic Committee, with the help of which he would lobby political leaders for getting more money from the lottery tax into the sport.
"It's one of the key things that the new president should address."
“If there’s gambling taxation, as political parties declare, I understand their arguments, but they have to say exactly where the money will go,” he said.
Paclík does not even take his connection with the Pilsen club as a burden now, although he himself argues that the chairman of the FAČR should be "suprantial".
Pelta is again connected with Jablonec, Rajchl with Dukla in Prague.
"It's not ideal."
"But I don't see the point in transferring my shares hypocritically to someone in the Pilsen club and returning there in a year and a half," Paclík said.
Pelta promised new statutes of the FAČR, which would exclude the electoral stalemate
Two days before the general meeting of the Football Association, one of the candidates for the chairman, Miroslav Pelta, promised that in the event of an election, he would work for the FAČR to have new statutes, which in the future would definitively exclude disputes over the electoral rules.
Jablonecký official is optimistic before Thursday's meeting in Nymburk and believes that the pre-election fight will significantly dampen today's success of the football team.
Pelta had already promised to focus on representing and raising funds if elected.
He decided to go to the last-minute election before the previous general meeting, so a two-month postponement could be useful for him to defend his notions of managing football and present them in a broader context.
"People at all levels have been doing football for a very long time and they expect the chairman to be known on Thursday."
"He feels it's high time for someone to lead the association," says the man, who is associated primarily with the Jablonec club, but who worked for many years in Sparta and the union's executive committee.
That's why he thinks he's had enough experience to manage Czech football.
He is convinced that behind the current problems with choice is the euphoria that prevailed during the reign of Ivan Hašek.
"Unfortunately at the June general meeting, statutes have been approved that were not as perfect as everyone thought."
"If they didn't allow different interpretations, we've had the chairman since September," Pelta pointed out.
It was the revision of the most important document of the civic association that he conceived as a decoy to the delegates.
"If I become chairman, I'll take it as a personal task, and I'm willing to take personal responsibility if it doesn't."
"It's a fundamental thing we can't be without," Pelta argues.
They continue to try not to define themselves against their opponents.
He has previously announced that he can imagine working with Jindřich Rajchl, whom he recognizes because of his legal education, and he knows full well that his opposing candidate would be his first vice-chairman.
For a long time he did not attack Tomáš Paclík, who, on the contrary, did not leave the thread dry on Pelt.
"He's been in football for over a year, he's already had some accomplishment."
"His attacks, however, were undignified and surprised a lot of people in the manner of an election campaign."
"That's why many of them now doubt his true character," Pelta, who is said to remain loyal to proclamations he announced in September, said.
According to him, his strongest weapon is the knowledge of football from the lowest competitions to representation.
It should be the showcase of football.
"And I'd also try to put an end to all affairs, starting with the Bohemians case or the reverberations of last year's corruption scandal."
"Right later, I'd throw myself into negotiations with the private and state sectors to secure enough money for the whole of football," the forty-six-year-old candidate told ČTK two months ago.
At the time, he also vowed that his arrival at the forefront of football would mean no personnel cleansing.
"I'd have to get acquainted with the contents of the individual employees first," Strahov said, where there is understandable nervousness about the long rulelessness.
Despite the current catastrophic scenarios, accompanied by an upcoming general meeting, he believes that delegates will not go to Nymburk unnecessarily on Thursday.
"If the only contentious point is how to vote in the third round, then we can get over it."
"Football needs credibility, and it will only gain it by finally having its leader again," Pelta remains optimistic two days before perhaps the crucial day of his functional career.
Rajchl promises that the FAČR would start working hard under him
If Jindřich Rajchl, the current vice-chairman, had been elected the new chairman of the Football Association of the Czech Republic at Thursday’s general meeting, many people in the association would not be pleased.
Rajchl promises that if he is elected, he will start working hard.
He wants to enforce systemic changes and make members of the executive committee responsible for individual football areas.
He also promised to solve the Bohemians case, improve relations with UEFA and FIFA, bring more money to football and continue the work started by the previous chairman Ivan Hašek.
"As chairman, I'd like to devote myself to football as a whole."
"From international contacts with UEFA and FIFA to representation, professional football, performance football to youth."
"But I'm not saying that I'm doing everything myself," Rajchl told ČTK in an interview.
"I'm a team player and I want to allocate competencies on the executive committee so that each of the members devotes themselves to a certain area."
"And I'm going to be handing out hard assignments to move football one mile step forward in every segment."
"It probably wouldn't make many people happy, because they'd start working really hard and not just criticizing the work of others," he added.
Due to the critical atmosphere in the football movement, he said he was considering running again.
"It's known that I work 20 hours a day."
"But I need to know that it's going to make sense and that I'm not going to have people behind me who are going to keep throwing sticks at me and questioning my every move," he explained.
"Many people told me I couldn't give up, because then others would give up."
"That's trust that I can't just throw away," he said.
He is also motivated by the continuation of his work, which he began with Hašek.
"There's been a lot of work done over those two years and I don't want to throw it away right now."
"There are started processes, which will have reverberation in a few years, and I would not like anyone to stop them," he said, fearing that in the event of the election of another candidate, Miroslav Pelta, the situation on the union could go back to before Hašek.
"I believe in systematicism, and that's a thing I don't think Mirek Pelta can do."
"Behind him, above all, there are people who would do football in such a way that it would only be for the chosen ones."
"I can be blamed for a lot of things, but certainly not that I'm siding with someone."
"I'm trying to be objective."
"I want to do football for everyone, because then everyone will have a good time and not just the chosen ones," Rajchl commented.
He also refutes claims that he would only be a Czech chairman.
"Even though some people are trying to say otherwise, I plan to do my best for Moravia."
"For example, it's time to think that the second league would be divided into the Czech and Moravian parts, whose winners would go to the first league."
"There wouldn't be a current situation where there are only three Moravian teams in the league, which is a problem towards young players in Moravia," Rajchl said.
Since Tomáš Paclík, the head of the Pilsen club, was also involved in the fight for the chairman, Rajchl does not dare to estimate his chances of being elected.
"But I still feel strong support in the Czech chamber, which elected me as its vice-chairman and sees that there are clear achievements behind me," Rajchl said, considering his achievements to save tens of millions of crowns on unfavourable contracts, negotiate television contracts and negotiations on the lottery law.
On the other hand, it is complicit that the union does not pull together.
While he declares Pelt to be "disqualified from returning confidence to Czech football", he considers the other of Paclík's opponents a strong candidate who has a lot to offer football.
"No one can dispute that he did a great job in Pilsen in that year."
"If he had been elected, he might have continued the things that Ivan Hašek had started here and would not have fundamentally changed the staffing in the commissions."
"He wouldn't want to twist it to his advantage," Rajchl said.
The most beautiful pools in Europe
Jumping into blue
Julia Stanek
Paddling pool from Ufono's point of view, a sumptuous swimming pool in Budapest: Whoever travels through Europe can experience his blue miracle - and after exploring cities or traveling, let your tired feet rest in a thermal spa.
The book will show where wellness by the water is the most beautiful.
When Iris Meder embarked on discovery trips over the past one-and-a-half-year, she was not allowed to miss her luggage: her swimsuit.
Lying on the water, she looked upheare into the artistically shaped domes, swam opposite the marble putti (the figures of a small naked boy, a child (with wings)) who were waiting for her on the edge of the pool and admired the carefully restored Art Nouveau swimming pools.
If she liked a swimming pool, in a moment she carried it into her notebook - even with remarkable details about the manner of construction and the history of the building.
More than 200 swimming pools have been explored in this way by Meder architecture historians and presents them now in their just-published book "Badefreuden (The Joy of Bathing) ": People's Swimming Pools in Munich, the historical palaces of the Schwarzwald swimming pools,"The hard core of concrete structures" in Taunus.
13 countries were led by her "Journey behind the most extraordinary spas in Central Europe": In addition to Germany, Austria and Switzerland, she visited localities in Italy, France, Bohemia, Slovakia, Slovenia, Hungary, Romania, Poland - and always one city in Luxembourg, Serbia and Croatia.
The 46-year-old German is a real enthusiast of swimming culture.
He doesn't exaggerate much with sports, says Meder - it's just something else in the water.
To rage is that fans of swimming have to settle for blue tiles and the smell of chlorine, while runners for health can inhale the intoxicating smell of spruce forests or listen to the hilarious chittering of birds in the park.
“One day, I asked why swimming pools always have to be so ugly,” says Mrs. Meder.
"So I began to collect nice swimming pools."
The water level doubles the architecture
Splashing in the jumping pool and essential oils for the sauna - the coziness factor is therefore so great that it applies to all the senses, so much Ms. Meder.
And when the founders tried, swimming pools can be a real feast for the eyes: "Instead of the floor, a man has a water surface in a spa."
"It's both transparent, but on the other hand it reflects the beauty of the whole building."
Everything visually doubles: high vault, colorful tiles, jumpboards.
The "Badefreuden (Pleasure of Bathing" provides everything you need to know about the architectural and cultural history of historical oases of wellnes in 190 pages - it arouses, however, the desire to jump into blue.
Remarkable specimens were discovered by float collector Ms Meder during her search in Hungary.
In addition to the beautiful healing spas, such as the 100-year-old Széchenyi spa in Budapest, it found water containing nitrates in the original karst cave (cave spa in Miskolc-Tapolca) as well as thermal baths whose swimming pools are filled with alkaline water and are located in a bottle-shaped building (Városi Termálfürdö in Jászberen).
On the other hand, there are many open-air swimming pools in Switzerland and Austria. While one has an entire city’s eye swimming pool from the Curyš Roostop, a snow-covered Arlberg can be viewed in an outdoor swimming pool in St. Anton – and: There’s a window in the steam spa, from which it’s possible to watch the ski slope.
Perhaps the most notorious design of the swimming pool awaits spa guests in Längenfeld: Ötztal Aquapark looks like a UFO that has just landed in the Alps.
From the three up-open pans in the winter, steam rises while in the background of Mount Ötztalu rises to the sky.
Alhambra in Schwarzwald
Not in every swimming pool, Ms. Meder's pool collector's pooch of chlorine rose into her nostrils.
Many thermal baths are smelled after sulfur, in the spa of Árpāpád in Békescsaba, Hungary it smells of heating oil to the extent that one can barely believe that it has reached the spa.
The smell comes from medicinal water containing alkaline bicarbonates, which is almost black and whose deposits cling to the skin as soon as only a person slips into the pool - a rather special wellness experience.
However, Iris Meder was also disappointed when looking for a nice spa: When she wanted to visit the Czech solar and climatic spa Luhačovice, she encountered a closed terrain.
In 1902, architect Dušan Jurkovič, the designed spa was closed.
When Mrs. Meder glanced through the crumbling windows of the red-and-white-yellow Art Nouveau building, she saw weeds growing massively between the tiles.
On the other hand, they built their demise in a magical swimming pool in Schwarzwald: in Palais Thermal in Bad Wildbad.
This spa has already undergone three phases of construction; one can discover not only Neo-Romanesque elements, which come from the time of its creation in 1844, but also the Moorish hall in the Alhambra style, which was annexed around 1900.
Since the careful modernization of 1995, Palais Thermal has been a popular spa resort for pool connoisseurs Mrs. Iris Meder, and not only because he comes from Schwarzwald.
“It’s already a performance to combine such diverse building styles into such a wonderful complex,” says Ms Meder.
For such a palace, immersion in the thermal pool is apparently a marginal issue.
Agrarian speculation
Deutsche Bank to investigate commodity trade
Christian Teevs
Does Deutsche Bank leave its trade in agricultural raw materials?
According to SPIEGEL-ONLINE, Josef Ackermann commissioned a working group to investigate the consequences of speculation for poor worlds.
In January, the head of the group wants to draw consequences from this.
Hamburg -
It is clear for federal citizens: 84% of Germans, according to one study, find it unacceptable for banks to speculate with agricultural raw materials like wheat or maize.
Two-thirds of those questioned are even demanding that Deutsche Bank and other credit institutions step out of this trade because it deepens the problems of the world’s poorest.
That's the result of a Forsa poll on behalf of consumer organization Foodwatch.
The study relies on a report that Foodwatch presented in mid-October.
In it, author Harald Schumann shows that bets at global commodity exchanges are driving prices high, and banks are therefore complicit in the global hunger problem.
The consumer campaign is aimed specifically at Deutsche Bank boss Josef Ackermann, although big banks such as Goldman Sachs or Morgan Stanley also act similarly.
Initially, commodity bets were meant to insure traders against fluctuations in prices.
In the meantime, however, most experts agree that participants in the capital market interested in high-risk trades detached themselves from supply and demand - and made profits on behalf of the poorest.
Other than competitors in the United States, Ackermann responded to criticism: He vowed to investigate the criticisms, saying that no trade was "worth being put at stake by Deutsche Bank's good reputation."
According to SPIEGEL ONLINE, the international working group is reviewinging complaints from Foodwatch-Report.
By the end of the year, the inspection is to be concluded, then the results will be presented to Deutsche Bank - along with a recommendation on how this should be handled.
At the end of January, bank officials want to know what the consequences will draw.
At the end there could be a reduction in commodity trade or even a complete rejection.
Ackermann is self-helping on his image.
Thilo Bode, the head of Foodwatch, welcomed the report, but intensified his criticism of the financial industry: “Betting on rising food prices shows particularly drastically how ruthless banks today are detrimental to the common good.”
May Ackermann, as top bank lobbyist, live up to his duties.
"Deutsche Bank has to cut from its portfolio all items for which money is being used for bets on food prices," Bode said.
"It doesn't matter whether Mr. Ackermann cares about starving people or Deutsche Bank's reputation."
Ackermann's view seems surprising at first glance, but it is clear that the group boss, a few months before his departure from office, would like to leave the perfect picture.
On Monday, Deutsche Bank announced that that the head of the board would not switch to the supervisory board.
By averting from disputed agrarian speculation, he could improve his image.
And he doesn't have to worry about the possible losses - his successor should deal with that in the end.
Every other would denounce investments in raw materials
Foodwatch is trying to maintain high pressure on the manager.
So far, more than 30,000 internet users have participated in the organization's campaign.
On Tuesday, Bode published a poll in which 1001 people were interviewed by the Forsa Institut on 7 and 8 November.
Accordingly, just eleven percent consider it legitimate that Deutsche Bank offers investments linked to bets on food prices.
Many bank customers would also deduce personal consequences if they learned that their bank was involved in such trades: Everyone else, as a result of the poll, would terminate all the investments for which the money flows into speculation with raw materials.
43 percent would warn acquaintances about such a bank, 49 percent said they will think about denunciating their account and move on to another institution.
The 21st-century House in Vienna
Moving pavilion
Ingeborg Wiensowski
The story of architecture with peaks, falls and a happy ending: The Austrian pavilion from the World Exhibition in Brussels 1958 won prizes, became a museum in Vienna, remained empty and desolated.
It is now reopened as an exhibition house.
When it is horrified for modern post-war architecture, clear forms, glass halls, bold booms and new building materials, which are typical of belief in technical progress, the Austrian pavilion of architect Karl Schwanzer is an aptive and famous example for the 1958 Brussels World Exhibition.
Schwanzer's pavilion received the Grand Prix d'Architecture for its lightweight and lofty construction made of steel and glass.
The building is still considered a milestone of contemporary architecture.
He became famous of the architect in such a way that during the next world exhibition he also built a pavilion of Austria.
With his legendary four-cyling BMW-palace plus as a key made by a secondary building in Munich, he eventually became internationally famous in 1973.
His, as a temporarite exhibition-designed pavilion after the world exhibition, was placed in Schweizer Garten near the Südbahnhof (Southern railway station) in Vienna, reconstructed and opened the Museum of the 20th century - it was named "House of the 20th Century".
However, the building was not suitable for the museum, either spatially or technically - there were no walls for hanging artistic exhibits, no satisfactory climate.
Nevertheless, it was used until contemporary art has received a new stand in Vienna.
Since 2001, the building has been empty and decaying more and more.
Now the architect Adolf Krischanitz has reconstructed the pavilion, it now bears the name "House of the 21st century" and will be used in the future as the Museum of Contemporary Art.
On Tuesday evening, Museum Belvedere, to which the palace belongs, will open the first exhibition - a happy intention in every respect.
Agnes Husslein-Arco became the new director of the Belvedere Austrian Gallery in 2007.
Husslein-Arco, a graduated art historian with an eminent career, i.e. in her youth a figure skater, later in Sotheby's, Guggenheim's museum and as the founding director of the Museum of Modern Salzburg, enforced emphatically the reconstruction of politicians and conservationists, provided public funds and private sponsorship money and determined the use.
In addition to contemporary art, the works of the sculptor Fritz Wotruba should be exhibited by a foundation of the same name, a café and a bookstore should be created, and an office building had to be built.
Strict oversight, low budget.
Krischanitz was an ideal cast.
Not only did he sensitively reconstruct and expand Joseph Olbrich's Art Nouveau buildings in Vienna and won the competition, but because Schwanzer studied at the Technical University of Vienna and the pavilion was an "immeasurably important place" for him, where he "almost every weekend" viewed exhibitions.
He knewed the original project and also knew the problems of the building, which lost its generosity and lightness during its relocation to Vienna and the change of use.
There were much bigger problems during the current reconstruction: the building was a "catapult for energy," Krischanitz says.
The profiles of the windows of the glass facade have been replaced, reinforced with insulating insulating glass has been used, and the glass roof has been replaced by a special safety glass.
Cooled bridge-steel beams were strengthened or replaced, and asbestos-filled ceilings remediated.
Two underground floors provided a place for new use, a recessed lighting canal between the building and the street provides lighting, a bridge leads to the entrance - almost a new building, but still a lofty Schwanzer pavilion.
The original entrance doors have been retained, the building is painted in an old anti-corrosive red-brown color tone.
And the cinema is even exactly the same as it used to be.
And all this under the strict supervision of the heritage office and the low budget.
"Often material had to be found that is good and cheap, and he also liked the heritage office," Krischanitz says, pointing to the broken floor of the basement.
Despite all the pressures, problems and reconstruction, the building remained flexible and airy.
It is said to be difficult with the pavilion, "who came here from Brussels," Krischanitz said recently, a typical "Drop-in-the-City-historia."
Such architecture is said to stand "always in the wrong place" and still wants to "awaken proper self-confidence."
In this case, however, the House of the 21st century is not in an inappropriate place, as an urban happy coincidence has happened: In the circle around Schweizer Garten, a completely newly designed "Municipal Quarter at the Main Station" with office and residential buildings is being created.
And perhaps there will still be another lucky coincidence: the 21st-century house could receive support from another Krischanitz building, as collector Francesca von Habsburg bought the Berliner Temporäre Kunsthalle and, as it can be heard, is already dealing with the city's neighborhood at the Schweizer Garten site.
The runway is planning a package for the winter crisis at a million-dollar level
The runway has pinned off a crisis plan designed to prevent train outages in the winter season.
The number of employees should be doubled.
It'll cost millions.
In the fight against the winter caused by train outages and delays, Deutsche Bahn spends an additional 70 million euros this year, according to the newspaper's report.
"Bild" has provisionally reported, citing an internal group plan, that a total of around 300 million euros is expected by 2015.
It is said to achieve better use of trains and rail networks even in extreme weather conditions.
Among other things, it is planned to double the number of internal and external staff to 16 000 people to remove snow on railway installations and platforms.
The aim is to get rid of the platform until the start of operation and 90 percent for the operation of the necessary turnouts within four to five hours of snow and ice.
Two months ago, Rüdiger Grube's dragon chief warned after a meeting at the highest level with representatives of the government and industry against train blackouts and delays also during this winter.
In the last winter period, the runway in passenger transport had significant problems, because, among other things, in difficult weather conditions it had a lack of reserves.
In this way, numerous new regional trains have been awaiting approval by the Federal Railway Authority for years.
In addition, delays in the dispatch of ICE trains are approached
For years, the track has also not been able to handle the fact that ICE train axles are apparently more likely to be partially inspected when one ICE train at Köln Central Station has been subjected to axle destruction.
Deutsche Bank pays million-dollar fine in U.S
After the commotion surrounding boss Josef Ackermann, there are new tramps in the U.S.: Deutsche Bank has to pay million-dollar compensation there.
As if Deutsche Bank did not have enough problems at home, the giant in the field still catched up with its past in the U.S.: The Frankfurt institution pays 145 million dollars (106 million euros) in one compensation procedure to settle disputes over the bankruptcy of five major cooperative banks during the financial crisis.
This is the sale of mortgage certificates.
NCUA's financial market regulator blames a number of big banks for seducing cooperative banks with false promises to buy financial products; that they may have reduced risks.
Such papers lost significantly in value in the course of the crisis and tore the banks with them into the abyss.
"We are satisfied that we were able to resolve the topic without having to drag the parties to court," a spokesman for Deutsche Bank said.
The bank pleads no blame for the settlement.
This also applies to Citigroup, which is committed to the payment of US$2.5 million.
NCUA President Debbie Matz welcomed the friendliness of both money institutions.
The National Credit Union Administration (NCUA) is responsible for U.S
NCUA is attempting to receive compensation for billion-dollar damages incurred.
Now terminated settlements are the first of their kind.
The regulator also clamped down on other big banks and sued JPMorgan Chase, Royal Bank of Scotland and Goldman Sachs in the summer.
Dubious mortgage-backed Securities are so-called Mortgage-backed Securities.
These are based on bank loans.
When the U.S. real estate bubble collapsed in 2007, it became fatal to a number of financial firms.
In September 2008, at the height of the financial crisis, the U.S.-Investmentbank Lehman Brothers changed its minding.
Although the events occurred a few years ago, a sharp wind is now blowing through the banks.
The investors and supervisory authorities have filed numerous lawsuits in order to remedy or suffer misconduct.
The Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) has triggered the largest wave of lawsuits.
He blames 18 international big banks that two state-owned U.S. financiers dealing with Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac's real estate have disadvantaged about $200bn in mortgage deals.
Deutsche Bank, too, has to face FHFA lawsuit, dealing with more deals totalling about US$14.2 billion from 2005 to 2007.
The supervising authority requires the Frankfurt Institute to be liable for "substantial losses" without, of course, stating an exact amount.
Deutsche Bank dismissed the demands as unfounded and said it wanted to defend itself.
Warren Buffett enters IBM with €10bn
Financial guru Buffett is investing heavily in IBM's IT business.
Since March, it has bought shares worth more than ten billion dollars.
So far, U.S. investor Warren Buffett has always declared that he will not invest in IT and computers, because the long-term development of companies is difficult to predict.
However, he has now read IBM's business reports and changed his opinion on investment in the industry.
Much earlier, he had to understand that IBM primarily offered services and acquired computer systems for IT-departments of other enterprises.
Warren Buffett's investment in the financial world is regarded as knighting.
As a result, IBM's mother rock can now be called "Sir IBM".
Because Buffett revealed on U.S. economic broadcaster CNBC that he had bought US$10.7 billion worth of IBM shares since March.
His Berkshire Hathaway has become one of IBBM's largest shareholders with a 5.5 percent stake.
Buffett sagate that even IBM didn't even know anything about his engagement yet.
He praised the management, which even sought proper returns during the economic crisis.
"You've done a great job," Buffett said about the strategy.
For a long time, IBM has been relying on lucrative IT services such as the operation of computing centers.
The more than 100-year-old computer pioneer also offers software and consulting as well as powerful computers for businesses.
Buffett has rather avoided tech companies to this day.
It claims that it invests only in undertakings whose activities it also understands.
Instead, Buffett reached for the freight railroad, the lubricant manufacturer, or the machine products.
In addition to about 80 of its own subsidiaries, its Berkshire Hathaway also owns shares in a number of large-scale companies such as Coca-Cola and Munich Re, the former Münchener Rück.
The 81-year-old took over Berkshire Hathaway's small textile company in the 1960s and built one of the world's most valuable businesses with wise investments.
Yet his way of life is modest.
Therefore, for many investors around the world, he is a cult figure.
His almost infallible sense of making money gave him the nickname "Orakel of Omaha."
When Buffett's investment became known, IBIM's shares rose by one percent before the start of the stock exchange.
"Big Blue," as IBM is also called, has a total price of more than 220 billion dollars on the stock exchange and is considered, along with Apple and Microsoft, to be the world's most valuable tech enterprises.
At Microsoft, so much Buffett wouldn’t want to start.
Founder Bill Gates is said to be his intimate friend.
Threat is palpable
Following the unveiling of "Cells in Zwickau," Turkish media doubt the German rule of law.
The newspaper even speaks of a "bloody ideology" that is now comiving again.
The murders of eight small businessmen of Turkish origin and one Greek from 2000 to 2006 had a right-wing extremist background.
This report has been reacted with fear and concern in Turkey and within the German-Turkish population in Germany.
For for many German Turks, this brings back the specter that has long been considered dead: Since the arson attacks in Mölln in November 1992 and Solingen in May 1993, there has been no comparable violence with a right-wing extremist background.
There has never been any doubt that there is xenophobia in Germany.
However, the discovered right-wing extremist network reveals an extent whose dimension can no longer be seen.
"Is the bloody ideology coming to life again?" were the headlines of the online edition of the Turkish newspaper "Haberturk."
"Sabah, on the other hand, highlights that one of the perpetrators should have been a secret police agent of the department for the protection of the constitution and fears that the German authorities, too, could be entangled in a right-wing radical swamp.
Deliberately, the Turkish municipality of Germany reacted deliberately: It remembered at the weekend by demonstrating in silence in front of the Brandeburg Gate the victims of racist violence.
Only two weeks ago did the Turkish municipality in Berlin, together with German and Turkish politicians, remember the ceremonial act of the 50th anniversary of the German-Turkish recruitment agreement.
In hilarious colors, a common life has been staged - as a history of success.
"Germany's 50th Gift" is the headline of an article in the Turkish internet platform "Haber X", which reports on the background of the murderous series.
Also, the publication of "Hürriyet" for Germany will not be taken not to carry out this conceptual connection.
"It's starting to smell," is the headline of Ahmet Külahci's column.
He emphasizes in it that people who paid taxes were murdered and made an important contribution to the reconstruction of Germany with their work.
Most critically, the procedure of the police and its late success in the search is discussed in German-Turkish internet forums.
Why didn’t the German authorities think much earlier to look for suspects in right-wing extremist circles, the authors wonder.
In this way, many are demonstrated at least rhetorically prompt to the use of drastic means: "if activity is carried out so superficially, there will be nothing left for the immigrants other than their own defenses and armings."
"That'll be the best self-defense."
"On the quality of clarification and adequacy of the punishment of the whole network, internal peace in Germany depends very strongly," writes "Selen."
What does it mean, if internal peace in Germany is endangered, they clarify the assassinations in Mölln and Solingen in the 1990s.
A photograph of a burning site in Solingen is deeply engraved in the memory of Germans and Turks.
For no other event has shown the Turks living here more clearly that there is a tangible threat to the Germans, that distrust and distance can result in right-wing violence
However, the assassinations made the Turks more apparent a sense of reciprocity.
Do not repeat the mistakes of the past
In the Turkish press at that time, the failure of German politics was criticized after the assassinations: Helmut Kohl refused to visit those in Mölln who survived.
After an arson attack in Solingen, he sent his condolent telegram to the Turkish state president, but allowed himself to be replaced by his foreign minister Klaus Kinkel at Köln - Kinkel calculated in his speech what fees the Turks handed over.
Quite as if the value of a man was measured according to the height of his taxes.
The current Turkish media coverage once again shows how deep the distrust of many German Turks in the country’s state-law arrangement is.
In addition to efforts for extensive clarification and a new evaluation of right-wing extreme terrorism in Germany, this time it requires open words.
Scandalous bank at Lake Zurich
The most adventurous bank of Europe is not based in Italy or France, but in Switzerland.
UBS suffered almost bankruptcy during the financial crisis and has been slipping from one crisis to the next ever since.
Now it's supposed to be compared by a German.
Axel Weber didn't think so.
After his noisy departure from the leadership of the Federal Bank in July, it became well-known that 2013 would take on an almost equally spectacular new job: the president of the board of directors of the Swiss big bank UBS - an unexpected and lucrative step in his career.
Axel Weber looked forward publicly, praising Switzerland for its beauty and UBS for its newly acquired solidity and making friendly remarks to future colleagues.
"Die UBS has achieved a turnaround."
"I look forward to working with Kaspar Villiger and Oswald Grübel."
But it was already clear in September that everything would turn out very differently.
One dealer from UBS in London bypassed all of the bank's security systems and wasted around 1.7 billion euros.
UBS CEO Oswald Grübel, not only a respected banker in Switzerland, had to leave.
The president of UBS's board of directors has also been scalded ever since - and everyone is asking: When will Axel Weber finally come?
It has already become known that Weber has been able to advise the bank since February 2012.
And he will take over the chairmanship of the board already in May - not only in 2013, as the bank communicated on Tuesday.
It's going to be tough for Axel Weber.
He's not a banker, he's rather a professor of economics and an issuance banker, and he'd certainly need a time to incorporate.
At the same time, he will take responsibility for the bank's strategy, which once again proved that it deserves the title "Bank of the biggest scandals in Europe"
For UBS is, on the one hand, huge - until recently it was the world's largest wealth manager - and has been gliding for years from one scandal to the next.
Nothing, it seems, won't a proud Swiss bank be spared.
It started during the financial crisis.
Until 2007, UBS enjoyed an excellent reputation.
The image of a silent and solid but also cunning wealth manager is combined with ambitious goals: She also wanted to mean something in investment banking.
On the one hand, UBS managed 2007 with 1.6 trillion dollars more money for wealthy clients than any other bank in the world.
And, on the other hand, she advanced as an investment bank to the league of the world's biggest banks
That made an impression.
Hans Geiger, a former banker and later long-time banking professor in Zurich, recounts: "At the time, UBS has always listed UBS as a role model of a good bank."
“From today’s point of view, one asks, ‘How could I have been so wrong?’
He was very wrong.
In 2007, it became clear for the first time that something in UBS went wrong.
In the middle of the year, Peter Wuffli, the head of the board, who has so far been considered a highly intelligent and also a brilliant banker, resigned.
A few months later, he knew why he'd left.
The bank's investment division wasted Subprime-papers and had to write off billions.
In order to withstand it, UBS announced an increase of 13 billion francs of capital.
However, it has actually found investors, but ones it would have turned its nose back recently: an opaque Asian state fund from Singapore, and an Arab state fund that was so discreet that one did not even know its name.
But that was just the first bump.
It turned out worse in April 2008.
The bank had to admit new billion-dollar losses, the president of the board Marcel Ospel, 2006 the second best-paid board of directors of Switzerland, resigned.
In October 2008, a disaster followed: UBS, due to its erroneous investments in the financial crisis before collapse and had to be rescued by the state.
Nationalbank has bought problematic securities from UBS for tens of billions, the Swiss Confederace has given six billion francs to mandatory exchangeable bonds, for which it could become a co-owner of the bank.
The whole of Switzerland has been shaken - because it has noticed how dramatic it is for a small country when it has such large banks on its territory.
After all, UBS 2007 was more than five times the output of Switzerland’s economy (see figure).
“We couldn’t save UBS again,” says Geiger.
The first disaster is followed by the second.
For several months now, UBS capitulated it in a tax dispute with America and showed a willingness to release data from wealthy customers who, if possible with the help of banks, cut taxes.
In addition, it agreed to pay 780 million dollars - as a return of unjustified profits from these trades, including a penalty surcharge.
In doing so, UBS became more or less open that it aided tax cuts.
At the same time, she lost something that had always been her capital in competition for wealthy clients: a halo of discretion.
After this scandal, Swiss banking secrecy was no longer what it used to be.
This was followed by the resignation of the head of the board of directors and the president.
And an exodus of customer money.
Thus, over the course of several months, the bank has lost its credibility in both central business areas: in investment banking due to poor speculation and in wealth management for aiding tax cuts.
And just as it calmed down a little, just when UBS was thought to have recovered, here comes this unfortunate business analysis: A single dealer wasted 1.7 billion euros before the bank noticed that something was wrong.
And it's clear: it's probably still not going well with risk management in investment banking.
“The Adoboli case probably found that risk control has changed too little,” says Martin Janssen, an economist and Finanz professor of finance at Zürich’s Banking Institute.
Also, the chief financial officer of a large German bank is sure: "The UBS problem is that it doesn't cope with risk control."
At the same time, it was precisely the idea of caution, with which the Swiss bankers advanced a long time ago in order to become guards of the world's great possessions: no too many risks, but security for money - also from this side, the world collapsed again.
Vaults of Swiss banks have become a symbol for unshakable reliability like Lindt and Sprüngli for particularly high-quality chocolate.
UBS headquarters in Zurich are still suited to the cliché of a good Swiss private bank - it's just a little bigger.
The position is already classic.
The bank has its headquarters in Bahnhofstraße in Zurich, which leads directly to Lake Zurich, which is framed by the snowy peaks of the Swiss mountains.
The buildings here are beautiful, shop rentals are among the highest in Europe.
Jewelry shops such as Tiffany's can be found here among Switzerland's traditional shops such as Sprüngli pastry shops.
Swiss banks have been based here for centuries: from Nationalbank to Palast der Credit Suisse to elite private banks like Julius Bär.
A heavy revolving door enters the UBS reception hall with a heavy revolving door.
Inside there are walls paneled with marble, in front of them stand massive leather chairs, next to the logo of the bank a large rudder to the "safe".
UBS knows what the customer is looking for here: security.
However, this idea by the Swiss banks is sometimes suppressed or betrayed by both Swiss big banks, UBS and Credit Suisse, as the head of a small Swiss private bank finds out.
His name would prefer not to read in the newspapers, in the end he concludes, like all Swiss bankers, also with both big bank deals.
UBS has probably got too bigger, it's figuring out - especially in investment banking, where, moreover, the Anglo-Saxons have expanded.
The Swiss probably don't have anything to say about it anymore.
"And if they did, they've adapted to Anglo-Saxon manners."
Indeed, it was Anglo-Saxon investment banking, which UBS changed, brought it high profits at first, and then a disaster: No European financial institute lost as much money in the Jungle around Subprime as Schweizer Großbank.
On October 15, 2008, the disaster became apparent.
"On Wednesday, the governing coalition of a small seven-million-dollar country decided on the largest bailout event in the history of its nation," author Lukas Hässig writes in his book "Der UBS-Crash."
Up to 68 billion francs, or approximately 45 billion euros, have been approved by politicians to save a large-scale bank from bankruptcy.
How could this have happened?
If you're talking to Swiss bankers, they'll see a problem especially in the size of UBS.
In June 1998, two out of three Swiss big banks merged into a massive concern.
A bank was created that was already too big for small Switzerland during the merger - and continued to grow.
At the same time, it aided that the two different bank predecessors of UBS complemented each other: One wealthy but sleepy bank in Zurich, with a centre of gravity in wealth management and a poorer but more internationally heavily involved.
Suddenly, internationalized investment bankers came advantageously to a lot of money, with which they could spin a large roulette.
There are two names associated with this rise in UBS: Peter Wuffli, head of UBS and Marcel Ospel, then chairman of the board of directors, from 2001 to 2007.
“I’ve always thought that Wuffli and Ospel are the team of the future,” says Hans Geiger.
"Wuffli, the highly intelligent, modest one who knows a lot about models and statistics and Ospel, a person following feelings around his stomach that has evolved from banking science entirely."
But Ospel relied on Wuffli towards the end.
And Wuffli, at the end, probably made a decisive mistake: "He probably thought his models were a reality," Geiger says.
"That's a tragic case."
Tragic, perhaps.
It's clear: Pride preceded the fall.
UBS employees clearly recall meetings of executives at which the group boss Wuffli repeated the same slogan over and over again: Knocking Goldman Sachs down the throne.
The U.S. bank was the world's most powerful investment bank.
She listed "League Tables," a waiting list documenting the success of the banks of that time.
In it, UBS struggled to place it at the top.
She's already managed a few steps.
In the foreign exchange trade and with the shares, she participated in the game at the top.
However, corporate advisers who have been commissioned emphasize one weakness: In international interest-ration trade, in "Fixed Income" bankers, there is probably a great distance behind the biggest banks.
UBS probably has gaps in U.S. Mortgage Products.
In order to close this gap, UBS committed herself to an adventure that almost cost her existence.
Hunger after growth, both the bank itself and its-based fund have invested to hedge risks into Subprime's U.S. investments.
It developed into a mighty machine that switched securities to U.S. Mortgage Loans and handed it over further, while also retaining the risks itself.
Also, when the other banks had long been looking for a way out.
"Even in the summer of 2007, when the real estate market was on fire, that's where UBS was buying," says banking expert Geiger.
How big the euphoria is, such a deep fall.
One manager after another had to leave when attempts to clean up were more difficult than thought.
Positions at UBS have become a catapulting seat and are swinging to this day.
Whoever runs the big bank now has an almost unmanageable job.
They need to find a strategy for a bank that all nearby business models have disappeared.
Geiger, a banking expert, already has an idea: Ending investment banking, concentrating on wealth management, a return to Swiss roots.
That's how far the bank doesn't want to go.
But investment banking is supposed to decline strongly, so much is now clear.
Next Thursday, the bank's new boss, Sergio Ermotti, wants to present his future strategy to the world.
In fact, the 51-year-old Swiss Italian was only seen as a transitional solution when the bank surprisingly broke up with Grübel's predecessor.
In the meantime, it has asserted itself: It is supposed to run UBS stores for a long time, the Zurich bank said on Tuesday.
Ermotti wants to bring the investment bank back back in the mid-1990s.
3500 seats cut.
But that won't be enough.
UBS needs a vision.
Who's she?
How does he want to make money?
These are the questions Axel Weber is already asking.
Publicly, however, he says only that he and his mistress will first find an apartment in Zurich.
Next year.
