Smart crows are expanding in cities
In many places, field ravens are considered "problematic birds": However, these highly intelligent animals can no longer be expelled from cities, experts say.
Some like them, others would like to get rid of them quite quickly: Crows have belonged to the image of the city for centuries, yet in some villages there are accumulated complaints of residents about impurity and noise caused by animals.
At the same time, there is no means against "problematic birds".
Field ravens may no longer be expelled from the cities, experts said at the first symposium on crows in Leero, East Frisia.
Experts from the entire federal territory on possible starting points were consulted there.
Seeking alternatives in cities
Conservatiors derived the problems of cities also from the exodus of specially protected field ravens: The use of chemistry in agriculture is probably increasingly destroying traditional areas for life.
Persecution, increasing development and felling of typical trees to nesting birds make life difficult.
Crows are protected.
Large colonies with more than 600 nesting pairs are, among others, in Soesto, Northwest of Vestphalia, including Jever, Diepholz and Achime near Bremen and Leer.
There, a contentious project of mass killing of crows in a rural county called negative headlines.
Critics have described the capture of animals into traps for birds and killing sticks to be particularly brutal.
"Normal expulsion, scaring, doesn't bring anything," said urban planner and landscape designer Werner Klöver of Leer.
Removing nests by spraying water or felling trees often has undesirable consequences, environmental expert Manfred Kaiser of Lahr in Badensko-Württemberg also said.
"Animals retreat, large colonies split and divide into other places."
Scaring is only conceivable as an exception for hospitals, retirement homes or schools.
"We have to live with them, we won't get them away."
Without the surrounding area, the problem of cities cannot be solved, was the opinion of Mayor Wolfgang Kellner of Leer.
A combination of expulsion of animals from sensitive residential areas and their attachment is conceivable to settle in other places.
"Information board instead of chainsaws" was promoted by the psychologist and ethnologist Uta Maria Jürgens because of a completely different treatment of animals: "In the end, we can end up with self-confidencence."
Ms. Jürgens co-founded Schleswig-Holstein Ascherberg's nature cloaking trail, on which the information boards provide lessons about the life of a highly intelligent field raven.
In the meantime, the project has already been accepted by tourists.
Ms. Jürgens also had her own opinion on criticism of the noise caused by birds: "Here's about every volume of communication, they've got a lot to say."
The intelligence and cleverness of the animals are legendary: Field ravens use not only aids in order to reach a treat, but even make their instrument as an aid.
Researchers at the University of Cambridge have recently tested behavior on four field ravens how far the intelligence of birds reaches.
In one experiment, the birds quickly learned that they had to bring stones to the collapse of the structure in order to get to the larva of the pier.
Without much training, they recognized what size and shape the stone must have in order to cause the platform to collapse.
In the second attempt, cunning crows then had to solve a more precarious problem.
In a vertically built cylinder, the researchers placed a tiny jar of piers.
Beaks couldn't reach for their prey.
However, the researchers put bits of wire there.
Of these, all four animals shaped a hook, with the help of which, as a hooker, she hunted the container behind the handle from the pipe.
Three of the four field ravens managed to do it even with a lockdown.
