SAP wants to double the number of its employees in China.
Europe's largest software maker, SAP, will invest billions in China in the next few years and aims to nearly double the number of its employees in the country.
About two billion dollars (approximately 1.5 billion euros) will flow by 2015, the Dax Group said on Tuesday.
“We’re going to create additional research and development facilities and employ the relevant workers,” said Bill McDermott, chief executive at a customer trade fair in Beijing.
The staff is now expected to grow from the current 2500 employees to 4500.
The number of branches of the world's leading software for businesses will be doubled from five to ten or eleven.
So far, SAP is present in Beijing, Shanghai, Guanzhou, Chengdu and Dalian.
About 4,000 of SAP's worldwide more than 176,000 customers are booming in the country.
Growth plans in China are also an important building block of Dax Group’s 2015 strategy.
SAP wants to increase its turnover by 2015 to 20 billion euros.
Last year, the Walldorfs spent approximately 12.5 billion euros.
Currently, the stores of the software manufacturer, which employs 55 000 employees on a global scale, are proceeding as well as almost never before.
SAP has been active in China for 20 years.
"Now we'd like to expand our activities (...)," McDermott said.
"Our goal, with the support of large-scale research, is to promote sustained growth in China."
Massacre on a personal level
Peter Englund's emotionally powerful new book titled The Beauty and the Sorrow: An Intimate History of the First World War begins with such a long list of performing characters that you get dizzy.
One piece of advice on how to behave at that moment: Carefully rip this page out and make a bookmark out of it.
You will become very familiar with all persons in Mr. Englund's novel narrative, and this list will serve as a navigation - as a kind of beacon - in moments when, like men and women in the story, you will feel confused and lost in different situations.
Mr Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also a new deputy at the Swedish academy, which awards the Nobel Prize in Literature.
The book we're talking about here is very unusual - the author himself describes it, quite aptly, as "anti-historical work."
It contains several very well-known names, important treaties or famous battlies, on the contrary, it almost lacks ambassadors, energetic journalists and debates about tactics and equipment.
It's not so much a book about what's happened, the author explains, but rather "a book about what it's been like". It deals mainly with "feelings, impressions, experiences and moods".
"Beauty and grief" combines the wartime experiences of 20 more or less dozen men and women, on both sides of the barricade, from female students and botanists to climbers, doctors, paramedic drivers and officials.
Some of these people will become heroes.
Others become prisoners of war, lose their limbs, go crazy or die.
Their lives flinch like candle flames lit in a church - the new are added to the tangle of fortunes, while others quench.
Mr Englund's book differs from classical historical novels.
It regulates the notion that the First World War was only about fearsome trench warfare on the Western Front.
"Beauty and sadness" skillfully explores other places where war dramas took place: the Alps, the Balkans, the Eastern Front, Mesopotamia, East Africa.
Soldiers in this book solve the problems that bee nests fall on them, have to spend Christmas in the shade of the pyramids or deal with the presence of tse-tse flies.
Almost from the beginning it is a very moving reading.
War engulfs the lives of people like some kind of natural disaster, like Hurricane Katrina smelling of gunpowder.
When there's gunfire in the distance and you're a woman who's at home with your children, will you run away or stay?
Who's actually coming?
Almost no one understands what's going on, not even why the whole war has been unleashed.
"The vacuuum arising out of a lack of facts," noted Mr. Englund "was filled with conjectures, assumptions, hopes, fears, prejudices, conspiracy theories, dreams, nightmares and rumors."
"Beauty and grief" follows the stories of individuals such as Florence Farmborough, an English nurse in the Russian army, and Richard Stumpf, a young sailor serving in the German fleet.
Their stories are mostly taken from biographies, letters and other materials already published.
The descriptions of their lives may sound scary and engaging, but thanks to the incorporation of small moments and details, they are absolutely vivid and believable.
Many of them deal with hardship and dealing with it.
In the story of a German student, the author says: "Substitutes, substitutes everywhere."
"Coffee substitute, imitation of aluminum, imitation of rubber, paper bandages, wooden knobs."
It goes even further and describes food substitutes in Germany.
It refers to "meat made from pressed rice cooked in mutton fat (and supplemented by a false bone made of wood), tobacco from dried roots and dried potato peels, shoes with wooden outsoles".
It mentions that there were "837 registered meat substitutes in the production of sausages and 511 registered coffee substitutes".
Some stories are about honor and bravery.
One American finds inner impulses for wildness and brutality, after which he declares about war: "In the end, you will understand that this is human nature. Not sitting in comfortable armchairts with cigarettes and whiskey, evening newspapers or a good book and pretending that this mask of yours represents civilization and that there is no barbarian hiding behind the starched self inside."
On the contrary, the British soldier realizes that he will most likely die soon, and no one even notices it, let alone grieve.
"When a person finally accepts the fact that he sacrifices himself, he would at least expect this to happen before the eyes of a grateful audience," he writes.
Instead, however, he feels like a "convict he secretly hangs."
Other remarks relate to the departure of old Europe and the emergence of new horrors.
"Conflict has increasingly become an economic competition and a competitive struggle," writes Mr Englund, "a war between factories."
He captured what he calls "the new kind of beast of the incoming century: an eloquent and ideologically sworn mass killer in tailor-made dresses, which performs its bloodshed without rising from the table."
People behave completely unexpectedly, deceit alternates with heroism.
Mr Englund also describes soldiers who deliberately tried from prostitutes to catch some STD to avoid serving at the front.
"The most grotesque example of this behavior has been the business of gonococcal pus, which soldiers have bought and rubbed their genitals in the hope of ending up in the hospital," he writes.
"The most desperate have rubbed him into their eyes, which has often blinded them for the rest of their lives."
In this Swedish translation by Peter Graves, Mr Englund's prose is brisk, but not flamboyant, which is perfectly suited to his human theme.
In dozens of small ascents, it captures the way in which war "unleashed completely uncontrollable forces: extreme nationalism, social revolution, religious hatred."
People are beginning to ask why their leaders are forcing them to fight.
The best books about the First World War mostly rather than with a complex narrative worked with hints, for example "The First World War and Modern Memories" by Paul Fussell or novels such as "On the Western Front Calm" by Erich Maria Remarque.
Mr Englund prefers an unconventional conception.
His book has the most overwhelming ending, which is unparalleled in popular literature.
But I won't reveal it to you.
However, you will feel as if suddenly the author jumped out of the book, snatched the page from your list of heroes from your hand and set it on fire.
After a previous mistake, this time for a minute precisely planned raid
Hundreds of police officers participated, some in helmets used in street riots.
The night from Monday to Tuesday was chosen because Zuccotti Park should be almost empty at the time.
Nobody knew about the event except a few high-ranking officers. The others were first told that this was an exercise.
At the forefront of the event was police commissioner Raymond W. Kelly, whose presence highlighted how many problems the police department had to face during the operation.
They were not allowed to repeat cases from recent weeks, when, for example, the use of pepper sprays against protesters took place, which violated the rules of the department and, moreover, a wave of sympathy of the public spilled over to the side of squatters.
Therefore, a police operation aimed at clearing Zuccotti Park from the protesters was launched only after two weeks of careful planning and training.
The leaders were inspired by operations carried out in other cities.
A massive exercise in case of behavior in crisis situations took place in the Randalls Island section, which was a preparation for an event in Zuccotti Park.
Lead officials intensified so-called training to suppress riots - anti-terrorism measures involving the rapid transfer of a large number of police officers - with a focus on the city's Lower Manhattan district.
The last part of the training took place on Monday night, on the Manhattan side of the East River.
Orders to move to the park came literally "at the last minute" declared someone to whom the action was originally explained only as "exercises."
"I know a few officers who have also been called into this event, and no one has definitely told them that they are going to Zuccotti Park," said the person, who spoke only on condition that he would remain anonymous.
"Only the highest-ranking members of the ward knew he was going to Zuccotti Park."
One of the reasons for secrecy was a lesson the city had already received once.
On October 14, city officials planned to clear the park, but then withdrew from the plan, because as soon as the protesters heard about it, hundreds of them flocked to the park before the operation began.
Tuesday's event was attended by police officers from various units, including intervention groups from all over the neighborhood - dozens of police officers, who are routinely used to strengthen neighborhoods with higher levels of crime.
Mr Kelly explained that during the day, a large number of people come and go to the park again, so one hour at night was the ideal time to start the event.
"It was appropriate to do this at a time when there will be as few people as possible in the park," he declared.
By the time the troops set off, they lined up the border of Pike Slip and Franklin D. Roosevelt Drive, near Manhattan Bridge Bridge, trucks of emergency units with arched lamps and amplions.
Lights and recorded messages roaring from amplions have frightened many protesters.
When members of the riot squad entered the park in their blower windbreakers, many protesters packed up their belongings and left.
Police say she didn't touch a single tent until 1:45 a.m
Other teams of police officers were spotted gathering at the edge of the park so they could break into it if any arrests were needed.
Reporters were forced to leave the park.
The police department's chief spokesperson, Paul J. Browne, said this was the case for their safety.
However, many journalists believe that the police wanted to prevent them from seeing her troops in action, and in addition, some police officers treated them rudely.
Mr. Browne claimed that television cars on Church Street leading along the park's western border could have filmed seamlessly.
Police proceeded in the western direction, weaving through a dense jumble of the protester's personal belongings, including luggage and plastic bags full of clothing. Behind them, teams from the hygiene ward set off, immediately raking up all that was left in the park.
Some protesters who refused to move were dragged out of the park. Documenting imagery appeared moments later on the internet.
The fiercest group of protesters took refuge near the kitchen in their campground, which lay near the center of the park.
Some created a roadblock, and the police began their methodological arrests.
About ten people in the center of the campsite tied each other to each other.
And two people tied themselves to a tree, Mr Kelly said.
They were summoned by emergency services members to cut locks.
There were no arrests until 3:30 a.m
The entire operation was completed about 75 minutes later, the statement from the police said.
Mr Browne said approximately 142 people had been detained in the park.
Most of the arrests took place because of "organising riots and resisting arrests," he clarified.
The highest-ranking police officer at the scene of the event was Joseph J. Esposito, commander of the police department, the highest-ranking uniformed officer in the department.
Phil T. Pulaski, commander of detectives in the department, also personally participated in the operation.
Mr Kelly was present but "didn't participate in the management of the operation," Mr Browne said.
A second group of police officers waited along the east side of Broadway, between Liberty Street and Cedar Street, in case it needed to be summoned.
On Cortlandt Street, a block north of the park, and on Pine Street, a block south of the park, roadblocks were erected.
From these places, the police then unfolded a protective belt even further from Zuccotti Park.
At one point, police officers were spotted, many of whom were equipped with shields, pushing people even further beyond that border.
There were about 28 people on the northern border.
The wildest events of the night took place south of the park.
Around five o'clock in the morning, one protester was spotted south of Pine Street as he jumped onto the hood of a police car, and several others who were emitting air from the tires of a police van.
At one point, a piece of plywood flew out of the crowd.
At the end of the operation, one police officer and one protester were hospitalized.
Auxiliary workers' union will sue the city for dismissal
Unions representing hundreds of school auxiliaries who lost their jobs last monuch are planning to sue the city on Wednesday. They say layoffs weren't necessary and, moreover, it was discrimination, because of the unreasonable impact on schools attended by poor students.
Judiciary is probably the last option left to the unions in District Council 37, in their quest to reverse the layout decision.
On the seventh of October, six hundred and seventy-two school assistants, parental coordinators and other employees who were among the city’s worst-paid employees lost their jobs. Their dismissed union leaders described it as retribution of the city for failing to give it access to a health care fund managed by work crews last spring, through which the city wanted to tape a hole in the budget.
City officials categorically rejected the allegations.
At a city council meeting held last month, Education Secretary Dennis M. Walcott said the redundancies were part of a budget approved in June.
One of the members of the council immediately countered that there was no specific discussion in the budget about the dismissal of auxiliary staff.
Representatives of District Council 37 announced that they had already filled in the necessary documents and warned the Department of Education that they would sue it. On Wednesday, they will convene a press conference, where they will formally announce a lawsuit at Manhattan's U.S. Supreme Court.
The defendants in the trial will be Mr Walcott and the Department of Education, while the plaintiff's party will be eight redundancies, all of whom are African-American or Hispanic, as will most of the other assistants who have lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokeswoman for the city's legal department, said the city had not yet seen the lawsuit and will therefore not comment on individual allegations.
According to the New York Times, the unions focused on the amount of those released in poor parts of the city, thus wanting to support their claims of unfair conduct, because they believe that poor schools have lost more assistants because they have long been denied sufficient funds.
They point out that while there were no schools in Staten Island at all, and in the Upper East Side section only minimally, in Brooklyn, in District 23 in Brownsville, 17 school assistants lost their jobs, and even 46 assistants were fired in District 8 and 9 in the South Bronx.
He recalls that five out of eight assistants have lost their jobs at Harlem’s Public School, even though it’s a school where 68 percent of students live in poverty. The lawsuit says, “It’s not a neighborhood full of movable parents who can procure funds to offset the deficit in the budget.”
The school's Public School 36 budget has been cut by 3.26 percent this year, and the school has also lost money when the city has changed the method of calculating bonus finances for every poor student enrolled.
Among other things, unions say high school principals may have been puzzled in the manner in which a document authorising the dismissal of parental coordinators was written.
In the document, the word "excess" was used to describe the event, i.e. to cancel the position, even though this word is much more often used to describe the teacher's transfer from the school's payroll to a database of available workers whose wages are paid by the central authority.
"Sixty-six parental coordinators have been dismissed," the complaint's proposal reads, "and not just moved to a database for the position of available workers."
The mission is over, Congress is ready to adopt an agreement on the situation in Libya
Finally, Republicans and Democrats in Congress appear to agree on something related to an uprising in Libya.
However, before this happened, the revolution had to succeed, for a long time the ruling dictator Muammar Gaddafi had to be captured and killed, and the participation of U.S. troops ended.
On Tuesday, the Senate International Relations Commission will decide on a statement backed by President John Kerry (Mass.) together with Senators John McCain (Ariz.) and Joseph I Lieberman (Conn.), in which it should congratulate Libyans on their successful uprisings and U.S
"By doing so, we want to appreciate the courage of the Libyans, who have started rebuilding their country," Kerry said.
"Both sides of the political spectrum would like to express support for their democratic efforts."
These sentences may look innocent, but no debate about Libya has been easy until now.
Over the summer, lawmakers from both sides were constantly arguing among themselves and with the White House about the wording of a number of declarations for approval or, on the contrary, the rejection of the military role of the United States in the NATO mission in Libya.
In June, Kerry and McCain presented a resolution authorizing the limited use of U.S. armed forces in Libya.
The proposal was rejected in the House due to the different views of the two political parties, so it was never even voted on in the Senate.
However, on the same day, when this motion was rejected in the House, the chamber also rejected a measure that would halt the financing of U.S. operations in Libya.
In doing so, the U.S. mission in Libya has literally found itself in a legislative purgatory - Congress has criticized the way President Obama manages the mission, but its members have clearly not irritated enough to stop supporting him in his actions.
In the end, other events overtook the hesitancy of the Congress.
Rebels captured Tripolis in August and Gaddafi was killed on October 20.
Nato's mission officially ended on October 31.
Now the Senate may be ready to act.
In a statement, the International Relations Commission "congratulates the people of Libya for their immeasurable courage and admirable tenacity, which they have expended in trying to liberate their people" and "commend the men and women of the armed forces of the United States and their allies, who have engaged in an operation to protect the Libyan population, for extraordinary bravery and professionalism."
The statement also "confirms the national interest of the United States in the successful and irreversible transition to democracy in Libya."
Will the new proposal get the full support of the International Relations Commission, or will legislators once again be divided into two halves, as was the case with the approval of the June proposal?
Senator Richard G. Lugar (Ind.), the Republican Party's chief representative on the commission, opposed the latest version.
Lugara's spokesman said on Monday he hadn't seen the final version of the new proposal yet.
There will be a vote in the House this week on an amendment to the Constitution that would require a balanced budget, which should be the top legislative priority of almost all Republicans and Democrats in Congress.
This measure, enforced by Bob Goodlatt (Va.), will be the First Amendment to the Constitution relating to a budget or other topic that will be voted on by the entire House or Senate.
So far, despite considerable efforts, other legislators have not yet managed to push their proposals back to the vote itself.
Goodlatt's proposal is just one of 68 amendments to the constitution that were presented in Congress and touched on a wide range of different topics.
Some of them were the same - the same proposal put forward to both the House and the Senate - and others are only minor variations of others.
At least 15 versions of the balanced budget amendment and another handful can be found in the House, which would like to "regulate federal spending."
Some of the proposals are identical, but their proponents - especially aspiring Republicans who want to improve their reputation - can boast of their "authorship."
Both the House and the Senate's decree on budget regulation, which was approved in August, required both the House and the Senate to vote on an amendment regarding a balanced budget.
In March, 58 senators voted in favor of a non-binding statement in support of the creation of such an amendment.
In addition to the budget, several members also want to discuss limiting the number of periods in which members of the congress could serve, while José E. Serrano (N.Y.) wants to increase the number of periods allowed to be president.
Representative Jesse L. Jackson Jr. (Ill.) offered a variety of additions that, among other things, would guarantee the right to quality education and medical care.
An amendment prohibiting same-sex same-sex marriage and an appendix prohibiting the burning of the national flag remains a permanent proposal.
Some lawmakers also want to allow federal laws to be announced if two-thirds of states oppose them.
Although members of Congress appear to be very active in proposinging various amendments, they do not stand out from the average compared to previous years.
In the 111th Congress, they offered 77 amendments, while in the 100th Congress there were 66.
Their activity even fades compared to the early 1990s, when, according to statistics from the Senate Library, members offered more than 150 appendices every two years.
However, they were not much more successful than current politicians.
The last amendment that was added - the 27th Amendment - was ratified in 1992, but was actually drafted by Congress in 1789.
"Beaten, but not a loser," Gifford says in her first televised interview since the shooting
Ten months after being injured from point-blank range by a shooter at a public meeting with voters in Tucson, Gabrielle Gifffords (Ariz.) appeared on national television on Monday night, singing along with a tune from the Broadway show "The Sun Will Come Out Tomorrow" and saying more than going back to Congress to recover.
Giffords appeared on ABC News with Diane Sawyer. It was obvious that the creation of sentences was still causing her problems and she needed help walking.
The entire show documented her difficult convalescence, which was necessary due to an injury caused by a bullet, which punched her skull in the front, penetrated the left half of her brain, and came out of her head in the back.
Three months ago, she surprisingly returned to the House to triumphantly take part in a vote on the debt ceiling.
In her first television interview since the January shooting, during which six people were killed and 13 others wounded, Giffords smiled, laughed and sang - describing her recovery as "difficult."
When Sawyer asked her how she was feeling, forty-one-year-old Giffords replied: "Quite well."
An interview with Giffords and her husband, astronaut Mark Kelly, was part of an hour-long special episode of "20/20," which aired on the eve of the couple's new book, titled "Gabby: A Story of Courage and Hope."
The special has tracked Gifford's entire recovery since the January 8 shooting.
In the videos filmed by Kelly during the first few weeks, Giffords can be seen in a hospital bed with her head shaved and a long scar across her forehead.
While her husband encourages her, she can't do more at the time than they just lift one or two fingers.
Months later, Giffords is captured sitting in a wheelchair and therapists teaching her how to carry out simple activities.
Nodding your head.
Ripping of the lips.
And then, her first word: "What."
Within a few days, Giffords had uttered another word - "toast" when she demanded a change in her breakfast.
Videos aired by ABC show that music played a pivotal role for Giffords' recovery: The congresswoman sings on them, along with a melody therapist, about Cyndi Lauper's "Girls Just Wanna Have Fun" by Tom Petty.
Speaking during an interview, Gifford, who devotes two hours a day to therapy, told Sawyer she didn't remember anything from the day the shooting occurred.
Her husband said Gifford was not reading newspaper articles about the shooting until March 12, when she first asked him who was the six who had been killed in the incident.
Jared Lee Loughner, who is being detaineed at Missoury prison, where, under the influence of forcibly administered drugs, he is awaiting trial.
"A lot of people have died," Giffords told Sawyer.
"Terrible, terrible, terrible."
Kelly told Sawyer that at one point his wife said during her convalescence: "I lost."
"So I said to her straight away: Gabby, you didn't lose," Described Kelly.
"You've just been beaten.
But you'll make it, you'll put yourself together, and you'll come even stronger than ever."
Military forces encounter compassion rather than respect
The event was a gala on Wall Street that raised millions of dollars for war veterans in New York who have no roof over their heads.
Kid Rock sang a ballad about helplessness, frustration and loss.
Hundreds of soldiers, sailors, airmen and Marines gathered around him.
A solemnly-dressed mob rise and applaused enthusiastically.
"Both men and women serving in the military were considered heroes," said David Saltzman, who hosted the spring benefit.
Admiral Mike Mullen also attended the festivial evening. In addition, Admiral Mike Mullen, however, saw the role of military units somewhat differently.
"Everyone was introduced as an orphan," the officer wrote in an email.
I'm sure the organizers meant well.
I know it did.
But what I saw wasn't respect, it was regret.
So completely opposite impressions only illustrate the uneasy relationship between the army and the often measured, sometimes glorifying the American public.
The army is boundlessly included by praise for its sacrifices.
But this praise costs something, the soldiers say.
The public is increasingly behaving, as if they feel sorry for individuals in uniform.
"We're not victims at all," said brigadier General Sean B. MacFarland, who commanded troops in Iraq and will soon leave Afghanistan.
"But it seems that the only way some can support us is to put us in the role of unhappy souls."
This topic is very sensitive for army commanders who don't want to look ungrateful or spoil their relations with the public they serve.
They also realize that the anger they had to face with troops returning once from Vietnam was much worse.
As a result, most of the words about regret are uttered quietly and in private among war veterans.
When the two sons of retired Colonel Mark Canciano returned from combat operations, where they served as members of the Marine Corps, their father warned them that people outside the army would look at their service from two angles.
From the point of view of some it will feel respect and amazement, as they defeated enemy insurgents and visited exotic places.
But others will wonder if "there is an evil, violent veteran hiding below the surface," said Cancian, who participated in the fighting in Iraq and took his place in Washington's government after his return.
He argued that when he was looking for a job, some of the people who interviewed him inconspicuously indicated whether, so sooon after returning with the fighting, he could withstand the stress associated with working in Washington.
"When you talk about your service, you have to reckon with negative reactions," Cancian instructed his sons.
The military's concern stems, in part, from America's indifference to wars.
Achievements on the battlefield rarely reap recognition in a country that has little awareness of its own military and finds little of its own benefit in the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.
"As a nation, we no longer value military heroism as it used to be during World War II," Lieutenant General David Barno, who commanded U.S. troops in Afghanistan, said in a statement.
Instead, recognition from both politicians and the public concentrates mainly on the suffering of men in arms.
Troops are honored for the number of actions they have endured, the number of friends they have lost, or the extent of their injuries.
The military is under increasing pressure to crack down hard on rape
This week, it will decide whether a trial of 28 women and men against the military will be launched over alleged inaction in the case of rape.
If not, hundreds of other petitioners are prepared to file the lawsuit.
Elizabeth Lyman's first-class soldier was 25 years old on the night she was allegedly raped by her colleague in the Army, and she was 11 weeks pregnant.
It happened in October 2008 and she was headed to the barracks at Miramar air base north of San Diego. After dinner with a friend, she encountered her male colleague carrying a box of 12 beers.
He asked her if she could join her, that they could drink a little in her room.
"There's been a warning light in my head," Lyman said.
However, a native Texan completely ignored her objections.
I thought, "No, he's 19.
"He's from Texas," she said.
"I wish I'd listened to my instincts."
He claims that just a few minutes after they walked into her room, the man walked over to her, threw her on the bed and raped her.
This week, November 18, Lyman and 27 othreign or former members of the military will await the verdict of a hearing that could have a historic impact. This will take place in Arlington, Virginia, and will discuss whether the allegations against former Defense Secretary Robert Gates and his predecessor, Donald Rumsfeld, will go on trial.
The allegations alleges that Gates and Rumsfeld have failed in the fight against the spread of rape in the military, contrary to the constitutional rights of soldiers.
The charges were filed in February by Washington lawyer Susan Burke, and the plaintiff had 17 members at the time. Over the next few months, however, their number grew to 28-25 women and three men, all claiming to have been raped or otherwise sexually assaulted by their colleagues in the military, and that the military had failed in investigation, punishment, and any election for justice.
Burke says she's after the highest-ranking army commanders because the problem starts with them.
"The army is a directive system," he says.
"Who are the people who are in a position that they can prevent efforts to impose measures against such actions? High command."
Burke also says she has contacted at least 400 other survivors since February, many of whom could become part of other lawsuits filed in the future.
Her strategy: Rather than to group all charges into this case - she stacked all the eggs in one basket, as she herself says - will file lawsuits in multiple different cases, if necessary, so that the pressure doesn't ease.
The U.S. Attorney Neil MacBride, who along with his co-workers has filed a motion to dismiss the case.
The court documents obtained by Newsweek details the defense's strategy - in principle, current or former soldiers can't sue the military for injuries sustained as members of the armed forces.
According to a 1950 Supreme Court order called Feres Doctrine, the federal government is not responsible for injuries sustained by soldiers on active duty.
"Alleged injuries relate to the military service of the plaintiffs," the document said.
The Ministry of Defence has declined to comment on the matter.
However, things have changed since the 1950s.
Although women are still not officially allowed to serve in combat, it is only vocabulary: it is difficult to find a clearly defined frontline in modern wars.
In wars in both Iraq and Afghanistan, teams of female soldiers played an increasingly prominent role, especially in communication with civilian women occurring in a war zone.
Result: between these wars, 150 female soldiers fell - two-thirds of them directly in combat.
It's time for modernization in the military, Burke explains.
She's not alone in her fight, and three other attorneys are involved in the case.
At Yale Law School, visitors to a veteran seminar on legal services prepare lawsuits against four major military academies for supposedly developing an atmosphere supporting hatred of women.
Independently of this, a non-profit organization of U.S. Vietnamese veterans gathers the basis for its lawsuit against the military. This lawsuit focuses on the diagnosis of a "personality disorder", which was allegedly detected among rape victims, so that the military has a pretext for their release - according to activists such as Ana Bhagwati, who belongs to a group of human rights campaigner Service Women's Action Network, this is a very common practice.
Her group had already filed a lawsuit against a state-state-made organization for dealing with veterans in December, accusing them of discriminatory practices that should have occurred when processing requests for assistance by persons who claimed to have been sexually assaulted on duty.
Nine separate proposals were unveiled in Congress, presented by a group of senators and deputies from both political parties, proposing a number of correctives.
According to the Office for the Prevention and Resolution of Sexual Violence of the Ministry of Defence, 3 158 sexual assaults were reported in 2010.
However, this type of attacks remains unreported in most cases, and according to the Pentagon’s own estimates, this number is equivalent to approximately 13.5 percent of the estimated 19,000 incidents that occurred in a given year.
The report issued in March also dismantles the level of prosecution of suspects: In 2010, 20 percent of cases reported in the military came to court - half compared to the civilian justice system.
The plaintiffs, in the case of Deputy Burke, describe the attacks only as the first of a number of traumas.
For example, first-class soldier Lyman reported the alleged rape to military police less than an hour after it should have occurred.
During the night, she said, she was asked to repeat the details of her rape eleven times to various members of the police, doctors and commanders.
She underwent all the examinations necessary to determine the rape, and in her bed was found the blood of an attacker - from the wound she did to his arm.
Six months later, in April 2009, Lyman underwent hearings before the court, during which she was asked questions about whether she was making love with her boyfriend on the day of the attack.
Six people were summoned to testify in favor of an alleged offender who was eventually acquitted.
"I remember the day when the verdict was handed down," Lyman says.
"I had the feeling of giving birth right there on the spot - I ran out of the courtroom with a scream."
"They put me in a psychiatric unit, and when I got out, I remember saying to my commanding officer: It wasn't his trial. He was mine."
Lymans ordered visiting a military psychiatrist, who eventually diagnosed her with a personality disorder, calling it unfitilited to serve.
In January 2010 she was discharged from the army without honor and therefore without entitlement to retirement.
Whether the Lyman case and other plaintiffs moves to the next stage or not, it's a run for the long haul, says John Turley, law professor at George Washington Law University.
Of course, he notes, "From my point of view, they should be able to progress to the next stages of discussion."
Among them and the verdict, however, stands the aforementioned doctrine, which has been criticized since the first time it has seen the light of day.
I am a lifelong critic of Feres Doctrine.
The army is several decades behind because their duties do not give adherence to the same degree of motivational incentives and deterrents.
He adds: "Chances are heavily against them... but it's important for them to try."
There will only be a change when good people are willing to fight.
If a decision is not made in their favour at the hearing, Burkevá, the plaintiff’s lawyer, will collect hundreds of other persons ready to file a lawsuit and begin working on further cases related to the subject.
"We won't stop fighting with the military on this issue, from where there won't be reform," he says.
Or until we die, depending on what comes first.
Walls have eyes: How do scientists examine you on Facebook
Before Anders Behring Breivik became the new face of right-wing extremism in Europe, he was just another young man who presented his anti-immigration views online.
On Monday, Braivik, who pleaded guilty to the July murderous rampage, after which there were 77 deaths left in Norway, attended his first public court hearing.
Although Breivik could act alone, he was certainly not alone in cyberspace: he spent a lot of time at the computer during the preparation of the attack, chatting with some of the millions of nationalists supporting right-wing groups on social networking sites.
After this summer's tragedy, scientists want to learn more about these people.
But how to find them?
Easily - just go to Facebook.
"We've realized that getting them isn't really that hard at all," says Jamie Bartlett, lead author of a recently released report by a British research team on European digital populism.
The content of personal information on Facebook is so extensive, Bartlett says that it was enough for scientists to simply use advertising on the site and, thanks to this, with complete accuracy to determine the desired demographic group - something that marketing workers have been doing for years.
Bartlett’s team across Europe found half a million fans of right-wing groups, after which they focused their attention and sent them advertisements. However, instead of inviting them to a newly formed group or offering them some dietary products, they called on them to fill out a questionnaire that raised questions about the level of their education, their relationship to violence and optimism due to their own future.
Through Facebook, Bartlett and his colleagues managed to get results from more than 10,000 fans of 14 different right-wing groups in 11 European countries - without having to leave the office at all.
"It's a completely new way of conducting research," Bartlett notes.
Some of the results aren’t so surprising: online supporters of right-wing groups are mostly young men who arouse the rising rate of immigration.
On the other hand, "those who combine their online activism even with off-line activism are more reasonable, more democratic and less violent than those who remain constantly hidden behind a computer screen."
DEMOS team work is just one example of how Facebook is becoming a new tool in the hands of scientists.
Research teams, health scientists and political researchers use these sites to study everything, from health problems to social trends hiding status updates, posts on walls, and "Like" buttons.
Facebook reports more than 800 million active users. Each of them on average provides three different data per day, making this social network literally an "information supernova", which is behind the development of many new surveys. The number of academic works, in whose name the word Facebook appears, has risen to almost 800% compared to the state five years ago.
For some scientists, the magic of Facebook is that it gives them the opportunity to study people without having to bother them somewhere on the street.
"Previously, when you wanted to conduct a survey among the people of the British National Party, it was very difficult to identify them."
"You'd have to get to a party where you'd never be allowed," Bartlett says.
"All of this is breaking down Facebook - you're just going straight towards them."
Others say the site can offer a way to identify and avoid a social-health problem.
In a recent study by Dr. Megan Moren of the University of Wisconsin, the research team found that university students discussing their drunken outputs on Facebook were far more at risk for the problem of alcoholism than students who were silent on the subject.
Moren proposes that peers of students monitor the site and come up with an offer of help to a student who publishes too many drugged statuses.
"The problem can't be solved if you can't diagnose it," Dimitri Christakis, co-author of Dr. Moren and director of the Center for Health, Behavioral and Children's Development at the Children's Research Institute in Seattle, told the Washington Post.
"We've found a way to identify endangered children we wouldn't otherwise have had a chance of discovering."
Facebook is constantly probing the data of its users.
The information team of the website has prepared statistics on the context of relational statuses and Valentine's Day, turnout in 2010 and the coefficient of satisfaction of the nation (mainly using the information to which they had access - proponents of internet freedom have long argued that the site should make its information treasure available to others).
But Facebook users know they're being tracked, whether they like it or not - in exchange for the ability to play Scrabble with a friend who's thousands of miles away, you need to provide the page with your personal information.
But what if Facebook users become part of research they don’t even know about?
Scientists have clearly defined rules regarding how to collect data off-line. However, online data is accessible to everyone.
"I don't think most users didn't even think that scientists could peer into their profiles," says Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Lab, about a 2006 study conducted using his students' public Facebook walls.
"As far as they were concerned, they took it as if there was only information between them and their friends."
Subsequently, it is necessary to consider the issue of methodology.
Even in off-line research, there is no guarantee that the subject is completely sincere.
It is not possible to verify on Facebook how much the information on the profile and posts on the wall are true.
"What you're saying on Facebook and what you're doing outside of Facebook, that's two totally different things," Selwyn explains.
That’s why Selwin, who has been researching new technologies for many years, thinks that pencil and notebook remain the best possible tool that can be used in research.
"When you're going out into the real world and talking to real people, it's something you can't replace," she says.
“Social research should be about society and social activities – and a hell of a lot of social activities are still taking place offline.”
"Cold and inhuman": Anders Behring Breivik participates in his first public trial
Anders Behring Breivik, a man who pleaded guilty to orchestrating two attacks during which 77 people were killed in Norway this summer, described himself as a "resistance fighter" during his first public court hearing in Oslo.
"I am a military commander in the Norwegian resistance movement and among the Norwegian Templar knights," he declared before the court, where 500 people gathered.
"I raise an objection to the Court of Justice, because organizations promoting the ideology of hatred have given you your mandate and because it promotes multiculturalism."
"I confess my deeds, but I confess no blame for them."
Judge Torkjel Nesheim interrupted Breivika during this monologue because he "didn't want to give Breivik the opportunity to use this hearing as a stage from which he could proclaim his views."
For the same reason, Breivik, who had brought a ready-made speech with him, declined to be allowed to address relatives of his victims at the end of the hearing.
Nesheim also ordered the police to keep Breivik in custody for another 12 weeks, forbid him from any intercourse with the media for 4 weeks, and announced that the final authorities would have complete control over all his visits and all correspondences for eight weeks.
About 30 relatives of the victims and survivors attended the hearing.
Some came in the hope that they would be able to look Breivik in the eye, others to make sure that he was held tightly locked.
They all took part in in order to push the case to an early closure.
"It seemed to me that it looked cold and inhumane," one survivor of the attack told NRK commentator.
"It wasn't pleasant, but I can say for myself that after I saw and heard the suspect, I mentally moved back a little further."
Breivik, in a dark suit and a blue tie, remained calm and professional throughout the hearing. When coming and going to the building without difficulty, due to the present journalists and survivors in the eyes.
Despite Breivik's bizarre notion that he is part of a larger "resistance" movement, the judge noted that he was not insane, noting that there was no evidence that he had any accomplice.
For some, it may be a consolation that the June 22 attacks appear to be the work of a single extremist and not a group of radicals.
However, this does not automatically diminish the grief and confusion that are still in the minds and hearts of the entire nation.
"I wish she'd fall out like a monster, but that's not the case," said a relative of one of the victims.
"If it did, everything would be much simpler."
7 Lessons from a Lost Interview with Steve Jobs in a movie heading to theaters this week
As soon as Steve Jobs died on October 5, the opinions of the people who compared him to Henry Ford began to appear and chose him as his genius the Greatest Modern Inventor.
In a recently rediscovered interview by Jobs, which has been dusted off for a special film heading to cinemas this week, for all Apple fans, his creative mind perfectly understood the business processes and workflows behind his products.
He was an amazing designer, a dreamer and a self-proclaimed member of the hippie movement, but at the same time also a pedantic organizer and a person who matches every trifle to the smallest details - he believed that many entrepreneurs suffer from "the notion that a really great idea is 90 percent of their work... [but] between a great idea and an excellent resulting product, there is still a long way to work."
In 1995, Bob Cringely worked on the Triumph of Computer Fool's Triumph, which deals with the dawn of personal computers. During filming, a unique, more than one-hour interview took place.
It had been ten years since Jobs was forced to leave Apple, and he was busy with his new computer company, NeXT, and was anxiously looking forward to how the general public would adopt the Internet.
Only a short part of Cringely's interview was eventually used on the TV show, and the original copies of the interview were rumored to have been lost while being transported.
Shortly after Jobs' death, however, VHS copies were found in the director's garage.
Just this material, slightly edited, forms the basis of the 68-minute film Steve Jobs: The Lost Interview, which is heading to selected cinemas this Wednesday and Thursday.
For someone famous for his reluctance to give longer conversations, Jobs here seems surprisingly enthusiastic that he can talk about his technological philosophy and trading strategies.
And he's also proving to be a very thoughtful man.
Four times during the conversation, there will be a longer pause between the question and the answer - 10 to 15 seconds of thinking, when you can see how Jobs thinks the answer perfectly so that it is as accurate and accurate as possible.
There are also moments when Jobs’ perfectionism comes to the surface, acting impatiently on Cringely’s questions and propending him to get to more important issues and topics more quickly.
The interview was filmed even before the Pixar, iPod or iPhone era, and there are terrifyingly prophetic ideas when Jobs evaluates what other companies are doing well and badly, offering their own vision of the future in the field of computer technology.
It’s one thing to read the posthumous reviews of Jobs’ career, but there’s something raw and inspirational about Jobs, whom we see here as an eager dreamer in flourishing his genius.
He has his own vision of what things could and should look like.
It wasn't too long, and after expressing his ideas, he also began putting them into practice.
A quick summary of seven scenes from the Lost Interview, which is definitely worth noticing:
How he learned how to manage a business:
"During the years of business, I've always asked: Why are you doing this? and the answer has always been the same: Well, that's just how it's done."
No one knows why he's doing what he's doing, he's never thinking in depth about things related to business.
That's what I found out.
Jobs continues by describing his efforts to simplify accounting at Apple.
He was completely puzzled by the way the costs were recorded - often beginning with the inscription "standard costs", they could only guess. At that moment he decided to go his own way - he developed an automatic tool that guaranteed that they could determine the trading costs in a moment.
How he made a mischief for the Pope:
Jobs recalls the "Blue Box," built with Steve Wozniak - a device that allowed people to effectively hack into a phone company and make free calls anywhere in the world.
There's been a lot written about the device in the past, but Jobs's enthusiasm, when he describes the Blue Box as a tool of power, is literally contagious.
It tells how he and Wozniak tested the device using a paid phone and allowed themselves to be switched from one AT&T network to another during the call, while using as many satellites as possible.
"At least half a dozen times we've been around the world like this, so when we've shouted something on a paid phone, it sounded a minute later from the other phone at the door," Jobs laughs.
"We were young, and we found ourselves able to build a thing that's capable of managing billions of dollars worth of infrastructure around the world."
The two of us made a small thing that was able to control a much larger giant - it was an incredible experience, and in my opinion it would neveratude Apple without it.
Jobs proceeds with a description of the joke, which he and Wozniak almost made to the end. Through the Blue Box in the middle of the night, they called the Vatican and asked if they could talk to the pope, impersonating Henry Kissinger in the best possible way.
Gradually, members of the Catholic Church were summoned to talk to an American diplomat in the middle of the night, and it wasn't until they aroused the pope himself to the phone that the pair chuckled and put the receiver down.
How he fell in love with technology (and made a phone call with Bill Hewlett):
When he was 12 and looking for spare parts to build a frequency meter, he finded Bill Hewlett's number in his phone book and called him.
Not long after their 20-minute interview, Jobs already had a secured summer brigade at Hewlett-Packard.
“It influenced me unbelievably. It was the only firm I’d known at the time and shaped my perspective on what such a company actually was and how it was supposed to treat its employees.”
Jobs later became one of HP's employees who visited corporate research laboratories in Palo Alto, where he saw "the first desktop computer that had ever been made."
He's been the size of a suitcase, he's had a CRT monitor, and I fell in love with him immediately.
As a teenager, in HP, I progressed higher and higher, hanging around that computer and starting to write programs for it.
How he invents innovations:
In several places, Jobs talks during an interview about how he invents new products on the fly.
At the beginning of his career, when he set out to sell a pair of printed circuit boards, one customer asked him to assemble the entire computer for him.
At his disposal had only 30 paid days, during which he had to devise, assemble and deliver the finished equipment.
When he was later at Apple, he recalls the problems they faced in developing a computer mice:
"I remember arguing quite fiercely... they were shouting at me that it would take five years [assembling a mouse that would cost 300 USD] until I got tired of it, I came out and found a designer."
Ninety days later, we were able to produce a perfectly reliable mouse for 15 USD.
How many companies run out of breath:
Long before he led the resurrection of Apple, Jobs foresightfully predicted that many industry leaders would waver in their vision and lose their market share.
"Let's just say you're working at IBM or Xerox, so you're doing better copiers or printers, so what's next?"
You have a monopoly on the market, so the company can no longer be more successful.
At that point, sales and marketing are already in control, so companies are run by people who are in charge of just that, while the people in charge of their own products are excluded from the decision-making process.
The technological genius who led the company to its unshakable position is now replaced by people who have no idea of good or bad products - dexterity and technical knowledge are essential.
That's exactly what's happened at Xerox.
Xerox could own literally the entire computer industry.
It could have been ten times bigger than it could have been Microsoft of the 1990s...
Instead of winning, however, they made a loss.
How it takes innovation as a form of art:
"There's still a long journey and a lot of work between a great idea and an excellent final product... and as the great idea evolves, it's changing and growing."
You'll learn a lot about him during that time.
You have to accept a number of compromises - certain things simply electrons, glass, robots or factories you can't force.
You have to keep 5 000 things in mind constantly - different concepts - and compose them together...
In the end, it depends on taste - you're trying to think about the best things humanity has ever created, and in a certain way you're trying to project them into what you're doing.
Picasso said good artists copy, but the best artists steal.
I've never been ashamed of stealing the best ideas.
Macintosh was so amazing and successful because the people who worked in it were outstanding musicians, poets, artists, zoologists, historians and coincidentally also the best computer experts in the world.
If it weren't for computers and new technologies, they'd be able to do amazing things in completely different industries.
Scalia and Thomas had dinner with opponents of the health care law at a time when their case was handled by the court
On the day when the Supreme Court sattered behind closed doors to discuss a politically controversial question about whether it would accept an objection to President Obama's health care law, two of his judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, attended a gala dinner sponsored by a law firm that will take part in hearings before trial.
The incident occurred last Thursday, when all nine judges gathered to discuss the submitted motions.
One of the cases discussed was a lawsuit filed by 26 states questioning the radical overhaul of health care passed by Congress last year - a bill that mobilized conservative activists around the world.
The judges have agreed to hear the lawsuit. However, a 5.5-hour hearing is expected in March, the outcome of which, moreover, will stir up the presidential election, which is scheduled for 2012 and will be in full swing at the time the decision is issued.
The lawyer, who will appear before the court and try to comply with the denial of the law, will most likely be Paul Clement, who served as attorney general of the United States under George W. Bush.
Clement's Law Firm, Bancroft PLLC, was one of nearly two dozen companies that helped sponsor the Federalist Society group's annual dinner, which has been dedicated for years to defend conservative legal principles.
Another company that has sponsored the dinner, Jones Day, represents one of the trade associations questioning the law, the so-called National Federation of Independent Entrepreneurship.
Another sponsor was pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc, for whom huge funds are at stake in the process.
The dinner took place at a Washington hotel a few hours after a court discussion on the subject of the case.
Mitch McConnell, the Republicans' top official in the Senate and a confirmed opponent of the health care law, also attended.
And introducing guests? Scalia and Thomas.
It's nothing new: This pair of judges have been taking part in the Federalist Society event for years.
And it's certainly nothing that's contrary to the rules of ethics.
In fact, these judges are acquitted of an obligation to observe the principles of conduct by which lower federal judges are subject.
However, if this were not the case, their behaviour would certainly fall under Principle C4, which states that "the judge may take part in the actions that are currently related and other organizations held for the purpose of obtaining funds, but the judge must not be a speaker, a guest of honour, nor must he be listed on the agenda."
Nevertheless, Scalia and Thomas’ relationship with the two law firms operating in the case, as well as companies with a huge financial interest in the case, were richly sufficient to alert activists calling for ethics in government.
"This shocking violation of ethics and indifferency towards the code of conduct is contrary to several judges' assertion that the Supreme Court behaves according to the same rules that apply to all other federal judges," Bob Edgar, president of Common Cause, said in a statement.
"Judges were drinking and having dinner at a social benefit, along with attorneys who are awaiting hearing their case in court."
Their presence and assistance in raising funds through this action undermines the feet with all claims of non-bias, which is unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas showed little sympathy for their critics and told them that they, too, seamlessly combined their judicial obligations with the actions of groups such as the Federalist Society.
Thomas's wife Ginni is a high-ranking Conservative activist.
Conservatives also argue that Judge Elena Kagan has an ethical problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan worked as the attorney general under President Obama when the first objections to the law appeared before the court.
Kagan's critics are pushing her to renounce involvement in the case, arguing that she fought for the law at the time as much as she could now be unbiased.
Kagan didn't indicate that she was about to resign.
Booening has won a recognose contract for US$18-billion worth of jet jets
Emirates is ordering 50 double-aisle Boeing 777 jet aircraft with the possibility of buying 20 more.
Boeing also sells six Boeing Dreamliner jets to Aman Air, for which it collects more than US$1 billion.
When a company receives an order for 50 pieces of its products, it is usually no reason to celebrate.
However, if it's Boeing Co.'s aircraft maker and the price of these 50 jets is a total of US$18 billion, it's different.
Equally important is where Boeing received the contract from: the Middle East, a growing gold mine for future aircraft orders.
According to predictions released on Monday, the Chicago-based company estimates that Middle-East airlines will need US$450 billion worth of US$550 billion over the next 20 years.
Over the weekend, Boeing announced its largest order in terms of dollar value for a commercial aircraft - 50 two-aisle jet Boeing 777s.
The sensational contract came from Dubai-based Emirates.
At the 2011 Dubai Air Show, Boeing announced that the airline had the option to purchase an additional 20 aircraft, bringing the total value of the order to US$26 billion.
On Monday, Boeing announced that Oman Air had ordered six Boeing 787-8 aircraft, sometimes also known as the Dreamliner, which boasts significantly better fuel consumption and equipment for passengers.
The total price of these six aircraft exceeds US$1 billion.
"This is an excellent news for the entire Boeing company," said Neal Dihora, an analyst for Morningstar.
"The quality of these specific customers is very high."
Many airlines in the Middle East belong to local oil-mining nations.
This means that they have not only money to pay for planes, but an increase in the price of fuels - mostly detrimental to the airline, because it is one of its biggest costs - is not so crucial, because the country itself earns higher oil prices.
This is important because it is not as likely as that of other airlines that they could cancel or delay aircraft orders due to an increase in fuel prices, Dihora says.
"They have such natural protection," he says.
These contract from Emirates consolidates Boeaing's leading position in a wide-hulled aircraft market, bringing Airbus SAS into the tight, which wanted to try to undermine Boeing's dominance with its A350 transport model.
Boeing's victory comes four months after Airbus officials announced that the first flight of their largest A350 jet, which is a direct competitor to the 777-300ER, will be postponed.
The postponement occurred because customers demanded a greater utility load of the aircraft and the range, so the manufacturer took the time to give the machine more engine power.
The price of Boeing Co's actions has risen by 1.5% to US$67.94.
The sister of the Mexican president has apparently been defeated in Michoacan elections
Luisa Maria Calderon claims her opponent, Fausto Vallejon of PRI, was helped by drug traffickers in pursuit of the governor's chair.
Felipe Calderono's sister appears to have lost the post of governor in Michoacán in a violent state election. On Monday, she subsequently declared that drug traffickers had helped one of her opponents to victory.
Preliminary results were attributed to the leadership in the governor's elections of the western state to Fausto Valley by the Institutional Revolutionary Party, or PRI.
"The interventions of members of organized crime in the entire electoral process, and especially until yesterday's election process itself, is alarming not only for Michoacán, but for our entire country," Luisa Maria Calderon said in an interview with the radio on the day after Sunday's elections.
"They threatened our candidates, members of an opinion polling group... confiscating ballot boxes, building road roadblocks... and letting people vote to order for PRI.
The PRI party ruled Mexico for seven decades until 2000, when it lost the presidency.
However, he is planning an early return to power, and victory in elections in Mikhoacán is an important step in this effort.
The PRI party hopes to will will win the July presidential election as well.
It seems Vallejo beat Calderon, who still leaded very closely the day before the election.
Calderon, a candidate for the Conservative party of her National Action Party brother, or PAN, declined to recognize Vallejon as a winner.
Silvano Aureolis, a candidate for the left-wing Democratic Revolution Party, or PRD, which until now has dominated the governor’s office. For the quarrelsome and unlucky persecuted party, it is a very unfortunate loss before the 2012 presidential election.
Aureoles also declined to accept the preliminary results as valid.
Vallejo, a former mayor of the state's capital named Morelia, denies any relations with drug traffickers and urges other candidates to recognize the results.
Michoacán has long been dominated by drug cartels specializing in marijuana, heroin and methamphetamines.
It is President Calderon's native state, and it was in Michoacán in December 2006 that he launched a military fight against traffickers.
However, the violence has persisted.
The mayor of PAN, Luisa Marie Calderon's supporter, was murdered just a week before the election, and many of the candidates withdrew from the election out of fear.
No matter how the election results in Michoacán look for the PRI party, victory will help the party gain a new impetus.
Enrique Pena Nieto's anticipated representative of the PRI party in the presidential election, from Wahington, congratulated Vallej and said: "I think this victory, given the coming year, could be very encouraging."
ADB calls on Asia to help save eurozone
Asian Development Bank urged India and China to be prepared to help save the Eurozone from its severe debt crisis, otherwise there could be an overall downturn in the world economy, which would also have a negative impact on the growth of economies in Asia.
Rajat Nag, CEO of ADB-bank based in Manila, said the world's two fastest-growing economies must "do everything in their power" to contribute to a rapid recovery of the currency bloc through the International Monetary Fund, or direct bilateral agreements.
He warned that Bric [Brazilia, Russia, India, China] countries were monitoring all European difficulties "too unexcitedly" and declared that Asian financial assistance, combined with European leadership and resources, would help ignite the long-term collapse of the global economy.
"We're all in this together."
"So it's useful for anyone who could help Europe out of the crisis," he told the Financial Times during an interview at the World Economic Forum in Mumbai.
"Asia may be protected to some extent, but it's certainly not immune."
"So if China and India can help, they definitely should do it."
For several months now, the European Monetary Association has been severely tested by a severe debt crisis.
In recent days, Italy's borrowing costs have risen dangerously and the prime ministers of both Italy and Greece have resigned.
Europe's rising debt crisis has lifted fears that it could bring the global economy into recession once again, and calls have begun to emerge that emerging leading economies will unite and help the Eurozone find a solution.
Mr Nag predicted that all Asian support would be channelled through the IMF, but noted that mutual assistance - such as the purchase of bonds of the European Union's Financial Assistance Fund, an institution for European financial stability - would provide greater bargaining opportunities for Asian partners of European countries.
Anand Sharma, India's trade minister, said "India will do what it will do," to help the Eurozone, as its own economy now suffers a decline in exports and dried-up of foreign capital tributaries.
"No one wants the Eurozone to remain unstable and uneasy," he declared.
"We are facing a difficult task and we have to maintain a high level of growth."
"It's not an alternative, it's a bare fact, because how else could we find employment for tens of millions of our young men and women."
Others argue that emerging economies, such as Indian economies, have nothing to help European economies when forced to face their own serious economic problems.
Ashutosh Varshney, a professor at Brown University in the U.S., said it would be politically very difficult to explain aid for Europe to India's 1.2-billion-dollar population when 800 million of them live from USD 2 or less for a day.
"They might once find out that the Greeks retire in their 50s and enjoy their holidays on beaches, which is probably hard to explain to them," he remarked.
Lee Howell, director-general of the World Economic Forum, similarly questioned why India's supplies should be used to maintain a well-paid job to many Greek citizens in poorly managed and loss-making sectors such as railing.
Mr Nag stressed that the eurozone crisis threatens a "dangerous domino effect."
The bank's forecast that there will be 7.5% of economic growth in Asia between 2011/2012 is now facing a "risk of decline" because of a European threat.
He declared that vulnerable emerging markets must create "unpredictable spending plans" in order to be protected from declines and significant outflows of capital from their economies.
Warnings from the Pentagon about possible defense cuts
Defense Secretary Leon Panetta on Monday in Congress put it under the cauldron when he warned that looming automatic cuts in the budget could jeopardize national security and trigger a financial chain reaction starting at the Pentagon and continuing through the battlefield in Afghanistan to civilian assembly lines.
The Pentagon will already have to spend a US$450 billion budget cut over the next ten years, but is now worried about a reduction of US$600 billion if Congress fails to enter into a spending deal.
"The effects of these cuts would be devastating for the entire resort," Panetta wrote in a letter to Senator John McCain, Arizona, and Lindsey Graham, South Carolina.
He said Congress' inability to come up with a budget agreement and the resulting so-called attachment will cause a 23% lumpage decline and halt many new projects.
"Due to such a huge reduction, used in this imprudent way, it will not be possible to carry out most of our construction or shipping projects - you can not buy only three quarters of the building or ship. Moreover, there could be serious damage to further modernization efforts," Panetta wrote to senators.
"We'll also force many of our civilian employees to separate, and since the reduction is supposed to take place so quickly, we'll almost certainly have to send civilians on forced leave to be able to meet target requirements."
"These changes can shake with the faith of those who care about our army and seriously damage its preparedness."
In the end, the cuts will also affect combat troops, Panetta announced.
"While the accounts for the financing of possible war operations overseas will not be directly affected by this stop, the operations there will be negatively affected by serious changes in other budgets," Panetta warned.
"There will also be cuts in contract personnel, which will result in delays in agreed-upon contracts supporting our people's wartime efforts."
"Paid employee statuses will be reduced, which will also result in delayed payments to suppliers and subsequent disruption of legal and political support."
Both senators wrote Panett off ten days ago and asked him for details regarding the possible impacts on automatic cuts at the Department of Defense.
"The impedimences of the shutdown at the Department of Defense could cause a rapid degradation of the view of the United States as the world's military superpower."
"We are strictly opposed to this harsh measure," the senators announced in a joint statement on Monday afternoon when they released Panetto's letter.
"It's not a conclusion we can live with, and we certainly don't want to sign under it."
"Planned cuts are a threat to the national security of the United States, which we should not allow."
"It will only be seen whether Panetto's letter and recent warnings will increase the pressure on Congress approving the budget and amplifying votes calling to omit the Pentagon from a list of cut areas.
Panetta has recently become increasingly open in talks about possible cuts, even though he has switched to his senior position at the Pentagon with years of experience approvinging budgets in Congress and the White House, so he knew he was embarking on a tough fight.
At a press conference held this minutual week, the defense minister hinted at a brief picture of what his resort might expect - the shell of the army, inside which, however, the core will be missing.
"He'll be like a ship without sailors."
"He'll be like a brigade without ammunition."
"He'll be like an air force without trained pilots."
"He's going to be like a paper tiger, like an army of barracks, buildings and bombs without a sufficient number of trained soldiers who could complete the mission," Panetta said at the beginning of a speech at the Pentagon.
"He'll be like a unit without morality, unprepared and incapable of coping with a possible enemy."
"As a result, he will welcome aggression..."
In an appendix supplied with a letter to McCain and Graham, Panetta specifies how reduction will result in "significant operational risks: late responses to crises, conflicts and disasters; a significant reduction in airborne options and deployments around the world; a presumed unacceptable risk in future combat operations."
In addition, Panetta added that some of the biggest defense projects could await terminations, including those that are already being tested, as well as those that are only in the early stages of planning.
The list included, for example, the project of the F35 aircraft, a planned new bomber, a next-generation ballistic submarine, a new coastal warship, and a new ground-based combat vehicle, to which the army and Marines wanted to replace the humvee.
Stopping further development and testing of the F35 would save US$80 billion over the next decade, but its proponents say it is a necessary next step in improving the military, which needs to be tackled by possible threats from China and other rivals.
How to Recognize a Lie
Taking a glance at recent headlines suggests how serious and ubiquitous lies and deceptions are in everyday life.
Republican presidential candidate Herman Cain is full-bogged with allegations that he has sexually harassed several women - both sides say the other is lying.
Administrators at Penn State University were charged with perjury as they tried to hide records that a retired football coach sexually assaulted the boy.
Last week, French President Nicolas Sarkozy was caught telling U.S. President Barrack Obama that Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu was a liar.
The lie managed to destroy entire careers and obscure the stability of many countries.
The New York congressman and internet exhibitionist Anthony Weiner made a fool of himself when he published information that was immediately debunked by unquestionable evidence.
Former presidential nominee John Edwards has been accused of abusing funds from his campaign to try to conceal his illegitimate affair.
And no one will ever forget the media circus that tore around Bill Clinton when he lied during his second term in office about his sexual relationship with Monica Lewinsky.
It's teeming with stories of cheating at school and university everywhere.
There are cases when teachers themselves give test answers to their students, just to look good in their assessments themselves.
Mentors who should teach the opposite send a message that lie and deception are acceptable.
How mucus are we dealing with?
Studies show that in a single day you lie somewhere between 10 and 200 cases.
Assume that many of them are merciful lies.
Another study showed that strangers lied three times in the first ten minutes of the first meeting in their lives.
Determining lies, or "recognizing lies," is an essential skill that everyone should acquire, whether for personal or professional reasons.
However, it is not a social game that is similar to, shall we say Charade, when a person shouts: "I have you". The recognition of deception is a serious branch of science working with scientific data collected during the last six decades at prestigious universities during detailed research projects, especially in the field of psychology and philosophy.
One of the results of the research is that most old myths about lies have been debunked.
Liars look into your eyes.
It's not like they're stuttering, stuttering, blushing or sewing.
However, do not deduce that it is somehow difficult to spot and detect liars.
Trained lie identifier can reveal the truth from sentence construction, small expressions in the face, the way of creating questions and their timing.
I’ve spent several years collecting scientific knowledge from a large-scale and emerging lie-recognition industry, and it’s clear that deception is a modern skill that can be easily learned, and in today’s complex world it can be of great help – especially if your job responsibilities include hiring, conducting interviews, negotiating, or leading people.
Good liars are able to read well in others too, let them relax in vigilance, direct their emotions and intuitively realize how the other side accepts them.
We know from research that extroverts lie more often than introverts, that men uttered more "self-centered" lies, while women say more lies "focused on others" - mostly to protect someone's feelings - that married people lie less often to their partners rather than single people (but the lies they say are usually "outrageous").
We also know that if you're perceived as a sinner, others feel less guilty when they lie to you.
How do you know if someone's lying?
Observe the normal behavior of the subject first.
That's called "initial observations."
Thanks to him, he will have a later finding with something to compare.
Notice the posture of the subject, his laughs, working with his voice.
You should know if someone routinely taps their feet all the time so you don't unduly accuse him of lying just because he starts tapping his foot in the middle of the meeting.
After that, try to determine clusters of false verbal and non-verbal behavior.
However, take this only as warning signs, not as evidence of lies.
People uttering a lie can stop moving through the upper half of their body when trying to remember their made-up story, they can rotate the tips of their shoes to the door, lean towards the exit, significantly change the posture, or show "relief after the end of the conversation" - an exaggerated relieved respite and a change of position when there is a difficult confession behind them.
People conducting interviews often issue a false signal that the interview is over, only to see what relief will look like after its termination.
Consider the language of the subject, too.
Famous is the story of how Scott Peterson once misclaimed and used past tense when he claimed that his murdered wife was alive, which ran a nationwide search for her body.
Deceptive individuals can also express themselves in proportion: "I have had no sexual relationship with the lady... Miss Lewinsky" or repeat the difficult question.
The most common verbal characters are only very slight.
Someone can use a lot of "mitigating language" when they answer a difficult question: "Well... to tell you the truth... from what I know... to the best of my knowledge."
We don't learn too many facts in such an answer, and it's often another warning sign.
There is no spell to reveal every lie, but it is possible to acquire the skills by which a lie can be settled.
Using these skills, a person will have a better chance of not finding a liar and a crook in his personal and work.
Controversial plan to build oil pipeline will be redirected after the threat of delaying approval by the U.S.
A few days after the Obama administration threatened to postpone the approval of a planned oil pipeline from Canada to the Gulf of Mexica - thereby pitting unions against each other, but reassuring environmentalists - a company in charging to build the pipeline announced that it was willing to redirect the project so that things could be set into motion again.
TransCanada announced on Monday night that it would move the planned pipeline route from Sandhills' environmentally sensitive area in Nebraska, and is confident that the project will still be approved.
The company announced this decision at a press conference at the seat of the Nebraska government.
TransCanada's successor, Alex Pourbax, argues that the company is confident that in the end the construction of the pipeline will be approved even though it will lead through a different route.
The announcement came after the Foreign Ministry announced last week that it was postponing a decision on the project's federal permission, from where it will not conduct studies of possible replacement routes through which it would be possible not to interfere with the Sandhills area in Nebrasca and into the aquitual layer of Ogallala, a sprawling underground water reservoir.
"This is really a move forward," Republican Deputy Lee Terry of Nebraska told Fox News.
A new environmental opinion commissioned by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs would probably postpone any decision until 2013, i.e. a year after next year's elections, but the authorities refuse to play any role as a politician in the decision.
In a written statement last week, Obama described the decision as a request from the State Department and expressed his support for it.
The 1,700-mile-long pipeline will connect Canada and the Gulf of Mexico.
From the province of Alberta to refineries in Texas, 700 000 barrels of oil are flowned every day.
In this way it will be necessary to intersect 6 states.
Environmentalists absolutely hate this project, but it's loved by work crews who estimate that 20,000 new jobs will emerge while working on the pipeline.
Legislators in Nebraska, however, opposed the plan and considered introducing laws that would force the company not to intervene in the Sand Hills region and into Ogalla's aquatic layer, the main source of drinking water and irrigation.
An existing study on the environmental impact commissioned by the State Department found that the project would have only a minor negative environmental impact, but a source from the energy industry said that an inspector-general from the ministry ordered a new review of the entire process of the original study and focused mainly on two issues.
Oncefully, it was whether the lobbyist hired by TransCanada, Paul Elliott, who was an adviser to Hillary Clinton in 2008, posed a conflict of interest on the agenda.
Next, whether the firm that was hired to conduct the original study wasn't chosen inappropriately because it had ties to TransCanada.
Patti LaBelle is accused of throwing upheave in fear because of her toddler
The New York family claims singer Patti LaBelle burst furiously into the lobby of their Manhattan home and terrified their daughter so much that she threw upheare in fear.
"Lady Marmalade's hit singer's output" has frightened eighteen-month-old Genevieve Monk so much that she has suffered "personality changes, sleep disorder" and "increased fear of strangers," her family claims in a lawsuit filed with the Manhattan Supreme Court.
On November 10 last year, stage technician Kevin Monk, his wife Roseanna, a kindergarten teacher, and Genevieve were preparing for a family trip.
When Kevin Monk put the car in, Roseanna Monk meshed with her luggage down into the vestibule of their house in Riverside Boulevard and carried Genevieve with her.
Roseanna Monk said she stopped at the door for a moment and put the baby down for a moment.
"Someone appeared behind my back and said, 'Do you know what your daughter's doing?' I said "yes" and I left to pick her up"
LaBelle rebuked the pregnant woman irritably.
"In an aggressive tone, she told me, "You shouldn't put your daughter at the door,"" said Roseanna Monk.
"I told her, "I don't care about your opinion."
She became furious and began to speak vulgarly - she shouted loudly the vulgar expressions...
She had a bottle of water with her, and she started splashing water from it.
He claims Genevieve had a hysterical fit.
"I said to the woman, "Look what you did to my daughter,"" the child's mother said.
LaBelle lunged at her mother - who still had Genevieve in her arms - and had to stop her until her entourage, who subsequently dragged her into the waiting car, was terminated by Roseanna Monkova.
The daughter, meanwhile, was screaming so much that she vomited, she added.
Later, the family sent a letter demanding an apology from LaBelle, but received no answers.
LaBelle's lawyer and spokesperson are not responding to our phone calls to comment on the case.
The lawsuit demands unspecified compensation, but the family's attorney, Sam Davis, said he took up the case free of charge and that the family plans to donate all the spent money to a charity dedicated to children with cancer.
"The purpose is to force Patti LaBelle to take responsibility for her actions," Davis said.
“Such behaviour is completely unacceptable, especially if there is an eighteen-month-old child directed against a school teacher holding an eighteen-month-old.”
Climate Adversity Hampered the Economy
During the first year of Mario López Valdez's rule, unemployment and unreliability rose in Sinaloa, and foreign investment declined, and so did the economy
The year 2011 will go down in the economic history of Sinaloa. This is the first year of Mario López Valdez’s rule, which calls itself the rule of change. This government has brought great expectations in terms of economic progress, however, due to adverse climatic conditions at the beginning of the year, there has been no growth, and there has even been a decline in the main indicators of economic development.
Low February temperatures caused millions of losses in the field of agriculture, limited the possibilities of growth of the state economy, caused a reduction in economic activity by 3.6% in the first semester of this year. In the field of agriculture there was a historical decrease of 31.16%, which disrupted the dynamics of other economic sectors.
According to a report on the Damages issued by the Mexican Council for Sustainable Rural Development, the damage was recorded on 582 835 hectares of cultivated land, of which 78.03% were completely destroyed, and on the remaining 21.07% partial damage was found. The main destroyed crops include vegetables, maize and peas.
The decrease in economic activity until November caused a constant increase in unemployment in the state. According to the National Institute for Statistics and Geography, the unemployment rate in the state saw an increased increase during the first three quarters. From January to March, 55 053 inhabitants of the state of Sinaloa were unemployed, which represents an unemployment rate of 4.53% of the economically active population. In the second quarter, unemployment increased to 5.28%, and between July and August, the average unemployment increased further.
Less liquidity
According to Gerard López Cervantes, director of the Faculty of Economic and Social Sciences of the UAS, the increase in unemployment can be explained indirectly, based on steps that have been taken at public policy level.
"We are witnessing rising unemployment, which is related to a variety of factors. These include, among other things, the loss of liquidity in the market due to the limitation of resources due to legislation passed by Congress in connection with the fight against money laundering. It is not easy for people who illegally seize dollars, it is not easy for them to earn them, invest them or use them for something," he emphasized.
Stopping migration
López Cervantes said the demographic factor also contributed to the rise in unemployment.
"The number of people who go abroad to work, to the United States, has decreased, both for reasons of difficult entry and also because, even if these people get abroad, it is difficult for them to get a job. Therefore, people stay and demand work from our state, and so unemployment is rising," he said.
Unreliability
The analysis of the labor market in Sinaloa also deteriorates due to increasing unreliability, which prevails in working conditions. According to the National Institute for Statistics and Democracy, 60.38% of employed residents in the state work without levying benefits to the Social Security Administration.
Other features of the labour market, such as low salaries, are added to unemployment and unreliability. In this administrative territory, the lowest salaries from all over the country, according to the registries of the Mexican Institute of Social Security and the Labour and Social Planning Secretariat.
The average monthly income of workers in Sinaloa was 5 352 pesets until September, well below the national average, which varies at 7 375 pesets.
Reduction of foreign direct investment
Together with the decline in economic activity and the increase in unemployment, there was also a significant decrease in foreign direct investment in the last 10 years in 2011. According to the Ministry of Finance between January and June, foreign direct investment amounted to 630 697 dollars, which represents a decrease of 87.74% compared to the amount of 5 143 312 dollars for the same period in the previous year.
On the other hand, the Ministry for the Economic Development of the government of the state lists investments of 17 280 million pesets per year. These investments were directed primarily to companies in the field of services.
Debt
In the context of the scenario of a decline in the economy, the government of this state applied in July to the State Congress to approve a loan of 3 billion peso. It would be intended to invest in infrastructure projects in 18 urban areas in order to revive the economy. The local Congress approved a loan of 2,600 million pesos. By November, there were no profits from debt, in any sector of the state economy.
Reaction
According to representatives of the business sector, the first year of Mario López Vald�és's government highlighted the government's lack of governing capabilities.
Miguel Loredo López, chairman of the National Chamber of Transformational Industries, declared that in 2011 there was a lack of reconciling the will of individual sectors, including the government of the state itself.
"The development of the economy in Sinaloa was really slight during this year. We need to work intensively to get back the ground we have lost this year. In 2012 we have to redouble our efforts, there has been a lack of harmonization of the efforts of all of us and, in particular, the proper and synchronized functioning of this new government. Hopefully next year will be much better," he said.
Slowness of the economy
Loredo López said events like frosts at the beginning of the year halted the state economy and curbed its development and growth.
"Weak economic movement was caused by a number of events, including frosts. One of the main consequences was the slowdown in the economy, which did not recover quickly enough so that it could restore dynamism, which would make it possible to achieve an acceptable level of movement of processes, products and services. That is why we found ourselves well below the expected forecasts at the end of the year," he said.
Redefining the mission
According to Teodor Salazar Uriarte, president of the Employer Confederation of the Republic of Mexico in Sinaloa, it is necessary to push the state towards more dynamic productive activities.
"Generally speaking, we observe that it is being worked. We believe that we need to prepare a long-term plan in which the goals and economic direction of the state would be redefined, because it is important to mobilize all the potential of material and human resources that Sinaloa has," he said.
The work that is in front of us
President Coparmex stressed the importance of establishing a vision of the future that would better guide the government's efforts.
"In general, I would say that the government has tried to achieve good results in different areas, there have been some advances in economic development, but it is important to create a long-term plan," he said.
He stated that the current administration has showned advances when it comes to public policy leadership.
"There are elements that make it possible to claim that the government is trying to be friendly. I can say that there is work done here, and I believe that the government is aware that there is also a lot of work ahead of us," he stated.
Condemned for 8 kg of marijuana
Navolato was sentenced to five years in prison and a 100-day fine for possessing eight pounds of marijuana.
The Prosecutor General's Office of the Republic informed that the convicted dealer is Omar Alexis Valenzuela Sandoval. He was convicted for the crime of endangering health, specifically for possessing marijuana for commercial purposes.
According to AP/SIN/CLN/687/2010/M-I, Valenzuela Sandoval was detained by members of the Mexican army on 7 June 2010 during an errand in Cipriano Valdez street, across from station 80 in El Castillo, Navolato.
The dealer was driving in a white-colored pick-up van. There was a black bag in the car and a small backpack in the passenger seat. Troops found 8 kilograms of 23 grams of marijuana.
For these reasons, the secured man has remained available to the Judges of the other department and the proceedings remain internal within the Centre for the Implementation of the Legal Impacts of Offences.
He owes contributions to projects
Social projects, which are part of programs such as Hábitat, Saving public places and 3x1 for migrants, face the danger that they will not be brought to an end or that they will be very late due to the lack of economic resources, Miguel Ángel López Miranda said.
The director of urban social development said that if construction work has gone forward until now, there is now a risk that the government of the state will not be able to secure appropriate supplies, which require funds of more than 11 million peso for three programs.
He stated that the total investment in these three programs is almost 50 million peso. The state accounts for 25%. By this time, the funds for the work were supplied only by the Federation and the city, therefore it is possible that there will be a delay in the implementation of the projects.
"They are supposed to secure almost 12 million, and even though the year is coming to an end, we didn't get anything at all. Even though we were supposed to get part of it in July," he said. "In order not to waste time, we started the projects and they continue very successfully, but we lack money".
He stated that last year, under the administration of Jesús Aguilar Padillo, the state did not meet the relevant supplies, and therefore at the beginning of the new period the city had to apply for a loan in order to pay more than 7 million, which was not given to them by the state administration.
"The same thing happened to us already in the previous six-year period, because the state did not have the economic capacity to be able to participate and the programs went on. In order to get to the mentioned programs this year, we had to raise funds that would cover what the state did not complete," he explained.
In spite of that there is a promise on the part of state representatives regarding the transfer of these sources, he said a request had been sent to Armanda Villarreal, secretary for administration and finance, to hasten the proceedings and the corresponding percentage to arrive as soon as possible.
"They were supposed to give us 12 million, and although the year is coming to an end, we haven't even received a peso yet."
The court approves the "end of analogous broadcasting" in Mexico in 2015
Replacing analogous digital signals is one of the most important changes the world has experienced in recent years.
The Supreme State Court (SCJN) dismissed a constitutional lawsuit filed by the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies against a presidential decree that wants to hasten the so-called "shutdown of analogous signals" and implement it in 2015 instead of 2012. He noted that there were not the necessary eight votes to invalidate it.
A minority of four ministers, including the court's president, Juan Silva Meza, believed that President Felipe Calderón had not exceeded his powers when he issued a decree listing the individual steps that would lead to the transition to terrestrial digital television.
With this result, an action filed by the Senate was dismissed because a qualified majority of eight votes had not been reached necessary to enable the court to comment on the constitutionality or unconstitutionality of the aforementioned Decree, which thus remains valid.
The draft judgment drawn up by Minister Olga Sánchez Cordero presents the possibility of declaring the decree unconstitutional, because the regulation of all issues related to radio and television is a matter that is legally reserved to the Federal Telecommunications Commission (Cofetel). This violates the principles of hierarchical subordination and exception to the law.
Only Zaldívar, Jorge Mario Pardo, Margarita Luna and Sánchez Cordero themselves voted for the project.
With this argument, both the Senate and the Chamber of Deputies supported a constitutional dispute in an effort to combat the decree. Minister Sánchez Cordero accepted the requirements and suspended the application of the decree and all the actions described in it, leading to the transition to digitization.
The meeting of ministers also decided to reject the action brought by the Chamber of Deputies, without entering into a discussion on the proposal, which Minister Sánchez Cordero is also in charge.
In his speech, the Prime Minister stated that he did not agree with the arguments of the proposal, in the sense that the federal executive cannot structure through the general standards measures to prepare the public administration.
“I don’t think that even if she did, it would affect the regulator’s autonomy,” he said, noting that public policy planning in strategic areas such as the telecommunications sector belongs to a federal executive that has a constitutional mandate to determine the direction of the state.
Compensation of analogous signals by digital is one of the biggest changes experienced by the world in recent years. It is related to better use of radioelectric spectrum, better signal quality and multiple channels.
He believes that delaying change would mean delaying the benefits of digitalization, which requires decisions on the public interest, because digitization has not only a technical dimension, but "also touches on economic, social, political and national security issues".
As he pointed out, these issues "require an operation of the entire established system, in accordance with the principle of the economic direction of the state. It includes a variety of competences entrusted by the Constitution to the hands of the government of the state".
He recalled that a number of countries around the world had already completed the transition to a digital system and did so through their governments, even though they had highly specialised technical authorities at their disposal.
He stated that it is not possible for the government, by issuing a dissenting decree that to some extent interferes with the powers of Congress, to introduce compliance with laws prepared by legislative powers into the administration.
In Michoacán, they analyze the possibility of counting "voice by voice"
According to data released by PREP, the difference between first and second place in the governor's election is less than 27 percent. So far, 879 out of 6074 electoral districts have not been counted.
The possibility of counting "vote by vote" appeared in this year's elections in Michoacán, where, in accordance with the data released by the Programme of Preliminary Election Results (PREP), the difference between the first and second places in the governor's election is less than 27 percent, and it has not yet been counted by votes of 879 out of a total of 6074 electoral districts.
In this context, the president of the General Council of the Electoral Institute in Michoacán (IEM), María de los Ángeles Llanderal Zaragoza, recalled that a few days ago, on November 9, the electoral body approved a number of measures that should harmonize local norms with federal electoral reform.
As will be recalled, Michoacán did not officially confirm his political constitution or his State electoral code or the Electoral Justice Act. The State Congress approved the reforms shortly before the legal deadline for their entry into force during this term should have expired, and Governor Leonel Godoy Rangel omitted their publication within the stipulated deadline in the Official State Gazette Act, saying that he had any objections against them.
On November 9, the Supreme State Court (SCJN) resolved a constitutional dispute initiated by local legislative power and declared the voice of the head of state "invalidable and illegal" about constitutional reforms because it has no right to "or approve or veto" them.
When asked the possibility of counting "voice by vote" María de los Ángeles Llanderal replied that, thanks to the approval of appropriate measures by the IEM General Council, it would be possible for both partial and total census to be carried out by the local electoral authority.
In this way, the partial counting of votes, one or more electoral packages, can be carried out under the following conditions: the election results do not agree; there is no record of vote counting and calculation in the relevant field, or it is not available by the chairman; or if there have been errors and obvious violations of election results.
In addition, the overall recalculation of votes in all ballot boxes may be made if there is an indication suggesting that the difference between a potential election winner and a candidate in second place is less than or equal to one percentage point; whenever an explicit request by a representative of the party and/or coalition that proposed a candidate in second place appears at the beginning of the session.
It will also be possible to make an overall recalculation if, after a proper addition, it is found that the difference between a probable winner and a candidate in second place is equal or less than one percentage point, and also whenever there is an explicit request from a political party whose candidate finished in second place. In this case, the electoral packages for which a partial census has been eliminated will be eliminated.
It should be mentioned that in such a situation there is an election in Morelia, where according to PREP there is a difference between the first and second places of less than 0.16%, even though 129 out of a total of 923 electoral districts have not yet been counted.
His last will
His ashes will be scattered in the Zapalinamé Mountains, the family confirmed.
The remains of pilot Felipe Bacio Cortés were transported on Sunday night to Saltillo, his hometown. There will be a ceremonial ceremony and a mortifical mass on Monday.
According to the family, the lieutenant colonel's last wish that his ashes be scattered in the Zapaliname Mountains.
After receiving the highest honours along with the other persons who died in the crash, Bacio Cortése's family returned to Coahuila's capital aboard the Mexican Air Force plane. The family and closest friends attended the ceremony at Campo Marte.
At noon the lieutenant's ashes arrived at a chapel located on Nazario Ortiz Garza, where family and friends also came.
At 5:30 p.m
"He wanted to return to Saltillo, and his wish was that his ashes be scattered in the Zapalinamé Mountains. That's how he always told his family and friends," Sandra Bacio Cortés, the pilot's sister, said in a statement.
According to her, the lieutenant colonel fulfilled all his wishes, he was at the peak of his life, "he was a wonderful man, an extraordinary father, a son, a loving brother living for his family."
Felipe Bacio Cortés left Saltillo very young and joined Colegio del Aire in Zapopán, Jalisco.
Some didn't believe it because they said he was even driving a truck, but he always followed his dream of becoming a pilot.
His nephew Jorge Alberto Dávila Bacio remembers him as a very good person, "he barely came to Saltillo, the whole family wanted to see him, as he did when he brought Martha Sahagún to Saltillo."
The young man even remembers that when he was seven years old, he went to Zapopán at the graduation of his uncle, which is why he is convinced that he died in what he liked best in his life: plane control.
They gave him one last goodbye
With Felipe Bacio Cortés, a lieutenant of the Mexican Air Forces, we parted ways with all the honors that Governor Jorge Torres López came to give him, the citizens and government of the state, the Mexican army through the Sixth Military Zone, the Mexican Air Force itself, the leaders and students of the Technical Secondary School in Saltillo, where he studied in 1985 and where he excelled in both his studies.
Bacio Cortés died in the past few days after the helicopter he was driving. On board was also the secretary of the government, José Francisco Blake Mora, and six others, all officials of that state.
At 18:05, the remains of Saltillel native Bacio Cortés (cremated) arrived to be handed over through the funeral service to the Mexican air forces under the guidance of Major Jaime Martinez, where, meanwhile, the family, his daughter Amanda and wife Cristina, mother, siblings and other relatives, students and teachers gathered.
Three abandoned dead bodies were found on Cadereyta NL
A few hours before the discovery, military officials presented to the media 11 people who had been detained in the area for crimes related to organized crime.
The bodies of the three men murdered by gunfighting into various parts of their bodies and head were fouled on an abandoned farmhouse of Cadereyta Jiménez, Nuevo León.
The discovery occurred on a 1.5-kilometre road from Cadereyta to Santiago, at the level of an area known as El Castillo.
The people who moved in these places noticed that a cottage with a beige-coffee fence had an open door from time to time.
When they looked inside, from a distance of approximately three meters, they noticed the corpses of three people, so they immediately alerted the appropriate authorities.
One of the people was wearing a sports T-shirt of yellow color and dark cotton pants and there was no shoes. Next to him was a man also lying in a sports T-shirt with white stripes.
A few meters away was the body of another person dressed in a red sports T-shirt and blue cotton pants.
All three were facing the ground.
According to data obtained in places, all three people were probably murdered in another place because bullets were not found.
There were messages addressed to one group of organized crime by the other, as well as a series of bullet marks, both on the building and on the door.
11 suspects arrested in Cadereyta
The Secretary of the National Defence introduced 11 people, including juveniles, who were members of a criminal group that operated mainly in the Cadereyta area and which is associated with kidnappings, murders, persecution and illegal burial.
According to the published information during the first attempt on November 11 in the Los Alveros colony, 8 people were detained and managed to free one detainee.
Three properties and a mobile phone have been secured. They have confirmed that they are members of a criminal group called "Los Zetas."
The next raid took place on November 12 at 6 a.m. The military personnel were at a trail in the Rancho Viejo area in Cadereyta when they noticed a car without a license plate and two people in it.
After stopping the car, they conducted a search and discovered one rifle, a casing and 18 bullets.
Then, based on these testimonies, one of the accomplices in the La Frauda area was arrested around 14 o’clock on 13 November. When he saw the soldiers, he tried to escape.
They have been charged with illegal burials in the municipalities of General Terán, China and Cadereyta.
A new Italian government will be named on Wednesday
Appointed Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet with the Italian president tomorrow to present him with a new government facing a crisis that is holding the country above the abyss of an economic catastrophe.
Appointed Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet with the Italian president tomorrow to present him with a new government facing a crisis that is holding the country above the abyss of an economic catastrophe that has put the entire eurozone in danger.
The presidential palace issued a report announcing that Monti, appointed on Sunday, will meet with President Giorgio Napolitano on Wednesday to confirm to him that he can form a government.
It is expected to be presented by a government composed mostly of technocrats, although it is not yet clear when the new government will take power.
Monti said he would present to the president the results of his conversations, which he conducted with political parties in an effort to form a government.
"I want to confirm that I have absolute confidence in the ability of our country to overcome this difficult phase," the former European commissioner told reporters.
Monti did not explicitly say he would be able to form a government, but the tone of his comments suggested that obstacles were overcome.
"The framework is now well marked," he added.
Monti has completed the process of forming a government in less than three days, i.e. in a much shorter time than usual. In the meantime, Italy is trying to combat the political and financial crisis, due to which the amount of debt has climbed to an unsustainable level.
The new administration, led by former European Commissioner Monti, must approve a tough austerity package demanded by European leaders. Only in this way will it be possible to restore Italy's hacked credibility.
There's a lot of pressure on Monti to hurry. Due to market instability, interest on Italian BTP bonds with a 10-year maturity exceeded the 7-percentage threshold, the level at which Greece and Ireland had to apply for rescue.
Emma Marcegaglia, president of the Italian employers' organisation Confindustria, told reporters after a meeting with Monti: "We have saided that we will support this government very much."
We believe that this government is the last chance to get Italy out of this pressing situation.
Monti's hopes of success have multiplied significantly thanks to PDL support from Silvio Berlusconi, who has been forced to resign on Saturday.
"We believe Professor Monti's efforts are destined for good results," Angelina Alfano, secretary of the center-right party, told reporters.
The support of the PDL, Italy's largest party, is significant, because until now, a number of its members have opposed a government with a technocratic majority compiled by Monti.
Support for the Parliament
Monti's new government must have strong support from parliament in order to push for unpopular austerity reforms.
Any obstacles or delays in a given effort could provoke a new and devastating attack on financial markets.
Italy's association of foreign banks has added to the coercion, warning that Monti's failure could be a disaster.
Monti began talks with political parties, unions and business groups, youth and women's organizations on Monday.
The round of talks will end Tuesday night.
Monti was appointed on Sunday by President Napolitan, which allowed a very quick advance in response to the crisis.
After a brief decline at the end of last week, when Berlusconi would resign, Italian bond interest has returned to the critical limit, respondinging to the uncertainty of whether the new prime minister will reap success.
Saving Italy, whose public debt is 1.8 trillion euros, would go beyond the capabilities of the eurozone's current financial reserves.
Monti declared that his government should stay in power until the next election, which is scheduled for 2013, despite general forecasts claiming that politicians will only give him time to carry out reforms, and then call an early election.
Monti said he would like to incorporate politicians into his cabinet, but large parties insist that this government must only be composed of experts to lead Italy out of the difficulties that the country got into because of financial pressure.
Political sources said mutual distrust and disagreement between the parties complicate an attempt to incorporate political figures into the government.
Spaniards take part in international matches more often than Costa Ricans
Of Spain's 22 footballers, ten will cross the mark of 50 international matches.
On Costaria's side, only one of the 22 summoned players has reached the number of 50 matches.
Huge contrast on Costa Rica's side if we compare the 22 players of both countries who met today for a friendly match at 15:05 at the San Jose National Stadium.
While the world and European champions, the number one-month rating of FIFA, have a wide range of Class A selection matches, Costa Rica has only defender Michael Uman, who was the only one to play 50 international matches.
He reached that number last Friday during a match against Panama (0-2) at Rommel Fernández Stadium.
Spanish footballers, who play in the Stars' Match and in the mighty clubs of the English Premier League, have maintained an intense activity for over a year at league championships and domestic cups and high-level matches within the European Cups as Champions League.
We also have to add the experience of the national Spanish team in complicated knockout matches to the European and world championships.
On the other hand, Costa Rican players have very limited opportunities for foreign matches in the area. For example, there is Concacaf, which is considered the lowest level within FIFA.
We can see that the biggest number of foreign matches from the Spanish national team has the goalkeeper Íker Casillas, who reached the former goalkeeper Andoni Zubizarreta with 126 international matches last Saturday before England (losing 0-1 in London).
Casillas will continue against Costa Rica today and will get a new record for Spain - 127 international matches.
Xavi Hernández, Barcelona's key player, has 106 games in his account.
Casillas and Xavi were even officially announced last week by UEFA along with other Spanish players, who have had a hundred matches in their account. Among them the aforementioned Zubizarreta with 126 and central forward Schalke, Raúl González, with 102.
Carles Puyol also approached a hundred with 97 matches.
They are followed by Xabi Alonso with 91; Fernando Torres with 90; David Villa and Sergio Ramos, both with 81; Andrés Iniesta with 62; Cesc Fábregas with 61; and David Silva with 53.
On Costarica's side and near Michael Uman, left wing Júnior Díaz moves with 48 tackles and goalie Bryan Ruiz with £45.
Further, only 5 surpassed the 30-match mark: Keilor Navas with 31, Randall Azofeifa with 32, Michael Barrantes with 31, Carlos Hernández with 36 and José Luis López with 35.
The smallest number of matches.
Gerard Piqué (37), Sergio Busquets (36), Santiago Santi Cazorla (32), Álvaro Arbeloa (31) and Raúl Albiol (31).
On the national side with less than 30 games stands Roy Miller with 26, Winston Parks with 26, Gabriel Badilla with 25 and Roy Myrie with 23.
The rest didn't exceed 20.
While Nacho Moreal has only four games and Jordi Alba two, in Costa Rica Víctor Bolívar has only one and Ólman Vargas and Edder Nelson are still waiting for their first match.
Spain's top scorer is David Villa with 50 goals, followed by Fernando Torres with 27.
On the Costarian side, Bryan Ruiz has the most hits, nine, two more than Carlos Hernández and three more than Roy Myrie and Parks.
The quintet included the country in its tour on the occasion of the celebration of twenty years of existence
"The road to Costa Rica is a dream that becomes a reality"
In an exclusive interview with Viva, bassist Jeff Ament of Pearl Jam said the group is ready to offer one of the best concerts at the National Stadium next Sunday.
We've been waiting for more than 20 years, but the group itself assures that for them the opportunity to play on Costa Rica is a dream come true.
That's what the bassist and founding member Jeff Ament, who spoke exclusively with Viva last week from Brazil.
The group is located on the South American continent as part of a tour on the occasion of the celebration of twenty years of existence, and on Sunday 20 will be at a unique stop in Central America.
He will perform from 19:30 at the National Stadium, along with the angelic group The X and national musicians from Las Robertas.
This is an excerpt from the interview, the entire transcript of which can be found on La Nación's website on the Internet.
For your Costa Rican supporters, it’s a dream come true. Pearl Jam will play in Costa Rica. Do you realize what your fans are experiencing?
The journey to Costaria is also our dream, which becomes a reality.
In South and Central America, we are scarcely the second time and the experience so far has been great.
I’ve only been to Costaria once, on vacation. It’s a wonderful country and I believe we’ll be able to stay here for a few days.
So we're very happy to be able to bring your music.
I ask, because they literally adore the group here, fans experience these days of preparation very intensely.
What do you say to your most rocky supporters?
Thank you for standing with us for so many years.
It took us almost 21 years to get to Costa Rica. Thank you following for so long and we are sorry that it took us so long.
Some people are already talking about the concert as the biggest show of the year.
Is it putting more pressure on you?
Some of the biggest shows we've shown over the last 21 years arose within a few weeks.
We've put on some great shows in Sao Paulo and we're hoping we've put ourselves in shape for a concert in Costa Rica.
We are really looking forward to this concert.
We love this country, we like to surf, we like the jungle. For both activities, Costa Rica is the best country in the world.
How did you get to know Costa Rica and its waves suitable for surfing?
We have a lot of surfer friends and I have a good friend who lives in Tamarindo. The waves here are incredible.
My friends go surfing Costarica or Nicaragua, or Mexico. These are the best speaking spots. And that’s how we learned about Costa Rica, through people who love surfing.
If we look at your tour, you've prepared a huge show with many songs.
What have you planned for your presentation in Costa Rica?
Since we've never played here, we don't have any restrictions on what we can't do.
We will try to perform the best possible show with a variety of songs. We will try to play a few songs from each album and introduce our fans to a good and diverse repertoire.
You're never repeating the repertoire.
Why?
I think it's partly because we have nine albums and we can play more than 120 songs and everyone likes different songs, that's why it's hard to choose what songs we're going to play every night.
When we have two or three songs in the repertoire that we haven’t played in a long time, we focus on them and we’re also surprised, because we remember why we liked them so much and that we may have forgotten about it.
That's how the show is more interesting for us, and that's why I think it's more interesting for people.
This tour celebrates 20 years of artistic career.
If you look back, is it hard to believe everything you've done?
Yes, I'm sure.
First, I don't think any of us could have imagined that we'd be in one big rock band and that we'd stay in it for more than 20 years.
We are aware of how much we have been given and we are touched that we can continue to play together.
We love each other and we love making music.
We're lucky enough to take a look at the places we've always longed.
We couldn't have done better.
What did it mean to you that you were an important part of the creation of the grunge genre?
It has always been an honor for us to be part of this group, in which giants are represented.
The people from Soundgarden, Mudhoney and Alice in Chains are our friends.
It's surprising when a movement arises from a group of friends, it doesn't happen very often.
From time to time, a group is formed in the city, but it is much less common for five to ten groups to emerge from one city at once.
We are proud of it and we are honoured to be part of the group from Seattle.
Sometimes we still play with some of them.
Last year we went to the festival with Alice in Chains and we were in Canada with Mudhoney, we saw Soundgarden on tour. We’re lucky that they’re all our friends and it’s an honor that we can be part of this movement that originated in Seattle.
It's also very unusual for a group like yours to stay together and still have the same members after all these years.
Yes, the four of us have been together for 21 years, and Matt (Cameron) joined us 13 years ago, which in itself is a time much longer than the usual duration of the group.
I think, first of all, we're all lucky that we're still in this world, and secondly, we're always interested in each other.
We are all close friends, and thanks to that, we have managed to open up musical paths that leave everyone with sufficient freedom for their own experimentation.
We trust each other, we appreciate each other's music and I don't think it could be better.
Your relationship to the word fame wasn’t easy. However, thanks to your success, you can do whatever you like.
How do you find balance?
As we grew, it gradually became easier to find balance.
We're trying not to go much on tour because it's very easy to fall into this trap of crowds and people who love our music.
When you realize that people have come because of music, you will remind yourself that you have to respect the music and what you have given it. You must not get carried away by the fact that you are famous, rock stars. Neither of us has ever really cared about this.
How do you remember another Mother Love Bone project?
I think we wrote great songs and in the beginning it was something spectacular.
We existed as a band for three years and never released an album until after Andy (Andrew Woods).
It was very frustrating because it took so long. The most important thing is that even today we are friends with Bruce (Fairweather) and Greg (Gilmore), who started in the band with us. When I listen to a song or look at the photos, I remember Andy, how unbelievable I laughed with him. It was always a joy to work with him, to laugh, to talk about music
She was an unbelievable person, and we're missing a lot.
The national band Las Robertas will be tasked with starting a show in Costa Rica.
Is it true that you know their work, especially Eddie Vedder, and that's why you've chosed them as foremasters?
Yes, the production sent us videos with several designs and we liked this group the most. That's why we're curious what Las Robertas will offer live and have to be very entertaining.
Why do you always change the words Yellow Ledbetter live?
That's a question for Ed (Eddie Vedder), so I don't know.
He sings it, and I think he's adding the first thing that comes to mind.
Who influences you as a bassist?
Lots of people.
Geezer Butler, John Entwistle, C. J. Ramone, John Doe, Paul McCartney, Chuck Dukowski.
There's so many of them that I could talk all night about bassists I like.
How did Pearl Jam manage to maintain grunge, even though it's no longer in vogue?
I think we've always always felt slightly separated from the word grunge.
When I say grunge, I imagine bands I’ve referred to as Mudhoney, Soundgarden, Nirvana, and all the others that originated at the same time. And if music is valid like this today, it’s something great, because it’s music that’s worth listening to and bands like Mudhoney play better music today than ever before.
In the beginning, your music had a slightly gloomy character, and now it's more positive.
What's behind this change?
That's interesting, because I feel like there's a few songs in the new album that's lighter, but there are also songs like The End or Juste Breathe, which are considered very grim, although they may sound positive.
For both of them, when Ed played them, I cried, and they're deeply dark songs.
I think that when you grow up and there's some trauma in your life, you're going to lose someone's family or a friend, these things are going to affect your art and music, and that's what these two songs represent to me.
I also think that you can be a happy person and still make grim music.
Peru:
Fujimori was hospitalized again for medical tests
Former President Alberto Fujimori was hospitalized at a public hospital on Tuesday, where he will be given tests because of the muscle loss he suffers in the area of his lower limbs, his attending physician reported.
72-year-old Fujimori, who is serving a 25-year prison sentence for human rights violations committed by his government (1990 - 2000), suffers from several diseases, including tongue cancer, because of which he has been operated on four times and is already in control by doctors.
Alejandro Aguinaga, who is his personal physician, told Canal N television that Fujimori will be undergoing tests within three days at the National Institute of Neoplastic Disease, where they should find out why it hurts him and why he loses forces in his lower limbs.
Aguinaga said that, in his view, Fujimori could receive a pardon for humanitarian reasons, however, the former president himself does not wish for pardon because he feels innocent.
It was operated on four times due to tongue cancer, lost 18 kilograms, and other ailments such as severe erosive gastritis, pancreatic cyst, kidney stones, high pressure and circulatory problems in the legs were added to these problems, the doctor listed.
Fujimori has been serving his sentence since September 2007 at the National Directorate of Special Police Operations.
In April 2009, he was sentenced to 25 years in prison for the deaths of 25 people killed by a murderous squadron of troops operating illegally in the early years of his rule.
Mujica on a visit to Mexico to strengthen political ties
An official delegation led by the Uruguayan President, José Mujica, accompanied by several ministers and entrepreneurs, will begin a visit to Mexico on Tuesday. It aims to achieve an expansion of trade between the two peoples and touch on the political themes of the region.
Mujica will meet with her Mexican counterpart, Felipe Calderón on Wednesday. This is the most important meeting of a wide-ranging visit full of meetings with representatives of politics, commerce and representatives of the Uruguayan community living in Mexico. The program has been published by the Urguayan presidential office.
The meeting of the two presidents in the city of Guadalajara will serve to exchange information on political matters between the two countries," the Urugau presidential office posted on its website.
According to local media, Mujica will try to get Calderón to support Uruguay in a dispute with France when French President Nicolas Sarkozy included the South American country on the list of tax havens at the G20 meeting in the French city of Cannes.
In his speech, Sarkozy threatened that countries that would continue to remain in the position of tax havens will be excluded from the international community. This statement has prompted a dismissive position by Uruguay and consultation with the ambassador in this European country.
Mexico has been chairing the G20 since November. The G20 is a group made up of the world’s most powerful emerging and developed countries. With the aforementioned Latin American country, an agreement on the exchange of information is to prevent double taxation since January this year.
In recent years, the Uruguayan government has sought to concretize fiscal exchange agreements in order to get off the "blacklist" of the Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), to which there are countries that have not implemented all the required international standards of tax cooperation.
From the commercial point of view, Mexico and Uruguay have since 2004 concluded the Free Trade Agreement (FTA), which has resulted in a strengthening of the exchange of goods between the two countries.
Bearded plumber runs in 3D thanks to "Super Mario 3D Land"
Super Mario has been fighting tirelessly for more than 25 years to save Princess Peach from the clutches of villain Bowser, but has never appeared in 3D before: in "Super Mario 3D Land" he'll fight for the girl's love with stereoscopic technology.
The title, coming this month to all stores around the world, has already been crafted from the start to take advantage of all the characteristics offered by the Nintendo 3DS portable console, which allows you to play three-dimensional games without having to use glasses.
"Mario is a character who has to say how stereoscopies should work without glasses," said a press worker specializing in Nintendo in Spain, Omar Álvarez, during a video game presentation in Madrid.
Álvarez stated that "Super Mario 3D Land" is the "first Nintendo video game created specifically for this carrier" because up to this day successful images like "Star Fox 64 3D" or "Zelda: Ocarine of time 3D" were adaptations of pre-existing titles.
A Nintendo spokesperson said that in addition to the fact that the image is 3D, "Super Mario 3D Land" is an "affordable" video game that allows you to enjoy a fast and smooth game.
Álvarez confirmed that "Super Mario 3D Land" contains two games in one: the first part is "simple", the task of which is to attract less skilled users.
Once you overcome this level, you can embark on the "second round" at the levels of the game, which are more challenging this time.
In addition, users can choose a real time between "aggressive D" or one that only affects the depth of action. However, it will be necessary to play in 3D in order to overcome all sorts of challenges and "not succumb to optical deceptions".
This new addition of Super Maria in 3D also anticipates the return of the character to the portable console, which has not happened since 2005.
In this video game, Nontine plays a note to those who watch the saga and repeat a successful formula from the previous episodes. It consists in that the bearded man’s plumber will have to go through dangerous minuted platforms to free Princess Peach.
Although the player finds news in movements, in the surroundings, in disguises and also among the enemies, the "playful" Luigi will also appear.
Colom approves Portillo's extradition to the United States
President Álvaro Colom announced on Tuesday that he has progressed steps on the extradition of former President Alfons Portillo, who is to be held accountable in the United States for the purpose of laundering 70 million U.S. dollars.
This is the end of a trial aimed aimed for Guatemala to respond to a request from a northern country calling for Portillo's extradition so it can be judged.
At the press conference, Colom confirmed that his decision was consistent with the independence of judicial and executive powers and that he kept in mind that the criminal court had already ordered extradition.
He assured that during his mandate he "did not interfere" with court rulings and authorized all extraditions that came to his desk.
In addition to Portillo, Sergio Ruano Marroquín will be extradited for murder and grievous bodily harm, and Edgar Estrada Morales and Víctor Estrada Paredes, both for drug trafficking.
Colom's move marks a step of execution after all judicial and constitutional instances have been exhausted to conduct a trial against a former president who ruled between 2000 and 2004.
In accordance with the decisions of the Fifth Criminal Tribunal and the Constitutional Court (CC), Portillo will be extradited after its legal situation in Guatemala has been resolved and respect for human rights is guaranteed.
Portillo was found not guilty of embezzlement of 120 million at the Ministry of Defence at the time of his presidency. This process was preceded by extradition, but the Public Ministry appealed and returned the case to play.
Trinijove helped 6000 youngers in a state of emergency
The organization was formed to help children without education, so that they do not find themselves on the margins of society.
The balance of the first 25 years of Trinijove’s social support organization is very important: 6,000 young people who were threatened with social exclusion have been given help. The organization has been helping them to overcome difficult situations for 25 years.
In addition to this, it is necessary to add a long hundred jobs that have been acquired for this social group.
Yesterday's anniversary also served to present the balance sheet as part of a ceremonial act, which took place in the former production premises of the Fabra and Coats factories, which was attended by more than 300 people.
Trinijove was founded in Trinitat Vella in 1986 to help young people with low-education, jobless, drug addiction, or where they could fall into social exclusion.
Yesterday's meeting in Sant Andreu was attended by a large number of representatives of organizations and institutions that have cooperated with the association over the past 25 years.
A diverse political representation, at the time of the elections, showed the unified nature of the work carried out by Trinijova.
Along with President Generalitat, Arturo Mases, and his predecessor Jordi Pujol, the ceremonial act was attended by former socialist education adviser Ernest Maragall, and former deputy mayor and current environmental-socialist councillor Ricard Goma.
Josep LluÍs Cleries and La Caix, Jaume Lanaspa, the director-general of social labour in La Caixa.
Mas said that Trinijove originated at a very difficult time and that today's time also offers a number of obstacles, but added that the work that the association has brought to this day "is a good basis."
The president cited Trinijove as an example of how it is possible to combat unfavorable circumstances and how to give courage to people in difficult situations.
“The crisis is nothing new for this organization and for the work it is doing,” he said.
There's been an eviction of a seized house where gigantic parties were held
Intruders have appropriated 11 of the 13 apartments in the building in Balmes, 51
The 12 occupiers left the house yesterday at noon without resisting
All indications are that the nightmare experienced by neighbors from number 51 in Balmes Street is finally over.
Twelve people voluntarily left the building at noon at noon, where they illegally occupied and where they organized gigantic parties that aroused neighbors. They left the building from the court order and without having to intervene with the police.
After the end of the operation, the lock on the door was exchanged to prevent a repeat of the situation.
"From now on, entry will be guarded," the administrator said.
Occupiers, who occupied 11 out of 13 apartments in a given building - the rest are inhabited - took away all the electrical and partly water equipment, in addition, painted the walls and left all sorts of garbage, especially cans of alcoholic beverages.
In the first three apartments, huge parties were held, which lasted up to two days and awakened the other tenants.
Next to the building is a disco, Balmes 51, which has also been damaged.
Its owner, Maria Pantinat, explained to Europa Press that while the building was occupied, sales had fallen by 80%.
The owner of the disco emphasizes that the occupiers of the building gave her unfair competition because they sold drinks for 3 euros, while at the disco, they cost 10.
In addition, the owner of the entertainment company had to respond to lawsuits of neighbors because of the noise. However, the problems were caused not by the visitors of the discotheque, but by the inhabitants of the occupied house, and especially by those who participated in the parties, whose frequency and intensity has increased in recent weeks, according to the angry inhabitants of the neighborhood.
The owner of the disco explained that the problems were so great that they even agreed with the owner of the building and have been forgiving rent for nine months.
City police have also confirmed they have received more complaints from neighbours about the situation over the past few weeks.
Last week, Alberto Fernández Díaz demanded that "the building be evicted immediately, putting an end to the problems of civic coexistence suffered by neighbors and the destruction of the area."
The chairman of the People's Party explained that only one floor had been seized before the summer, but in recent months the occupiers have reached almost all floors of the building and almost all apartments, and there have even been thefts in one of them.
In this regard, he added that "it is necessary to put an end to impunity and to be consistent when it comes to acts against civic responsibility that took place in the building."
Yesterday, city police also vacated the building in Nou Barris.
At 15 o'clock 30 minutes, police made their way into Building 35 on Mont-Ral Street after being summoned by neighbors who saw four people entering the building.
All four were immediately detained.
Park or mountains?
The architects met on the occasion of the proceedings, which entail more than a controversy about royalties
The city's 16-gates tender again opens a debate about the role of Collserola Park in Barcelona.
In the mid-1980s, at the time of the greatest flourishing of the already defunct Corporación Metropolitana de Barcelona (CMB), this self-government printed several pamphlets in support of Collserola Park.
The flyer defined the mountain as a large park of metropolitan Barcelona.
What's more, it was supplemented by a numerical comparison with New York's Central Park.
After CMB disappeared in 1987, the park returned to the minds of many as Barcelona's top border. The Catalan metropolis is one of the few that does not navigate the axis north - south, but up (mountain) - down (sea).
Collserola's qualification as a natural park is going in this direction.
The reference was no longer Central Park, but Yellowstone with wild boars instead of Yogi bear.
Such was the position until September, when the town hall announced a mega competition consisting of 16 auditions for the same number of entrance gates to Collserola.
Of course, the gate records the idea of the park.
It's either this, or someone's trying to put the gate in the field.
So there's only one question: Collserola, park or mountain?
During the competition, both from a purely organizational point of view and based on reflection on the city, this question becomes the central theme of all Barcelona architects.
It's already a topic as wiped as when two members of this old caste of the chosen ones shake hands in a meeting in the elevator.
And it was this feat that provoked one of the rare matches between what three Barcelona architects and leading university professors think.
Miguel Roldán, Daniel Modol and Marta Bayona: the selection process has its justification.
According to Roldan, after the issue of the bay party began, it is "necessary" to discuss the "mountain side", so Ildefons Cerd's speech on green apples has not been brought to an end.
"We have to base ourselves on the principle that Barcelona's largest park is the beach.
Among other things, its great availability.
Collserola is more complex,” declared the architect, for whom the name “gates to Collserola” contains a “lexicological trap”: we must not imagine them “as entrances to the park, but rather as a demarcation of zones where it is possible to operate”.
Modol, like Roldan and Bayona, with experience in the world of urbanism qualifies the design as an "intellectual challenge" offering "some dangers."
Danger arising from the "uncertainty posed today by the project of a public place" after the experience of the 80's, which had "its swan singing" in the Forum area.
The first concern is the very delimitation of the boundaries, where it should be built.
"To define something is to take another step", that is, in principle, to move one step further.
“It is necessary to keep in mind the new urban and planning tools, because the current ones (with reference to the General Metropolitan Plan (PGM)) can cause a real disaster.”
If we focus in more detail on the question, Modol believes that the valley slope of Collserol can be "considered a park", but the Barcelonian part does not, for purely topographical reasons.
Modol also recalled that there is already work done by Patronat de Collserola, which "may not end up in the dump," although he welcomes the fact that the town hall offers his committee to intervene in the project from "the first moment."
Moreover, he understands that the proximity of the mountain and the city is a kind of pass that allows architects to engage in the debate, but "doubts" it should be precisely this technical profile that will guide multi-disciplinary teams ("it's all about the skills" that needs to be created: "Architects should only be tools."
Bayona suggests that the answer to the question that gave the name of this game is complex, becase Collserola’s global vision is lacking in the basis for the selection process: “There have been 16 tenders that deal with all the axis of the mountain – the sea, but there is no longitudinal study to look at the area and understand it as a whole.”
This causes at least him to apply for all 16 competitions.
Otherwise, the project of one gate may be referring to the concept and definition of the architect of the adjacent gate.
In his view, the town hall should guarantee that the zone located immediately above Ronda de Dalt should secure the entire length of the equipment zone, which would ensure a smooth transition between the city and the greens.
The casino pardoned to Jonás Larrazábal, who will be released
After 72 days of imprisonment, the legal envoy Cadino Red pardoned Mayor Monterrey's brother in a trial in which he was prosecuted for supposed blackmail.
Manuel Jonás Larrazábal spent 72 days in prison and will now be released after Casino Red's legal agent has pardoned him before the Second Criminal Court in a given precinct, which discussed a blackmail case in which Mayor Monterrey's brother was indicted.
After the assassination at Casino Royale, Casino Red owner Sergio Gil García posted a series of photos and videos in which Manuel Jonás was supposed to ask him to pay for an economic quota to allow Monterrey City Hall an illegal operation in a betting house.
After a public indictment, the prosecutor's office launched a criminal prosecution of the brother of a panistic mayor. On September 2, he was detained and imprisoned for 30 days.
Then, after spending 4 hours in front of Public Ministry Agent number 3 for property offences, he was transported to Prison 1, located in Barrio Antiguo in the state's capital.
He remained at this location for the next 49 days after a second order on imprisonment was handed down and a Public Ministry agent refuted the possibility of complicity or organized crime.
On October 19, he was transferred to the Cadereyta Criminal Court on suspicion of extorting Casino Red owners with one-and-a-half-million peso. However, he remained for only a few hours in prison with moderate oversight because he was threatened with death.
The leaders allowed him to stay in urban cells in San Nicolás de los Garza during the trial.
On October 25 he was presented with an arrest order issued by the Secretary of the Second Criminal Court, Jorge Yánez, who declared that after almost two months of the investigation, enough evidence was gathered to prove Jonás’ guilt. The evidence includes, for example, disbursing the amount of 1.5 million peso to casino representatives in exchange for a promise that the operation would be allowed.
This Monday, Red's legal envoy, Víctor Aldo García Gómez, appeared with Judge José Luis Pecin and asked for mercy for the mayor's brother of the region. Based on this, he will soon be able to return to freedom.
During the lawful conduct, a representative of the Public Ministry assigned to this court, Ramiro Arias, asked García Gómez whether the damages had been repaired and which of the associates decided to pardon. However, the lawyer made it clear that the name would remain secret.
On the basis of the official file 197/2011, the legal pardon of Jonás Larrazábal is granted. It will not be possible to file any complaint against him in the trial. It is also not possible to claim compensation for damages or punishment.
After re-trial with a legal agent around 15:50, the court ordered the immediate release of Larrazabal Breton.
The court secretary, Jorge Yanéz, arrived at a prison in the town of San Nicolás de los Garza to tell Jonás that he had been granted an official pardon and his case had been postponed to the archives.
He also informed him that the judge had issued an immediate ordinance on his release.
Secretary Dionisio Pérez Jácome presented details about the plane accident in which the governor's secretary, Francisco Blake, and seven others were killed.
The Secretariat for Communications and Transport (SCT) reported that the results from the investigation into the accident, in which the secretary of the government, Francisco Blake Mora, and seven others were killed, showing that the helicopter landed directly on the terrain without the pilot having the chance to attempt a forced landing.
At a press conference, Dionisio Pérez Jácome, secretary for Communications and Transport, said that before taking off the plane, the pilot stated that climatic conditions of the flights allowed.
"The parts that appear indicate that the unit came into contact with the terrain several times, the wings entangled into the branches of the trees 25 meters from where the unit lost the hull."
He also informed that the unit was intended for 19 people, therefore it met all the requirements for the transfer of personnel.
In addition, he said, based on an investigation carried out in the impact area, no parts had been found on the ground.
"During the search, it was found that parts of the aircraft were not located in another location, so the plane hit the mainland as whole.
"The helicopter was designed for 19 people, meeting all the requirements for the transport of people.
The terrain exhibits a slope with a slope of seven to thirty degrees.
The secretary assured that at the moment the most important thing is to get results as quickly as possible. However, the investigation can take several months or up to one year.
Gilberto López Meyer, general manager of airports and auxiliary services, declared that at the moment of impact, the helicopter of the Presidential General Staff was moving at cruise speed and the accident occurred on soft ascending terrain.
“This information supports the hypothesis that it was a normal overflight operation at the time of the accident.”
At a press conference, SCT representative Pérez Jácome listed the six fundamental points of the investigation:
Visual flight:
In accordance with aviation standards, the flight was carried out according to visual flight rules, that is, the crew must see the terrain during the flight.
Meteorological conditions:
AICM reports indicate adequate take-off conditions, according to which the plane progresses towards the border, between DF and State of Mexico were noticeable layers of clouds at low altitudes.
Chosen routes:
It is likely that because of clouds, the crew was looking for a lower area and greater visibility all the way into the Cuernavaca Valley.
The location of the impact.
Coordinates of impact and the last recording of the helicopter from the radar coincide, it is likely that there was no loss of control or disruption of the trajectory of the flight before the impact.
Distraction of aircraft residues in restricted territory:
The information we currently have at our disposal allows us to assume that the impact occurred on a straight trajectory and from the side.
A sketch of the dispersal of the plane's remnants suggests that at the time of hitting the mainland, the helicopter was whole.
There was no fire or explosion.
There was no evidence at the scene of the crash indicating that there had been an explosion or fire.
Recent results of the investigation show that there may have been several contacts with the mainland.
During fieldwork, the remains of the aircraft were found at an altitude of up to 9200 feet above sea level, the terrain slopes, the amount of inclination goes from seven to 30 degrees.
The photos show footage that was probably disintegrated along the route.
The information provides elements that appear to indicate that the helicopter may have come into contact with the ground in several places.
After a careful search in the vicinity of the accident, in a territory that is more extensive than the territory of demarcation, the remains of the helicopter were not found, which reinforces the assumption that the impact occurred in a state when the helicopter was still integrated.
The Mexican Institute of Health Insurance has launched research into a gene that causes obesity
The Institute of Health Insurance conducts research with the aim of discovering a gene responsible for the obesity of children and adolescents. It implements the research with the support of experts from the IPN Institute
The Mexican Institute of Health Insurance (IMSS) announced that it is conducting research to detect a gene causing obesity in children and young people. The research called "Genetics of Obesity in children and juveniles" will make it possible to obtain results related to overweight.
In a report by the head of the Research Medical Unit in the field of biochemistry from the Specialized Hospital of the 21st-century National Medical Center, Miguel Cruz López explained that a multi-disciplinary team composed of doctors, nurses, nutritionists, and social workers has been established.
This team relies on the support of specialists and pupils of postgraduate studies from the National Institute of Public Health (INSP) and from the Centre for Research and Advanced Studies (Cinvestav) of the National Polytechnical Institute (IPN).
The general public, having or not authorized, can also participate in the research. People can come to the participating IMSS sports centers in Valle de México, where tests will be carried out by juveniles from 8 a.m.
He emphasized that the research began at the Cuahtémoc (Mexican State) Sports Centre and from October 8 this activity has also been carried out at Nezahualcóyolt (East of the Mexican State).
Once data ranges from 500 to 600 children, the study will continue at the Independencia Sports Center (southern from the Federal District) and eventually at the Morelos Sports Center (north of the Federal Centre).
Explained that the aim of this research is to determine the components and importance of genetics when it comes to the development of obesity, and to trace risk factors on Mexican children and young people to prevent the development of chronic degenerative diseases.
On the occasion of International Diabetes Day, which is celebrated on November 14, he recalled that the aim is to collect data from three thousand children and young people between the ages of six and 14, with the consent and under the supervision of parents, for the application of specific nutrition questionnaires.
The questionnaire will also ask about physical activity and family history of hereditary diseases in order to avoid risky factors that could cause diseases such as diabetes or kidney insufficiency.
He specified that every Saturday all participants make anthropometric measurements (weight, height, waist circumference), blood pressure is measured, glucose, triglicerides, cholesterol, insulin is determined and genes are examined (trying to identify the genes involved in the development of childhood obesity).
Cruz López said this research reflects health workers' concerns over an alarming increase in children's weight.
‘Health polls conducted between 1999 and 2006 showed that in just six years there was only an increase in obesity of 77% in boys and 47% in girls. The dramatic increase also occurred in the case of overweight’, an IMSS expert said.
He stated that one of the reasons for the search for indicators is the assumption that the population of the country differs from other populations in its genetic history.
He reported that the average Mexican has 65% of Indian-American genetic equipment, 30% European and 5% African. This fact is reflected in the genes that are responsible for the emergence of diabetes and overweight.
He explained that, in general, the genetics of obesity is commonly explained as the inability to burn the excess energy we consume (calories) and which is stored in the form of fats.
The researcher explained that the direct benefit of the participants is that they receive an overall assessment of the condition of the children, which determines whether they show any known overweight or obesity.
He stated that even the presence of wrinkles of dark color (acantosis) in the neck and forearm indicates the likelihood that a given individual will suffer from metabolic problems and pre-candy conditions.
So far, the information is confidential, doctors pass it on in writing to their parents, explaining all the measurements and lifestyle changes they have to make in order for their children to avoid disease.
Cruz López explained that there are three main components that cause overweight, obesity and other chronic diseases: a sedentary way of life meaning a lack of daily physical activity, all-day sitting in front of the television, and using means of transport instead of walking short distances.
Excess weight is also caused by a large caloric intake, i.e. greater consumption of sweetened beverages, carbohydrates (tamales, bread, pizza, burgers, etc.) with a high content of fats and proteins, and the genetic burden that is the subject of this research.
There won't be potholes in Asphalt from UNAM
Rafael Herrera, a scientist at UNAM, creates modified asphalts with polymers, creating a mixture with better functionality, regardless of climatic conditions and temperature
If paving had been taken care properly, there would be no need to repair almost 200 thousand potholes after each rainy season in the Valle de México Metropolitan Area, pointed out Rafael Herrera Nájera, a scientist at the Faculty of Chemistry (FQ) from UNAM, who also added that if the construction and maintenance of asphalt pavements were adequate, there would be a significant reduction in potholes, or even could be completely removed.
In his lecture Technological cause of potholes, which he uttered during the International Year of Chemistry, announced that the laboratory under his guidance develops modified asphalts with polymers, which should create a mixture with similar characteristics to asphalt, but at the same time greater mechanical resistance, both at low and at high temperatures.
Herrera Nájera believes that this kind of modified asphalt could be used in places with an extremely warm climate, for example, in some cities in the north of the country. This would improve functionality and reduce the formation of potholes.
Explained that asphalt, the heaviest part of oil, is a group of hydrocarbon molecules, molecules with a large molecular mass called asphaltens, and others with low molecular weight, which are called malteneses. Together, they give the asphalt a typical viscoelastic character.
The viscolastic character of asphalt is that at high temperatures, around 180 degrees, it behaves like a liquid, at 120 degrees it has high viscosity, and at 50 degrees it is almost solid, while at low temperatures the asphalt tends to break, he added.
This means that the resilience of asphalt in the streets is not uniform, but changes during the day according to meteorological conditions and temperature, a chemical engineering expert pointed out.
Scientist from the Faculty of Chemistry observed that most of the streets in Ciudad de México are covered with flexible paving, which consists of six layers of materials, the last three of which are mixed with asphalt.
Among the uppermost layers there is an asphalt insert, made up of small stones poured in the asphalt and then compressed.
He emphasized that every stone that makes a liner is covered with asphalt.
During the passage of cars or creating pressure, it is therefore possible that the forces in the pavement decompose well.
If it were modified asphalt, it would decompose even better, a chemical engineer thinks.
He added that on the asphalt liner, the last layer called cloak, made up of fine stones, i.e. sand also covered in asphalt.
Since this layer will come into contact with the rims of the vehicles, it must have more mechanical force than the asphalt insert and must ensure the friction of the road.
Due to the fact that as part of the pavement it will be subjected to a heavy load, the sheath must have a sufficient thickness and be careful of the material from which it is made.
The climate, especially humidity and temperature changes, are also a very important factor to keep in mind. In particular, water has a big impact on the functionality of the asphalt insert.
Once the terrain is wet, the water spreads inwards and it is difficult to get out, this causes softening of all layers of pavement and thus the formation of potholes.
He explained that that during the formation of potholes, the film of the cloak is the first to be damaged, and then the asphalt insert is damaged. However, the damage does not occur at one moment, but it is a gradual process, which should be stopped already at the first signs using appropriate maintenance programs.
As a member of the National System of Scientists, he also explained that the load on vehicles is another important factor in creating potholes, and therefore the thickness of each individual layer of paving must correspond to the load of vehicles and must be guaranteed that these loads will not be exceeded. This is the only way we can prevent the frequent formation of potholes.
Rafael Herrera pointed out that when it comes to asphalt, we still have a lot of work to do and the research is at the beginning. For example, asphalt emulsions, which are already available on the market in other countries and make it possible to easily repair potholes without having to use high temperatures.
What's been missing from the Valle candidate to become the new Miss Colombia?
Analyses of factors that contributed to Melina Ramírez Serna did not win the crown of Miss Colombia.
Valle thus wins the 14th title of the first multimiss.
Valle del Cauca again failed to reach Miss Colombia's crown.
Melina Ramírez Serna had to be satisfied that she found herself on the podium of winners, winning a silver crown and sash, on the basis of which she became Colombia's new first multimiss.
Based on the results of yesterday's final in Cartagena, in which she won and scepters took over Miss Atlántico, the aforementioned department won the 14th title of the first multi-miss. Melina thus joined the crowds of women from Valle del Cauca, who came within reach of the victorious crown, but were eventually declared the first multi-miss. Among them are Miriam Ospina Beno
The question that many ask themselves. What was missing?
The result is the defeat of a big favorite, who in all the previous rounds showed that she has the opportunity to become the new Miss Colombia, and not in vain to become the Queen of Police and the Most Pretty Face of Joile de Volgue. These titles were achieved, for example, by Taliana Vargas or Natalia Navarro, who subsequently took home the title of Miss Colombia.
One of the key factors that decided that the jury decided to name it only First Multimiss is the fact that it did not convince its answer to the question: "Which book did you like the most and why?" by Martín Murillo Gómez of Leamos literary editorial.
When asked, the candidate replied:
“I read a lot. I very much believe in the role of literature, I think it’s one of the forms of culturalization. The book that influenced me the most in my childhood was ‘Little Prince’, a very deep book.
I’m currently reading “Crime and Punishment”. It’s a book that’s part of the world’s literature that we should all know. And today I’ve got a book with me from Irene Nemirovski, a Russian author. It’s a wonderful book called “El ardor de la sangre”.
She didn't like this answer both because of the term "culturalization" and, secondly, because the candidate didn't explain what the individual books in question marked her.
At the end of the ceremony, Melina declared that she was calm and delighted with the result: "The world is wise what is to happen, it will happen.
And you people, read, if you can. The little prince is a wonderful book.”
One of the other hypotheses is that Valle has ruined the favorite's positions.
The Bogoth journalist suggested that the candidate’s most popular, most famous from the beginning, and the one who won all the previous prizes (Oster’s Gastronomic Challenge, the title of the public for the best art dress and the Queen’s mother) aroused the envy of other contenders, stressing out the queen, who then failed on the scene.
It was evident during the first presentation of swimsuits on Hilton beach and during the two shows before the final coronation.
Even though she shone on both of them, it was obvious that she lacked the strength and fervor, which was demonstrated by representatives from Magdalene and Atlántico.
Ariel Osorio, a journalist of the "As at Home" program at Canal RCN, believes that Melinda's first multi-miss title confirms the fact that the candidate who wins the most titles during the competition ultimately doesn't go with the Queen's crown."
Moreover, in recent days Valle has held a rather unkind attitude towards the media, and that has not paid off for him.
Another hypothesis is that Valle failed to convince the jury of its interest in social work, which is the basis of the National Beauty Contestation.
Just to the brink, two days before the election's election, international jury member Lucy Doughty said they were looking for a woman aware of her country's social needs.
"In the Miss Colombia competition beauty in itself is not enough, there is a need to see something more than just physical beauty. It is necessary to focus on social sensibilities. It is important for the candidates to be involved in such projects before entering the competition," she explained.
There is also speculation that Valle has lost points due to deficiencies in its background and fibrosis that was noticeable on the buttocks. However, this is completely contrary to the verdict of the jury, which elected her miss "natural beauty" for the healthiest body, proper nutrition and responsible physical activity.
Jorge Hernán Orozco, who is preparing the queens, acknowledges that Melina did not have the best body, that there were better girls, "but the problems with fibrosis on the butt, which in my opinion was not very noticeable, took away her points only on the last day".
Meanwhile, journalist Guido Hoyos of Cromos magazine believes that the Valle native made the mistake of undergoing several surgical procedures.
"Surgical interventions are fine, but they didn't succeed in Valle.
The body was never her strength, and this is where it took its toll."
Because of this award, which was first awarded by the National Beauty Assembly, there was a cruel prediction that came true after the swimsuit parade. It was claimed that this was the satisfaction Raymundo allowed Melina not to give her the highest crown in the end.
Whether it's true or not, it's certain that Melina started a year of her reign today, not as a queen, but as a princess, out of a position that gives her the opportunity to represent Colombia at various international competitions, where she will be named the National Competition itself.
However, it will go down in history as one of the queens who, despite the thrilling success in Cartagena, did not reach the highest crown.
Such was the work of the resident of Cali, who identified the corpse of Alfonso Cano
Fingerprint expert from Cali went down in history as a man who confirmed the death of "Alfonsa Cana."
The work of these officials is crucial.
On November 4 last year, a CTI fingerprint expert in Cali entered history as a man who confirmed the identity of Supreme Leader Farc: "Alfonse Cane," by comparing his fingerprints.
Of the seven fingerprint experts working in the CTI department this week, it was the turn of Eliazar González, a man from Puerta Tejada, who has been working in the field of judicial research for 21 years.
At the beginning of his career, González worked at the town hall in Chocó, where he often had to go through the forest for more than five days to get to the place where there were corpses that had to be identified.
In 2000, he moved to the capital of Valle, and after taking the appropriate courses, began specializing in fingerprints. This science deals with the study of friction of the skin, i.e. the palms of the hands and feet.
In the week when the operation was carried out against Alfonsi Cano, Eliazar was told to take his clothes with him for at least three days.
Something big was expected.
On Friday night, he set off for Cauco in a helicopter from Palmira.
He knew that he would have to identify two corpses and that he suspected that one of them was the man who led the world’s oldest guerrilla. That’s why they asked him to take Guillermo León Sáenz’s fingerprint card (his real name).
However, when he arrived with the investigating team at the place where the corpse was located, he did not recognize it because of his physical appearance. He looked completely different than in the media.
He didn't have a beard, dressed as a villager in the area, without glasses, much slimmer.
But fingerprints don't lie.
Along with galton magnifying glass, ink and transplanter and light Eliazar began to compare dactylic records of the corpse with card entries.
The eye didn't disappoint him: it was "Alfonso Cano."
That same night, a man originally from Puerto Tejada was commissioned to confirm to the Secretary of Defense, Juan Carlos Pinzón, whether he was really the commander-in-chief of Farc.
Searching for clues
For the dactyloscope from Sijín de Cali, the acknowledgement shown to Eliazar González by President Juan Manuel Santos at a press conference of the operational service against "Can" is very significant.
A lot of people don't know our jobs.
Dactyloscopy is an exact science, without the possibility of error, which can play a key role in decision-making.
In addition to identifying corpses, these people assist in crime research such as theft or homicide, confirming the identity of those caught.
When they're caught, a lot of delinquents present fake documents or don't have papers at all.
We're taking their fingerprints and looking for them in the database.
The investigator recounted that in the event of injury, he had to remove a piece of the wall of the house in the house, because at this place the thieve relieved to pull out a vault full of jewellery and money.
Walls are difficult because they aren't meant to take fingerprints, but in this case, we were able to get a full-handed imprint and identify an offender who was involved in several thefts," he recalled.
Fingerprints can also confirm or disprove suspicions in the case of murder.
Not long ago, they found a dead man with bottles of alcohol around them.
Based on the investigation, it was found that one of the family members could be responsible for the murder, and the imprint on the bottle actually confirmed it.
Although it can't be fully confirmed that he really was a murderer, it's obvious that he appeared on the crime scene.
A CTI fingerprint expert explained that from objects, fingerprints are "blurred using chemical or physical reactive substances."
When they appear, a transplanter or tape is used for their collection.
After that, fingerprints are sent to Asif, which is a database of delinquents, or to the Registrature.
"The problem is that there are still many Colombians whose figures are not recorded anywhere," the CTI expert said.
He explained that not everything is as shown in the CSI television programme.
Like everything that glitters, there's no gold, not on everything you touch, there's footprints.
However, if it can be used, the appropriate comparison must be made.
"Indignation" protests in New York
Hundreds of demonstrators crowded into Zuccotti Square, where they wanted to camp.
A large part of them waited on orders to vacate the space and moved to a nearby park.
Members of the Occupy Wall Street movement provoked representatives of New York on Tuesday as they tried to remake the court order again in Manhattan Square, from where they were taken out in the morning. Afterwards, they tried to seize another New Yorian park.
Hundreds of manifestors crowded into Zuccotti Square, where they wanted to get thanks to a court order, which permits "indignant" to return to the location. The other part of the movement reacted to the evacuation by occupying a park in the north of the city, where further detention took place.
"If the court does not allow us to return to the square, we will settle in a different place and continue the lawful battle in order to return to Zuccotti, both through gatherings and peaceful demonstrations," said Mark Bray, one spokesperson for the "Occupy Wall Street" movement.
Bray clarified that the response of the "indignants" to eviction of the camp ordered by the mayor of New York, Michael Bloomberg himself, went all the way to the trial, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order banning a violent eviction of the site and allowed the manifestors to continue camping here.
In addition, "indignance" has also seized another park located at the intersection of central Canal Street and Sixth Avenue, near the entrance to the Holland Tunnel, which connects New York and New Jersey, and the parish grounds at Trinity Church.
At least for an hour they managed to occupy the place, but then the police came and detained some manifestors. Bray didn't know the exact number, but stated that he had seen handcuffings deployed even to some journalists.
The organization assures that several hundred people settled at the scene. A delegation of religious leaders also came to express support for them and also tried to negotiate with the police.
In the meantime, Zuccotti Square, which is a private property, remains closed. A court hearing is expected, which is expected to take place just on Tuesday, and during which the legality of the morning evacuation should be analysed.
Some of the relocated manifestors were even distributing and handing out copies of a court order and even giving them to members guarding the square, sparking "some small clashes," Bray said, denouncing the fact that some of the officers beat the demonstrators.
A court order forbids authorities from evicting members of the "Ocupa Wall Street" movement or using "laws that were published after it began procurement, or forbidanting demonstrators from accessing a park with shops or other places they previously used."
Mayor Bloomberg told the press that due to that court order, the city has suspended the reopening of public space and has alerted demonstrators that local laws do not allow them to return with tents and sleeping bags.
The city's top official personally defended Zuccotti Square's morning eviction, an operation in which 200 people were apprehended and approached because manifestors "violated the law."
Bloomberg explained that he ordered the square to evacuate because it became "a place where people didn't go to protest, but breaking laws and, in some cases, hurting others" because "some shops were given threatening letters" and "neighbours were worried about the quality of their lives."
“Morning evacuation has been appalling and shows that Bloomberg is trying to maintain financial interests at the expense of the U.S. working-class people,” a spokesperson for the movement accused the mayor of “not appreciating freedom of expression.”
Representatives of the "Ocupa Wall Street" movement also said they planned to organize "big events" for Thursday in cooperation with community and trade unions to commemorate the two months after which the protests last, which began on September 17.
Pamela Anderson will represent the Virgin Mary in a special Christmas TV show
Along with Michael Bublé and other notable Canadians, he will appear on the television programme "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1
Pamela Anderson was an assistant to Tim Allen in "Home Improvement," a lifeguard in "Baywatch," a bunny in "Plaboy," the wife of year-old musician Tommy Lee, and now the actress and model will be the Virgin Mary in a special Christmas show of Canadian television.
CTV announced today that Anderson, along with sentimental song singer Michael Bublé and other prominent Canadians, will appear on the television program "It's and Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1 and will embody Our Lady.
CTV today showed footage from a television program in which Anderson, also known from online domestic pornographic images, will appear with his ex-husband, "Mötley Crüe", Tommy Leem, as Our Lady holding a doll in the crösters.
Peters, a well-known Canadian comedian of Indian descent who has recently served as a presenter on Charlie Sheena's "My Violent Torpedo of Trudt," will appear on the scene.
In addition to Anderson and Peterse, Canadian singer Bublé, who married Argentine model Luisana Lopilato in Buenos Aires at the end of March and is close to controversy, will appear in CTV.
Recently Bublé called Kim Kardashian at one of her concerts in New York a "fox."
Japan is coming back to life after a devastating earthquake
GDP grows by 1.5% thanks to household consumption, ending the period of falls, which began in March
Since March last year, Japan's economy has been paralyzed, now recovering from a devastating earthquake that has outgrown into a nuclear crisis, the country's worst-ranked disaster since World War 2.
The Tokyo government's first estimates quantified material losses at US$300 000 million, a wound that heals well despite a difficult global situation.
After three quarters of falls, the value of GDP between July and September came in such a position that it was possible to turn the site with definitive effect: GDP grew by 1.5% compared to the previous quarter and by 6% per year.
In addition, the second-quarter results - for the Japanese, the tax year begins in March 2012 - moreover, they forecast the largest growth rate compared to the January-March 2010 period.
The revival can be partially explained by the purchase of machines that are necessary for the restoration of areas destroyed by the earthquake and subsequent tsunami.
Businesses increased their investments by 1.1%, spending will fall by 0.9% compared to the January-March period. This is the first decline after six months of continuous growth.
However, the real awakening of Japan’s economy stems solely from an impetus that forces us to take advantage of the crisis to come out of strengthening or to restore infrastructures. The point is to take advantage of the scarcity to start completely from scratch.
Japan has woken up. In particular, thanks to the development of domestic consumption, which currently accounts for 60% of Japan's GDP.
This variable increased by 1%, thanks to an increase in spending on vehicles, roads and leisure activities.
Stigma yen
Flows were confided in the internal market when yen – the Japanese currency – became one of the “rescue values” for investors fleeing the eurozone’s debt crisis and U.S. worries. It’s a privilege, but it’s also a big burden on export businesses.
With a stronger currency, products abroad are more expensive, which is negative for a country whose economy is determined by the financial health of large technology groups.
For example, Toyota or Sony attributed their weak results in July to the yen exchange rate in September.
While markets continue to besiege the EU's outskirts, Yoshihiko Noda's government is prudent.
"We have to pay attention to the risks that persist, such as the destruction of some foreign economies, the impact of floods in Thailand and the rapid increase in the yen," Motohisha Furukawa, Secretary of State for Economic Policy, said yesterday.
Indeed, perspectives aren’t encouraging.
The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) specified that most member countries showed clear signs of an economic slowdown in September.
An indicator that foresees inflexibility in the world economy has shown that the economy of major powers is slowing.
It fell four-tenths to 100.4 points compared with 100.8 in August.
The eurozone even got below the average, which is located at the level of 100. It got up to 99.13 points.
Athens spreads uncertainty again
Papadimos says austerity measures will be approved, but Conservatives won't make any written commitment
With the swift and risky marginal guarantees and uncertainty that has reignited once again, Greece is becoming increasingly entangled in the labyrinth, which leads to an exit from Europe's major debt crisis.
The assumption that the "conformity" of the new transitional Greek government is due to the demands of Brussels rather than the true conviction of political forces is beginning to be confirmed.
While the new prime minister, Lukas Papadimos, admitted yesterday that there was no alternative for structural reforms and that the 100-day period he was given at his appointment is likely to be short for their application, the chairman of the Conservative New Democration Party, Antonis Samaras, declared that he would in no way support the new austerity measures and reiterated his request to call a new election on February 19.
"The main task of this government is to apply the decision of the summit of October 26," Papadimos declared yesterday during a parliamentary debate that will culminate in a vote of confidence in the government tomorrow.
A package with 24 programmes to promote employment, the reform of a disciplinary code of officials and new measures against tax evasion will be among the first initiatives.
However, Samarase's message does not clarify doubts about his position: "We are committed to helping the transitional government, but we are no longer committed to anything else."
In his address to the EU and IMF, the Conservatives’ president went a little far. These organizations are supposed to unblock the £8 billion that Athens urgently needs. At the same time, however, he declared that he would not support and sign any commitment regarding the implementation of austerity measures.
The European Commission, angered by the impact caused by Papandreo's games with the referendum, highlighted yesterday that its demands are unexaggerable: it won't unblock any aid if the new government and main-party leaders don't sign "written commitment without ambiguity and as soon as possible."
A spokesperson for Economic Affairs, Amadeu Altafaj, explained that the inspectors from Troika will return "very soon" to Athens and negotiate with the new government about "what it needs to pay for the sixth part" 8 billion, but there is no definitive date.
The Greek spinner has a lot to knit for a while yet.
Queen Sofia: 'There are tools for progress and hope'
The Queen highlights Spain's privileged position in relation to this kind of finance
Spain has "achieved an important position at global level in terms of microfinance".
In the last two decades, Queen Sophia recalled that in the past two decades, the country has become the second donor to the planet in terms of this instrument of cooperation, the essence of which is "that this is an instrument of social progress, justice and hope for a better future for all of humanity. It is a tool based on trust in man."
The Queen yesterday launched the 5th Global Micro-Loan Summit in Valladolid with the belief that "there is no doubt" that this kind of operations "is absolutely necessary" in order to achieve the Millennium Goals set by the U.N. by 2015. "Especially, this is about meeting goals designed to reduce by half the number of people who live with less than one dollar a day, that is, with an amount that sets the poverty threshold."
Spain "will continue to work" and expand the network of volunteers "in all corners of the planet and takes this task responsibly," Queen Sophia stressed, hoping that the initiatives and benefits of the Summit, which will last until Thursday, "will ensure the creation of better capacities and opportunities for the poorest, and especially for women", to whom these loans are primarily intended.
"We have achieved a lot" and "many challenges are still ahead."
The microloaning summit is supposed to be not only a discussion forum, but also a platform that will support "basic goals."
Her Majesty explained that the goal is that by the end of 2015 175 million poor families have access to these financial services and that 100 million families get beyond the poverty threshold under which they are currently living.
The concept of micro-loans spread to Spain and other countries of the world "when Muhammad Yunus, a pioneer of the idea, has embarked on this journey," the Queen said, adding that this was "an act of justice and necessary recognition."
She described her father as an "admirable idealist who embarked on a path to the fight against poverty". She became acquainted with his work 15 years ago when she went to Bangladesh to get acquainted directly on the spot with his loan policy.
This experience was followed by others. The first worldwide summit in Washington and other meetings, such as in Côte d'Ivoire in 1999, Canada in 2006. "In all these meetings, I was able to establish constant developments, explained the Queen, who, thanks to her support for this project, earned yesterday the title of "head of the family" given to her in Valladolid by the "movement of the poor".
Queen Sofia concluded her speech by urging attendees to engage in discussions and seeking solutions, thereby allowing them to continue the fight against poverty.
'Ethical imperative'
Minister of Foreign Affairs, Trinidad Jiménez, said that solidarity "is an ethical imperative" that all democratic states have to follow.
In this sense, she said it would be a mistake to associate cooperation with developing countries only with a period of ballooning, because this cooperation is a "permanent duty."
Jiménez believes that microloans represent a "serious and honest policy to eliminate poverty," and now, more than ever before, the governments of developed countries must work in accordance with the criteria of solidarity and do not allow solidarity to fall victim to cuts.
For Spain, microloaning is one of the main lines of development cooperation.
The Secretary of State for International Cooperation, Soraya Rodríguez, has welcomed the success that celebrates a concept based on giving confidence to the people to whom everyone else is shutting the door. The mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier León de la Riva, has welcomed participants in a city that "opens its door to the spirit of solidarity that is contagious."
The CDU proposes to elect a European president on the basis of universal suffrage
Without serious trouble for the chancellor and chairwoman of the Christian Democratic Party, Angela Merkel took place on Monday, November 14 in Leipzig, the first day of the Christian Democratic Union (CDU).
The question of Europe's future was originally not on the agenda, but it did not happen until the autumn, in particular to be satisfied by the members of the party, who regard the government's policy today towards the Greeks and perhaps tomorrow towards the Italians to be lax.
The proposal concerning the President of the European Commission filed by management has been adopted almost unanimously.
Only nine out of 1001 delegates were opposed, with 10 abstentions.
A several-minute standing ovation at the conclusion of Angela Merkel's speech, and then a few hours later Wolfgang Schäuble, a very pro-European finance minister, left no room for doubt.
The main idea of the chancellor and proposal can be summarized in the slogan: the response to the crisis "is not less Europe, but more Europe".
Not just because Europe means peace, but because "nine million jobs depend directly on the euro."
According to the chancellor in Europe, "the time for change has come."
This could be expressed by changes in the Treaty of Lisbon, allowing it to strengthen European Union control over the budgetary policies of eurozone member countries, but it could also result in the election of the president of the European Commission on the basis of universal suffrage "in order to give Europe one face."
In any case, this is one of the ideas adopted by the Congress, of which Wolfgang Schäuble is clearly an inspirator.
It is certainly unlikely that all twenty-seven countries will quickly agree on this revolutionary step: the United Kingdom opposes it, and CDU leaders think Nicolas Sarkozy will take the same stance.
But the direction is set.
Likewise, the delegates rejected by a large majority the proposal to evaluate the weight of each country on the board of directors of the European Central Bank, depending on the weight of its economy, which will evidently result in the strengthening of Germany's weight.
Driven by worrying about calming the climate inside her party, the chancellor has made several references to Konrad Adenauer and Helmut Kohl. However, she has not tried to gain a congress of talk about "small" European states or states that are in trouble, willing to criticize arrogant Germany.
Although the proposal concerning Europe devotes one paragraph to the importance of "French-German friendship", Angela Merkel did not cite France.
Only indirectly.
Just two hundred years ago, there was a bloody battle in Leipzig.
In fact, it was one of the most humiliating defeats of the Napoleonic army.
What does scam when incapacitated?
After support, the UMP party and the government are launching an offensive against incapacity for work.
Nicolas Sarkozy will be in Bordeaux on Tuesday, December 15, and will visit the Office for the Provision of Social Aid, where he will give a speech on the subject of recurrencing since 2007 on combating social fraud and the redistribution of solidarity.
As an accompaniment to this offense, which is based on the will to make savings in the context of increased rigor but is no less clear from the point of view of the right-wing presidential campaign, the right-wing majority wants to focus on incapacity for work.
At first, because of budget savings, the government intends to impose on private sector employees the fourth day of incapacity for work before they start taking support in sickness.
Clearly, without the consent of a business, an employee who falls ill would lose four days of wages, i.e. at least 15% of their monthly income.
The measure, which risks causing a lot of bad blood, but which, according to Les Echos, could bring 280 million euros.
And also a measure that would be accompanied by another measure of symbolic reach: to give civil servants one day unreimbursed incapacity for work (until now they have no incompetency compared to three days in the private sector).
While these issues are discussed, the right-wing majority is also launching the issue of fraud, which this time focuses on mischievously tied to incapacity for work.
"The checks will be much more extensive and, moreover: if you're caught, you'll be back," Xavier Bertrand pointed out during an interview provided to RTL on Sunday, November 13.
As in other cases, the effects of the notification seem somewhat disproportionate due to the truth about iniquities in case of incapacity for work.
In fact, these have been the subject of constant surveillance since 2002 and the political onslaught that was bearing fruit.
The French are no more sick than elsewhere in Europe.
Let’s stop at a few official numbers first.
According to Caisse nationale d'assurance maladie (CNAM - state health insurance companies), more than 237 million euros of daily compensation were paid in 2006.
According to the French Institute for Research and Documentation in Health Care, in 2008 daily refunds accounted for 11.3 billion euros for health expenditure.
Of this amount, 46% concerned maternity allowances and accidents at work and 54% concerned incapacity for work, i.e. 6,2 billion euros.
Only 2.5% of healthcare costs.
According to a study by Alma Consulting, the French had an average of 14.5 days of incapacity for work in 2010, compared to 17.8 days in 2009.
An average that hides certain deviations: another poll published in 2007 on Monster.com's job opportunities website showed that 75% of the 40,000 French employees surveyed replied that they had not chosen any day of incapacity for work.
Another study, this time carried out by sickness insurance institutions, specifies that in 2010 37.8% of incapacities were less than 8 days, 22% from 8 to 14 days, 15% from 15 days to 1 month, 15% from 1 to three months, and 11% lasted longer.
When we compare them to their European neighbours, French employees are not more prone than elsewhere to incapacity for work.
One long study conducted in 2010 by two CNRS (National Research Centre) researchers showed that between 1994 and 2001 the overall absence (for health reasons or not) oscillated in France between 10% and 11.8% versus 20% and 28% in Denmark, 15% in the UK and 18% in the Netherlands.
Cheating during incapacity for work represents only a very small part compared to black work.
When it comes to fraud, these are of little scale.
The strengthening of the legislation since 2002 has caused systematic checks aimed at incapacity for more than 45 days.
In 2008, CNAM noted that with 1.5 million checks, 13.8% of the 285,000 were carried out due to short-term incapacities "unauthorised or too long," i.e. 37,050 cases.
In the case of systematically controlled incapacities of more than 45 days, 11% of cases "unadapted or unauthorised" amounted to 1,2 million checks, i.e. 132 000.
Thus, a total of 169 000 "unauthorised" cases for several million employees who had incapacity for work.
A number that represents much less than a number expressing the work on black.
According to UMP Member Dominique Tiano, the author of the June report on this issue, this figure would represent 9 to 15 billion euros missing in the collection of taxes for a year, i.e. more than the total amount of daily refunds and thus infinitely more than the fraud associated with the latter.
Papademos: Greece will have to apply a new bailout plan
Greece will need a new bailout to rebuild its economy.
On Monday, December 14, Prime Minister Lucas Papademos spoke before parliament, adding that a hundred days given to his unity government would not be enough to fulfill this task.
“We need the support of our European partners and a new bailout budget plan in order to get our economy back on our feet,” he said.
Taking the decisions taken on October 27 at the eurozone summit will be the "main task" of the new government, as our country's participation in the eurozone is at play, he added.
As a result, Mr Papademos projected that the country's public deficit from now until the end of the year would be "around 9%" of gross domestic product after being 10.6% in 2010 and 15.7% in 2009.
Right rejects new austerity measures
Right-wing leader Antonis Samaras voiced his support for the measures already approved on Monday in order to try to escape from the debt crisis, but cautioned that his party does not commit to any additional austerity measures.
"We will not raise our hand for any new measures," Samaras stressed at a meeting with MPs on behalf of his New Democracy party.
We have agreed on targets for reducing the deficit and debt and combating waste, but we are opposed to any policy that would hinder the economic recovery.
Mr Olli Rehn, the European Commissioner for Economic Affairs and Currency, who warned that the IMF and the EU would not block the necessary loan of 8 billion euros without a written guarantee from all parties that would support the envisaged measures, Antonis Samaras replied that his word was sufficient and that he would not sign anything under external pressure.
Euthanasia and assisted suicide need to be legalized, expert committee says
According to a report by the Royal Canadian Society, Canadians are being denied death.
In terms of palliative care, the commission urgently requires governments, health care institutions and doctors to work together to provide the best palliative care, even in cancer cases.
Canadian society lives by the idea of denying death.
Only 9% of Canadians actually accept the possibility of talking to a doctor about the conditions in which they want to die and takes decisions in this sense.
This is one of the conclusions of an extensive report by the Royal Canadian Society, which involved six experts working in different fields, including ethics, law and medicine.
This commission advises the government to change criminal law in order to allow assisted suicide and euthanasia if this option appears necessary for a patient considered competent to take such a decision.
At the beginning, experts cite a recent poll by the independent Economist Intelligence Unit, which compared the quality of death in 40 countries of the planet.
Even though Canada was ranked 10th, the study notes that “the medicalization of death in Canada gave rise to a culture in which people are afraid to talk about death.”
Medication yes or no, 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, experts with the mandate of the Royal Canadian Society tell us.
And in spite of wanting to die at home that most Canadians have, 68.6% of them die in hospital.
This average applies to all of Canada, while in Québec province this number is the highest with 86% of those killed in hospital.
When it comes to the delicate issue of euthanasia and assisted suicide, the report's authors come to conclusions that go against the position of the federal government, which has already said it does not want to change anything about the criminal law relating to euthanasia and assisted suicide.
According to experts, who have also subjected control to all countries where these approaches are legalized or are not considered a criminal case, there is no evidence that this decriminalization causes any abuse in the application, i.e. in the practice of euthanasia or assisted suicide to non-appropriate or non-conspirants.
On the contrary, Mr. Jocelyn Downie, co-author of the report, confirmed yesterday during a teleconference more than numerous cases of these misconceptions in countries where euthaniasis and assisted suicide are not legalized.
"Euthanasia is also carried out in Canada, where it is clearly illegal."
“Assisted suicide is also done in Canada, where it is clearly illegal,” explains Jocelyn Downie.
Canadians are inclined to euthanasia
Recall that, according to an investigation carried out by Angus Reid in 2010, 85% of Canadians have already spoken out in favour of euthanasia because they believe they will allow end-of-livers to ease their suffering.
And 66% believe that the legalization of euthanasia will not mean that we are sending a message that the lives of the elderly or the handicapped are worth less.
With regard to persons who practice assisted suicide, 41% of those questioned believe that they should not be prosecuted.
The situation is less clear about maintaining or interrupting patient care, the report's authors say.
They also propose in the list of their recommendations that it should be stated in criminal law that withdrawal from treatment for which there is a legally confirmed refusal is not punishable.
The Commission of Experts also requires professional doctors to be educated for the obligation to respect end-of-life refusals without fear of prosecution.
In terms of palliative care, the commission urgently requires governments, health care institutions and doctors to work together to provide the best palliative care, even in cancer cases.
Finally, in a matter of palliative or finally soothing already widely used in Canadian health facilities, the commission demands that it be considered euthanasia when it no longer serves to reduce physical pain and to be subject to the same procedures.
Reservations from other experts
Some experts on medical law have already voiced their reservations about the conclusions of a report by experts from the Royal Canadian Society.
Pierre Deschamps, a specialist in medical law, has qualified the position of experts-signatories of the report as "extreme" because, among other things, it does not limit the practice of euthanasia to the sicks at the final stage.
“This opens the door to a situation where anyone who would be tired of life and who would be more than 18 years of age could be asked to help him commit suicide,” says Pierre Deschamps, whose has researched rhyme reports yesterday.
P. Deschamps also underscores that it looks as if the authors of the report put the autonomy of a person above all the other values that shape society's social fabric.
For "in society, he says, there are warning signs and limitations."
Medicinal law specialist Margaret Somerville also expressed "strong objections to the conclusions of the report, which she qualified as a "manifesto for euthanasia".
Margaret Somerville, who is herself a member of the Royal Canadian Society, doubts in particular about the data on the absence of misconduct in some countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are legalized, i.e. in the Netherlands and the U.S. state of Oregon.
Jordan king calls on President Assad to resign
Damascus speaks of "complete"
Syria is increasingly isolated
Bashar al-Assad's supporters manifested before the Ministry of Foreign Affairs yesterday.
Yesterday, diplomatic pressures have intensified on Syrian President Bashar al-Assad, who, with the support of Russia, resists them and speaks of a "complete" against his country.
Two days after the suspension of Syria's membership in the Arab League, the European Union intensified its sanctions against Damascus, while Turkey and Jordan, the countries neighbouring Syria, took a stand in favor of President Assad's departure.
King Abdallah II of Jordan yesterday was the first ruler of an Arab state calling on President Bashar al-Assad to be killed in Syria, where about forty civilians and members of the regime's military forces have been killed.
“If Bashar had the interests of his country at heart, he would have to resign, but he would also have to create the conditions necessary for a new phase of Syrian political life,” he said in an interview with the BBC.
On behalf of his party, the head of Turkish diplomacy, Ahmet Davutoglu, regretted that the effort dedicated to Turkish mediation with the Syrian regime, which had been done since the beginning of the year, has not been crowned with success.
“Those who do not live in peace with their people in the Middle East and can’t satisfy them will leave,” he said, alluding to neighboring Syria, against whom Turkey is taking an increasingly tough line.
The United States rejoiced at "strengthening the consensus against Assad and the rogue conduct of the Syrian regime" following the decisions of the Arab League and the European Union.
"The international community, the United States, the European Union, the League of Arab states, countries like Turkey are applying an increasingly harsh tone" against repression in Syria, noted Mark Toner, a spokesman for the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
According to the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights, 16 civilians and 19 members of pro-regime forces were killed in the city of Deraa in the south of Syria.
According to this NGO, two other civilians were killed "in gunfights and concentrated shootings from heavy machine guns in the city of Jobar" in the Homs area.
At a press conference, Syrian Foreign Minister Walid Mouallem estimated that the country had “gone the way out of the crisis.”
After all, he reacted vividly to the decision of the Arab League to suspend Damascus membership, which, according to him, is a "dangerous step".
Syria "does not bow," he added with the assurance that "completes spun against Syria will not succeed."
After its decision to suspend Syria’s membership, the Arab League is currently deciding a “civilian protection mechanism” and plans to send 500 human rights defenders, journalists and military observers into the country.
French Foreign Minister Alain Juppé is also favorable with the idea of sending U.N
The league's new special meeting convenes for tomorrow in Rabat to take a position on a plan to resolve the crisis, which was decided on December 2, in which the withdrawal of armies from cities plagued by disputes and liberation of imprisoned manifestos was envisaged in particular.
Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov yesterday considered the decision of the Arab League to be "erroneous", while China urged Syria to adopt a plan to solve the crisis, but at the same time completely avoided support for possible sanctions against Damascus.
Italy
Monti demands time and casualties from the Italians
Italy's next government chief, Mario Monti, has asked the markets for a bit of time so that he can form his team and start implementing a program that will contain "victims" in order to regain the credibility lost in recent months of Berlusconi's government.
The former European commissioner was discussing with political parties yesterday before meeting with representatives of trade unionists and employers today.
It should assemble a relatively slim cabinet composed of technocrats not belonging to parliamentary parties.
"Monti talked about an important agenda with many sacrifices," said Francesco Nucara, an MP for one of the numerous parliamentary groups attending the talks, after a meeting with the proposed prime minister.
The Speaker of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, expects Mario Monti to ask parliament by Friday to express confidence to make sure that the new government has sufficient support.
The consultation will end with Mario Monti introducing a small team of twelve ministers to President Giorgio Napolitano.
Mario Monti yesterday commenated the nervousness in the financial markets and asked them for time.
In "democracy, the exact deadlines" when it comes to the preparation of the government and its agenda, he emphasized, adding that it is "sure that the markets will be patient and understand."
His appointment as president was favorably embraced from the start, but concern resurfaced after an unexpected September slump in eurozone industrial production.
The appointed prime minister should assemble a team involving mainly technocrats, although he emphasized that he would like to accept "politicians".
He expressed his belief that the new government will last until 2013, i.e. until the date of the next election.
According to Speaker Gianfranco Fini, the new government will receive the confidence of parliament by Friday.
Mario Monti, after taking office, promised to negotiate with extraordinary urgency and with the aim that Italy will "become a strong link" of Europe again.
A spokesperson for European Commissioner Olli Rehn stressed that "even with the new government, our diagnosis of the Italian economy does not change."
The European Union believes that, despite the austerity plans adopted in recent months, Rome will not achieve its goal, which is a balanced budget in 2013, calling for new tough measures.
Confindustria business leader Emma Marcegaglia, who will meet with Monti on Tuesday, focused on the need to revive the economy "since a country that does not show growth cannot adhere to the deficit parameters."
Mario Monti, a former European Commissioner (1994-2004) recognized for his expertise and independence,"professor symbolizing, according to the authors of the editorials, "a call to seriousness" and "other Italy", represents an "epochal change" for Italy after 17 years of "Berlusconism".
The great unknown will be his team's time of life.
President Napolitano would like to avoid early elections, as Italy must place 200-billion-euro government bonds by April 2012.
A Soviet scientist has helped Iran's nuclear program
Soviet scientist Vyacheslav Danilenko helped Iran develop a detonator usable for an atomic weapon, a U.S. NGO specializing in atomic risks reported Tuesday.
The Institute for Science and International Security (ISIS) relies on a recent report by the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) and other documents leaked from the U.N
Last week, the IAEA communicated its "serious concern" in terms of the "possible military dimension" of Iran's nuclear program.
According to the ISIS Institute widely drawn from IAEA documents, V. Danilenko (born 1934) should work for three decades since the 1960s at the Soviet Military Research Center in Chelyabinsk (Ural), where he was supposed to be engaged in the production of synthetic diamonds by the explosion method.
In 1989 or 1991, he was to leave the center to establish a "nano-diamants" company in Kiev.
The economic difficulties of his company were supposed to lead him to contact the Iranian embassy in Ukraine in 1995.
Prior to his return to Russia, he was supposed to cooperate on the Iranian programme from 1996 to 2002.
In its latest IAEA report, it recalls "clear indications that the development of the detonation system carried out by Iran" was based on the work of a foreign specialist who not only knew this method well, but who, as one informed the Agency about it, worked during a substantial part of his working career on this technology within the framework of the country's military nuclear program where it came from".
Anti-Muslim crimes and offences have risen by 50%
According to FBI statistics released on Monday, the number of anti-Muslim-focused criminal offences rose by almost 50% from 2009 to 2010, while at the same time other racial and religious violence declined slightly or rose slightly.
According to these figures, the total number of violence committed against Muslims increased from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, i.e. a 49% increase compared to 13% in violence against Catholics, a decrease of 4% for offences against Jews, and an overall increase of 14% in anti-religious crimes and offences.
The total number of "hate crimes" has risen very lightly to 6,628 cases, of which 47.3% were motivated by racial differences and 20% religious differences, according to the FBI.
"After the decline in 2009, it is disturbing to see these criminal acts and offences to rise again," emphasized Human rights First, an organization dealing with human rights "the increase in anti-Muslim violence is particularly significant," the organization added in its communiqué.
“Human Rights First has long emphasized that violence against Muslims, as well as all forms of hate crimes, must be seen as a serious violation of a person’s rights,” Paul Legendre said.
"The U.S. government can and must do more to deal with these mischievouses," he adds, estimating that this could take place by improving police reports of "hate crimes."
"Hateful criminal act" in U.S. federal law is the legacy of the civil rights fight.
This rule of law was adopted after the death of Martin Luther King to punish violence related to race, skin color, religion, origin and now sexual orientation.
Twitter is changing, and it’s more like Facebook
A site providing microblogging services Twitter introduced design changes on Monday to allow its users to observe the activities of successors, somewhat liking Facebook's services.
New offers "activity" and "name" allow you to know better and directly the activities of other persons watching you or those whose answers ("tweets") you follow.
This allows you to know if they allow you to track posts, whether they sign up for new accounts, whether they have favorite posts, etc.
This new Twitter face has been tested "in August on a small percentage of users" before it was extended to the entire network on Monday, Twitter spokeswoman Carolyn Penner told AFP.
Twitter, which was known for its little instinctive functioning, with terms somewhat incomprehensible (RT for the repeated dissemination of someone else's post, followfire pour recommander de suivre un autre utilisateur) faisant le bonheur des initiés, s'ouvre ainsi and un public plus large avec un maniement plus and celui des réseaux
