Austria
enrollment of a golden rule on a balanced budget into the Constitution
On Tuesday, the government of a grand coalition of Social Democrats and Christian Democrats, which is in power in Austria, decided on Tuesday to enroll the "golden rule" of budgetary policy in order to reduce the public deficit and thus avoid a possible downgrade of Austria's rating by credit rating agencies (AAA).
It was Social-Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann himself who announced this decision at the conclusion of a meeting of ministers in a situation where Austria's public debt, with its 74.6%, is high above a criterion of a maximum of 60% established by the Maastricht Agreement, but much lower than the debts of countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
"If Austria's solvency had been reduced by just one degree from AAA to AA+, we'd have to pay three billion euros more in interest each year," argued Michael Spindelegger, the multi-chancellor and secretary of state.
Austria thus builds on the request of a summit of eurozone countries held in Brussels on October 26, 2011, where the heads of state and government strongly requested that by the end of 2012 the reduction of the public deficit and the achievement of the balance of public accounts be entered into the constitution or into a law of the same value by the end of 2012.
The instrument adopted by the Austrian government is approaching the "German model", Germany was the first European country to adopt such a "rule".
By 2017, the public structural deficit will have to fall by 0.75% of gross domestic product (GDP) every year, and from 2017 the general structural deficit of the federal state will not be allowed to exceed 0.35% of GDP each year, while the Länder, as well as municipalities, will have to report a balanced budget.
The aim is to get public debt under the 60% criterion established by the Maastricht Treaty in 2020/21.
Stronge only
Noda threatens new intervention in foreign exchange markets
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that Japanese institutions would have to intervene again in foreign exchange markets if they were just supposed to continue strengthening.
"We'll intervene like we did for the last time if we find too much instancy" in the yen's exchange rate, he explained to Senate Y. Noda.
On Tuesday, it rose only again to the historical values imposed by Japanese authorities on October 31 for intervention.
On that day, Tokyo sold yen for dollars to reduce the value of the Japanese currency, which only recently broke the record against the dollar in force since 1945.
But only since then has it regularly risened supported by the purchases of investors, who consider it a "safe haven" in precarious times of the world's economic slowdown and the European crisis caused by indebtedness.
On Tuesday, the dollar fell below 77 yen again, and the euro fell below a symbolic bar of 105 yen near the levels that triggered the latest intervention.
Prior to the 2008-2009 financial crisis, the dollar was worth around 120 yen and the euro more than 160 yen.
This flare-up Japanese currency is detrimental to island exports, as it raises the price of Made in-Japan products and reduces the value of yields earned by Japanese companies from abroad when they convert them into the domestic currency.
"Japan is recovering" from the destruction caused by the March earthquake and tsunami that devastated the Tohoku region in the northeast of the country. Noda, who fears that this price increase won't kill a fragile recovery in the egg.
The world's third economic power resumed growth in the third quarter (1.5% relative to the previous quarter) for the first time in one year thanks to a survey of growth in exports and household consumption burdened by the disaster.
But the prime minister concluded that the high value of the yen did not reflect Japan's "national economy."
Japan's finance minister Jun Azumi called on the Japanese Central Bank (BoJ), who launched a two-day meeting of its political and monetary policy council on Tuesday to take all possible measures to weaken the yen.
But Hirohide Yamaguchi, the institution's multi-governor, pointed out that BoJ had previously taken "appropriate measures" suggesting that no additional monetary weakening could be expected on Wednesday.
At its last meeting at the end of October, the Securities Issuance Institute raised its Treasury purchases by 5,000 billion yen (47 billion euros), raising the ceiling of the amounts it devotes to the purchases of various securities to flood with liquidity.
Eurozone:
rates rise, stock exchanges fail
The risk of contagioning the debt crisis is still undermining European indicators.
Despite the appointment of new heads of governments in Greece and Italy and better growth numbers in France and Germany, the rates of weakened countries continue their growth.
European stock exchanges are still concerned about the stability of the eurozone on Tuesday, November 15, falling into negative expectations.
In Paris at 10 a.m., the CAC 40 index loses 1.4% to 3.064 points.
The Dax index in Frankfurt loses 0.4% and in London 0.1%.
“Withing new tensions over the proceeds of eurozone loans in trouble, stock markets around the world continue to make only part of recent gains.”
"Nervous investors are only too aware of the risk of contagion," explains Terry Pratt, a broker at IG Markets, quoted by Reuters.
The Tokyo Stock Exchange closed with a 0.7% decline suffered due to market participants' concerns about Europe's ability to cope with the debt crisis.
“Japan, like the rest of the world, has its eyes fixed on Europe, and the exchange will only continue in a narrow corridor of variations until investors have evidence that the situation is stabilized,” estimates Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
This re-launching uncertainty about the eurozone's future is damaging the currency itself, which continues its downturn on Tuesday.
The euro is reaching its lowest level since a month and a half ago.
At 10 a.m., the European currency is 1.3570 dollars at a retreat of 0.4%.
According to brokers, the currency backed away after Chancellor Angela Merkel's CDU party accepted a proposal at its congress on Monday presumiting the possibility of leaving the eurozone for countries that find themselves in trouble without having to leave the European Union.
Italian and Spanish rates far beyond 6%
The interest rates on loans in Italy and Spain remain at a very disturbing level.
On Tuesday morning, the yield of Italian 10-year bonds reaches 6.85% and the yield on Spanish 10-year bonds is 6.2%.
These tensions affecting the interest rates of southern European countries, but also in France, continue to strengthen the concerns of market participants.
The head of the future Italian government, Mario Monti, strongly demanded time in the markets so that he could remedy the situation in Italy at risk of poison caused by the amount of its debt.
Greece's new prime minister, Lucas Papademos, has confirmed that the implementation of the decisions taken on October 27 at the eurozone summit will be "the main task" of his government.
Spain finds itself once again in a deliberate line of markets as elections to the House of Commons take place in the country on Sunday.
The rate difference ("spread") between the ten-year-old bonds of Germany and Spain reached a new historical maximum.
In anticipation of the numbers announcing the growth of the eurozone
Statistical numbers say France experienced economic growth of 0.4% in the third quarter, mainly due to household consumption, i.e. a slightly better-than-expected growth of 0.3%.
However, France's National Institute for Statistics and Economic Studies INSEE revised the downward numbers for the second-quarter to a decline of 0.1% of GDP instead of stagnation.
And in truth, it's the fourth quarter that worries economists the most.
During the same period, Germany registered growth of 0.5%.
The first estimates for the entire euro area are expected at 11 a.m., while the European Commission has just highlighted the risk of a new recession.
Energy Policy:
Facts must prevail over dogmas
If there is a subject that cannot be grasped on the basis of short-term deliberations or under emotions, this is energy policy.
The disposability and price of electricity have a direct impact on the purchasing power and competitiveness of enterprises.
It's natural for an election meeting to be an opportunity to think about such deposits and about a place that occupies energy from the core.
The debate will only be successful if all the economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed possibilities are explored.
The French must base their position on objective facts and rational data.
There are 7 billion human beings on the planet so far, but demographics will trigger a duplication of demand for electricity from now until 2050 if we don’t want to deprive a large part of the world’s population of an important part of our lives.
At the same time, it will be necessary to face a shortage of fossil resources in order to continue to produce electricity, while it is not possible to forget the necessary need to reduce the emissions of gases causing the greenhouse effect in order to combat climate change, a subject which is currently disappearinging from environmental debates.
An accident in Fukushima does not affect any of these parameters.
This is whay Germany remains alone in its decision to reject nuclear energy.
However, this is not the case of Belgium, which submits its decision to find some other source capable of replacing itself, or Switzerland, which considers it right to build only power plants of the last generation.
On the contrary, Great Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, China, India, Brazil to quote at least some countries continue with determination in their projects.
After Fukushima, the United States decided to expand its park by one unit by restoring the construction of one power plant interrupted after the Three Mile Islad plant accident.
If the German model, based on renewable energies, is sometimes presented as an example, reality contradicts this.
The choice of Berlin will be reflected in the increase in the price of electricity, the increased energy dependence on gas imported especially from Russia, a sharp increase in CO2 emissions caused by the construction of natural gas and coal-fired power plants.
We will feel all these unpleasant effects within a few months.
The case of Denmark merits that we reflect on it.
The European champion in wind farms, from which he consumes 30% of his electricity, is also one of the largest consumers of coal and gas due to the irregularity of wind, due to which CO2 emissions and Danish electricity prices are higher by 65% and 50%, respectively, than the European average.
On the contrary, thanks to the immutability of our energy policy, our neighbors will pay an invoice for electricity 40% higher than the payment of French households.
While in a globalised economy, energy costs represent a key factor of competitiveness and a guarantee of keeping industry in national territory, French businesses enjoy the benefits of Europe's cheapest electricity.
For this reason, any significant decrease in the share of nuclear energy would trigger a strong increase in the price of electricity, which would make illusory beautiful declarations about the re-industalization of our country and the defence of purchasing power.
The price that a large majority of French people refuse to pay, just as they fundamentally oppose this energy, as opinion polls show.
Recall that our country's energy policy has allowed the sector to build with 125,000 direct jobs and with 41,000 follow-up jobs.
A large number of small and medium-sized-scale job-creating enterprises that cannot be transferred abroad to the international markets have been heavily exported to international markets.
If France decided to leave the core, it would sacrifice a considerable number of these small and medium-sized enterprises and lose 6 billion euros a year, which the country receives for exporting nuclear facilities and services.
Which plant owner would order a nuclear reactor designed to operate for 60 years while planning the decline of its manufacturer?
In solving the energy equation, it must be admitted that there is no magical source for the production of energy, indeed that there is no diabolical one.
The development of installations producing low CO2, nuclear installations and renewable energy installations carried out by the French industrial group AREVA testifies to the complementarity of energy sources.
In the case of nuclear energy, it is an absolute necessity to maintain the highest level of safety.
In France, we have the State Office for Nuclear Safety, which conducts uncompromising and transparent oversight of existing nuclear power plants as well as the construction of a new power plant in Flamanville.
Our teams, whose professionalism is recognized, have the idea of safety in their blood.
Fifty years later, the nuclear industry has encountered three serious accidents.
The only one in Three Mile Island took place in conditions of normal operation and was left without human sacrifice and consequences for the environment.
In Chernobyl, the drama caused reactor concepts and inexcusable human errors.
In Fukushima's case, some appear to forget that the crash was the result of two natural disasters of unprecedented magnitude that caused the deaths of tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear industry will take the lessons of this crash just as it did in the two previous ones.
The fruit of this step and the co-operation between the French and German authorities dealing with nuclear safety, operators and designers is that an EPR-type reactor represents a concept capable of resisting such phenomena.
While all those who wish to build a nuclear power plant consider it, halting its construction in Flamanville would be an excellent gift to the offered competitors of the AREVA Group and EDF, and easing the way to seize a leading position in nuclear development.
It is necessary and legitimate to look for the best energy solution for our country, but it would be irresponsible to let emotions, dogmas and machinations dominate the debate so decisive for the economic, social and financial situation of our country.
Is immigration a burden or a chance for an economy?
Immigration policy under Nicolas Sarkozy has been criticised from various angles - the overwhelm of the police, the courts and the administration subjected to a policy of numbers, the compatibility of this policy with the status of a "human rights country" that France boasts about.
Recently, it was an administrative harass, the object of which was foreign students, which became current, when the interior minister (see Libération of May 23) declared that France "does not need foreign talents, bricklayers and waitresses."
But so far, it has rarely been dismantled at an angle of the economy.
It has to be said that in this area there is a rather broad agreement between the left and the right.
On the right tone in 1976, Jacques Chirac stated by his statement that "900000 unemployed should not be a problem in a country where 2 million immigrant workers are located" and Michel Rocard on the left, saying in 1990 that France "can't accept all the misery of the world."
The only difference is the degree of generosity
In 2005, the fear of an invasion by hordes of Polish plumbers on French territory was shared both on the right and to the left.
In both camps, immigrants are seen as a burden on the economy and French society.
The only difference lies in the degree of generosity that we choose to provide to immigrants.
In his program, François Hollande confines himself to banalities, stating that joint development will allow our "migration problem" to be solved.
This idea of immigration as a burden and a problem may apply in elections, but economically it is very expensive.
As economists Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Cameron recall in their recently published work synthesizing the latest knowledge about immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.): there is a broad consensus among specialists on the positive impact of migration movements on economic growth, wages and employment in countries that accept immigrants.
Restricting immigration cripples growth and is detrimental to employment.
Fears of the impact caused by migrants are based on the idea that these people can replace French workers, especially the less skilled ones, and create pressure to reduce their wages.
But the experience shows that, in fact, immigrants are much more supplemented than by substitutes for French employees by performing jobs in sectors with staff shortages.
Few skilled immigrants in sectors that do not attract French employees, and those most qualified in dynamic sectors in which education is not enough to offer places.
Positive influence on wages
Just as a surgeon will have difficulty working in a country where there is a lack of anesthesiologists, these replenishments between Frenchmen and migrants cause the arrivals of immigrants to have a positive effect on the wages and employment of French employees.
Giovanni Peri thus calculated that growth in migration movements of 1% would cause a long-term increase in real wages of between 0.6 and 0.9%.
At the same time, it does not take into account that the diversity of immigrants contributes to the creation of ideas and economic growth (a high proportion of naturalized immigrants among U.S. Nobel Prize winners; Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay, and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Migrants in European countries are also net contributors to social systems by an average of 1% of the total budget.
For example, the World Labor Organization estimates that in Germany, one immigration older than the age of 30 will bring a net contribution (income minus expenditure) of 150000 euros in its lifetime to public budgets.
Nicolas Sarkozy expressed at the beginning of his mandate the desire to go for the lack of a percentage of growth "with bared teeth."
These teeth used to deter immigrants had the opposite effect.
Europe invented democracy, it remains to think about European democracy
Democracy is a rare and very fragile commodity.
The European continent should remember this, after all, even though it was invented in Athens two thousand years ago, during the 20th century he experienced the tragedy of world wars, totalitarian regimes, the Holocaust, gulags, but also Franco in Spain, Salazara in Portugal and colonels in Greece.
Some people may have believed that in order for democracy to triumph definitively over its enemies, it will be enough to make choices and carry it out in the most direct way possible.
However, it has been known from Napoleon that it was an ease of thought.
Universal suffrage, although a necessary condition for democracy, is not a sufficient condition and can become a "tool of oppression", as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot once said.
No, democracy requires much more: public space, the public spirit, values, the organization of separation and control of powers, education, enlightenment, social and economic solidarity, justice.
Europe's crisis, which we are experiencing, is an economic crisis, but it is just like that and from the beginning the crises of politics and democracy.
Confessing the mistake of a Greek yes-no referendum in this direction will have the value of two revelations in this regard: the revelation of the necessity of the great return of political power, but also the revelation of a disturbing sense of his impossibility, his inappropriateness.
This ambivalence needs to be thought out in order to be overcome.
The truth is that the frontlines of the Democratic struggle have relocated today, and that many of the Republican fighters are no longer shooting in the right direction.
We cannot get anything if we require a series of national referendums held according to necessity and disorganisation.
Bianco exposed to a European absolutist monarch or a leap with his feet tied to the void of the nationalist depth: what a choice for a citizen!
No, we don't want guillotine knives, but we don't want joint, full-fledged, peaceful and continuous performance of choice and democracy, this time at the European level.
There aren't crises in Greece, Italy, Spain, Portugal - we wouldn't even know where to stop the list - because there's a crisis in Europe.
It is the whole of Europe bowing to the dictates of markets and credit rating agencies, which suffers from its incompetence and its lack of solidarity.
It is the whole of Europe that is humiliated on the international stage.
We are unsettled in the transfer of sovereignty to Europe - but it is a whole Europe that is slowly losing its sovereignty and, with it, each of its peoples.
The management of the European project of democratic sovereignty must urgently be restored.
Because only together will Europeans be able to reduce the burden of their debts, get rid of the rule of the markets, and prepare for the future.
The east of the crisis will assume a decision at the European level that cannot be taken without complete democratic legitimacy, otherwise it would lead us into disaster.
Having fought for a long time against this proposal by the Socialists and Jacques Delors, everyone today strongly demands a European economic government.
Well, so be it.
But to think this without stronger political and democratic integration, and experienced as such by citizens, would be a new folly and a new dead aisle.
We have to take on a new stage of European construction.
In order to create a republic in France, it was necessary to "be" republicans.
A revolution of interests couldn't have been enough.
This lesson continues to apply.
We need to build a Europe that is more economically integrated.
This can only be done by building a Europe more politically democratic and genuine European democratic sovereignty.
Let's become Europeans to build Europe.
'Contact-free' chip card is spreading
In an optimistic atmosphere, it will open on Tuesday in Villepinte, near Paris, the world smart-card fair.
According to Eurosmart’s cabinet, 2011 is expected to end with an 11% increase in the global sales of microprocessor cards (banking cards, SIM card phones) on more than 6 billion units.
The most load-bearing segment is non-contact technology, which allows you to pay in public transport or make purchases just by approaching the card to the reader.
460 million smart cards will be sold worldwide this year against 320 millions sold a year earlier!
Their share is expected to rise further in 2012 (expected 580 million units) and reach around 10% of the total smart card market (6,925 million cards).
After being in the tow of APEC countries for a long time, Europe acquires the terrain in contactless payment.
Approximately 26 million Visa bank cards are already equipped with this feature and 75,000 payment terminals are accepting them.
In France, the beginnings are more modest: 400,000 Visa contactless cards have been issued.
But the big department stores are working for two.
Carrefour released 2.5 million MasterCard contactless cards this year.
In order to include a higher gear, banks need to equip their clients with them from now on.
"Two major French businesses are committed to distributing non-touch bank cards systematically in the future to their clients," announces Gérard Nébouy, CEO of Visa Europe France.
Banks test paying by mobile
But this is only the first stage for the means of payment of the future: a mobile phone.
Only a small number of devices are equipped with NFC (Near Field Communication) technology required to carry out transactions today.
"They're delayed."
"But their true expansion should happen next year."
"For 2012, we estimate their sales in the world at between 80 and 120 million units," explains Marc Bertin, president of Eurosmart.
But banks are already preparing in advance.
Crédit agricole yesterday announced the launch of a test carried out in the city of Caen, which will run from December to June next year.
iPhone devices of approximately 200 clients and co-workers will be equipped with a case that allows contactless payments.
BPCE has a similar project in Strasbourg and Marseille.
And Société générale also announced yesterday that it is preparing to introduce an offer allowing its clients to pay with a mobile phone without contact.
The terrorist front extends from the Sahara to Nigeria
Paris is troubled by ties between Al-Qaida in the Islamic Maghreb and Boko Haram sect.
Long-term suspicion that there are unions by the Al-Qaida terrorist groupe in the Islamic Maghreb (AQMI) and Nigerian sect Boko Haram are confirmed.
On Sunday, Deputy Algerian Foreign Minister Abdelkader Messahel even acknowledged that Algiers had "sure of it from now on."
"The manner in which both organizations operate, and intelligence reports show that there is great cooperation between them," he explained, without specifying further.
Paris seems to have similar analyses.
An investigation into the kidnapping of two Frenchmen in Niger in January 2010 could help illuminate connecting bridges.
After beigning members of AQMI from one restaurant in Niamey, Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory were to be killed at the same time as several of their captors in an operation conducted by special French troops.
French and Nigerian investigators were supposed to find several phones with chips belonging to terrorists.
A terrorist axis
The analysis of the calls leads to several persons in Mali, Niger and Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), investigators are particularly interested in two numbers: one leads to a Nigerian who has long resided in Maiduguri, a city in the north of Nigeria that is Boko Haram's cradle, and the other to a man who is said to be close to AQMI and a sect.
“It’s progress, but there’s no need to wait for more compelling evidence before we talk about operational cooperation between AQMI and Boko Haram.”
"But it's possible."
“We’ve known for years that the sect has enjoyed external financial and intellectual support, and that it’s becoming more and more active,” said Kunle Amuwo, a Nigerian investigator.
In recent months, this "support" has become more and more evident in Boko Haram's developments and manifestations, or in its operating practices, which from now on include human sacrifices or increasingly complex explosive devices of the same mode of execution as devices used by AQMI.
Since then, the international community has put pressure on Nigeria to take into account the problems raised by Boko Haram.
In fact, the Nigerian authorities have for a long time regarded the group as a community of enlightened mystics of little worthy of interest.
Quick success
Boko Haram was founded in 2002 in Maiduguri around a mosque, school and fundamentalist Mohammed Yusuf. Boko Haram, which in haoussa language means "Western upbringing is a sin," approving the establishment of an Islamic emirate in the Muslim north of Nigeria.
In this poor region, such a connection has a rapid success.
The sect is politicised and requires strict observance of Sharia Islamic law, while its supporters begin the fight against the anti-central government.
These people focus first on churches, bars and authorities.
In 2009, Boko Haram supporters become extraordinarily active, multiplying their attacks against police stations.
The government's reaction, as usual, is still the same.
The army will encircle the city and the result is 800 deaths.
Sect Founder Mohammed Yusuf is captured and killed in prison.
From that moment on, the group becomes radicalized and leaves its location in the north.
At Christmas 2010 he will carry out an assassination in the city of Jos, in which 80 people will be killed, followed by others in the capital, Abuja.
In June, it's in Abuja again, where Boko Haram attacks police headquarters in their first suicide attack.
And in August, the U.N
The creation of a terrorist axis in Africa that should lead from Mauritania via Nigeria to Somalia now worries all security specialists.
Now that Al-Qaida of the Islamic Maghreb has encircled Mali and iger and the Somalis of the Al-Shabaab movement are operating in Kenya, Boko Haram emerges as a new danger to the continent.
Last week in Nigeria during an interview with President Goodluck Jonathan Alain Juppé said France was on standby and ready to "pass all the information."
The world according to presidential candidates
On issues related to China, Iran or helping Israel, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain and Rick Perry arrive in simplification and ignorance.
Can America, weakened by a crisis, manage the world through simple or even simplistic ideas?
Listening to presidential candidates, voters might think so.
For several days now, Mitt Romney, Herman Cain, Rick Perry and others have been repeating vocume statements and concise guidance on foreign policy, which has traditionally been a strong point for Republicans.
Frequently unfounded slogans about China, Iran or helping Israel merge among themselves.
Mitt Romney, the best-placed candidate in the race to nominate the Republican "Grand Old Party," has threatened by vocating the Chinese before the World Trade Organization and their allegations "for manipulating their currency," which is a major theme intended in America by migrating entire parts of its industry towards Asia.
All the worse, if this must include the launch of a trade war, he said.
Jon Huntsman, Obama's former ambassador to China and the only candidate with some sophisticated vision of foreign policy, has almost stalled at an attitude that "smoothers" emotions.
He demanded tough but constructive dialogue with Beijing (President Obama's current stance).
But Huntsman, who is in last place in election estimates, remains unlistened.
The Picture of President Reagan
Romney has also pledged military strikes on Iran if sanctions fail in halting its nuclear program.
"If I'm elected, I'll imprison Iran," he declared in a fan-french manner.
"If Obama is re-elected, they'll have a bomb."
He also pledged to increase military aid to Israel, accusing Obama of disappointing in his responsibilities to this partner.
Entrepreneur Herman Cain, the second best-placed candidate, demonstrates an embarrassing ignorance of the documents.
He recently called for a sharp stance against China's military threat because Beijing is "trying to develop nuclear capabilities" - evidently unfamiliar with the fact that China has possessed an atomic weapon since 1964!
Texas Governor Rick Perry, who supports Israel and wants strikes against Iran, says he is ready to send the U.S. military to Mexico to fight drug cartels.
The use of torture against the presumed terrorists banned by Obama in 2009 is also recommended by Cain, Perry as well as another candidate, Michele Bachmann.
They're all far elsewhere than Republican nominee John McCain, a heavyweight in foreign policy in 2008.
Adversaries of the current president say that Reagan also had simple ideas, and yet he won the Cold War.
They recall that Obama himself was an amateur and that he added water to his wine in the fight against terrorism.
They emphasize the failure of his naïve dialogue with Iran, the hasty departure of "boys" from Iraq, his excuses regarding Libya and thus attribute to him the label of the weak
The attack is not easy.
The population is judging President Obama's national security policy, which removed Ben Laden, rather positively.
From Iraq, it has no confidence in military interventions that lead to mischief.
And with the argument that America needs to be reconstructed economically in order to resuscitate its leadership, Obama is knocking into a more correct place.
Nord Stream, a new entry port of Russian gas to Europe
The Nord Stream pipeline connecting directly Eastern Europe to Russia and led along the bottom of the Baltic Sea began to function.
It must supply gas equal to the equivalent of 30 million households.
Gazprom, a Russian gas giant, wanted to celebrate the event properly.
More than 500 guests, among them 4 heads of state and government, took part last Tuesday inaugurating Nord Stream, a new pipeline that will connect Russia and Germany directly under the sea.
Symbolically German Chancellor Angela Merkel, Russian President Dmitry Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon and his Dutch colleague Mark Rutte turned jointly around the valve controlling the entry of Russian gas into the Western European network.
With this new pipeline, 27.5 billion additional cubic meters of Russian gas will start flowing every year through the first pipeline of 1,224 km, linking the region of St. Petersburg with the federal state of Mecklemburg-Pomerania.
The second pipeline, which must be completed at the end of 2012, will make it possible to supply a total of 55 billion cubic meters of gas, an amount equal to the whole of French consumption.
The possibility to build a third pipeline on the same route is already being recalled.
For Gazprom, the timely completion of this huge construction has the character of victory.
Poland and the Baltic countries strongly objected to the project.
In fact, Nord Stream will make it possible to supply gas directly to Germany without being routed around the country by a third state (Poland or Ukraine).
At the same time, these two countries are losing somewhat on power: the possibility of pushing Russia with threats to close its access to Western European markets is becoming more difficult for them.
This was strongly expressed by Warsaw when it did not hesitate to compare Nord Stream to the "new German-Soviet Pact", citing the alliance between Hitler and Stalin during the occupation of Poland in 1939.
It means saying it's a politically sensitive issue.
Western Europe, with its support for this Russian project, gained more certainty of its purchases.
But it's at the cost of weakening Europe's energy, because the project has created rifts among Europeans from the East and West.
Basely 5 years ago, Russia managed to assert its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and wealthy Western states with high gas consumption whose deposits are coming to an end.
“This pipeline is part of our partnership with Russia, a new artery that connects us organically,” François Fillon confirmed at the opening ceremony of this pipeline, demonstrating at what point the big gas contracts are both political and economic.
All of this explains the broad smiles of Gazprom leaders present in large numbers in Lubmin, the gas supply point.
The construction of this pipeline is also an important technical act.
The undersea piping is made of concrete sheathing steel to protect it from the anchors of naval vessels.
It rests at the bottom of the sea at an average depth of 200 m.
During its construction it was necessary to avoid minefields dating from World War 2.
“Because of security, almost a hundred of them had to be removed or destroyed,” says Jens Müller, a spokesperson for Nord Stream.
It was also necessary to increase measures for the protection of fauna and flora.
The pipeline itself is a long metal snake weighing like 242 Eiffel Towers, which, along its entire length, is not interrupted by valves or maintenance stations.
One control platform should have been built near Sweden, but the project leaders rejected it when the Swedes were little lustful to see the Russian state-reading society permanently settling off their coast.
"In the event of a fault, we stop the passage of gas in less than 1 minute," Jens Müller specifies.
In the case of leakage, the gas contained in the pipeline escapes into the air.
But this case is considered by the builder to be unlikely, after all, the welds were designed so that leakage would arise once every 100 000 years.
The inspection of the condition of the pipeline from the outside is carried out by an undersea robot drawn by a ship, while the inside of the pipeline is inspected by a device circulating inside.
This cigar-shaped device with a diameter of 1 meter is inserted into the interior of the pipeline and is pushed by gas.
At the destination over a full three-day journey speed of 3 m/s will provide accurate information about possible deformations of metal parts.
The long-distance pipeline has been designed to operate for fifty years without the need for the slightest repair.
The circulation of the gas is simply ensured by pressure.
The value of this pressure is 200 bar at the start and 100 bar at the finish.
Five companies joined forces for the implementation of this project.
Gazprom, which holds a 51% stake, remains the leading position.
German companies E.ON and BASF (15.5% each), as well as the Dutch company Gasunie and the French GDF Suez Group (9% each).
These five companies formed a joint venture called Nord Stream in charge of the construction and operation of the pipeline.
The company is headquartered in Zoug, Switzerland, where taxation is extremely advantageous.
Gazprom subsequently leased this company for five years with a contract, which can be renewed, all its capacities for gas transport.
"Gazprom will profit from the total capacity, whatever the amount of actually transported gas," explains Jens Müller.
The financing of the construction of the pipeline was ensured by 30% shareholders and 70% by bank loans.
The total cost of the works should reach 7.4 billion euros for both pipelines.
For GDF Suez, investment costs have risen to 240 million.
But minority shareholders are certain that their costs will return to them, because only Gazprom takes the risk.
The pipeline rises from the Baltic Sea in Lubmin on a sandy coastle with pine forests.
Valves 4.5 meters high form the output gate of gas.
The sensor measures the pressure, temperature and other characteristics of the incoming gas before being transported to German pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, this terminal receives one million cubic meters of Russian gas every hour.
By reaching full capacity, it will be 3 million per hour and 6 million in 2012.
Moleskine - branded notebook
Founded fifty years ago, Moleskine wants to provide the joy of writing once again.
It brings the smell of adventure, hours spent writing in the light of a candle.
Moleskine notebook is easily recognizable with its black rectangular boards and with its leaves of faded white color, as if aged over time.
After all, Moleskine's notebook, as we know it today, was born in 1997.
At that time, the Milan-based Italian company Modo Modo decides to launch a new design but distinguished notebook.
Maria Sebregondi, then a consultant working for the company, takes up the introduction of a new product.
The notebook is inspired by the old "moleskin" production technique (imitation of leather), which is, for example, used for adjusting the surface of benches.
This type of notebook was greatly appreciated by writers and travelers.
“Moleskine has returned an old and forgotten object that was an icon of avant-garde artists until the 20th century,” says Maria Sebregondi, who has since become the brand’s leading figure at Modo Modo.
Success is here.
Between 2006 and 2010, sales rose by 26% to settle at 12.7 million units last year.
The situation is distant secret production from the first years, when only 3 000 notebooks were made.
Moleskine crosses Italy's borders and exports to 70 countries from France to the United States.
It is difficult to create an artificial portrait of the buyer, because the notebook attracts a businessman as well as a student or professionals.
The image of Moleskine's notebook includes that its owners are cosmopolitan, cultured and open-minded people.
The small Italian company, which had 12 employees in its beginnings, now has almost one hundred of them, and since 2008 it has two offices, one of which is in Milan and the other in New York.
He sends his notebooks to the bookstore and through the FNAC store chain to small boutiques in the centers of cities, where he was able to assert his identity.
“We’ve been following the spirit of the times and this need for new writing, through the tumultuous development of e-mails,” explains Maria Sebregondi.
This cultural signature accompanies the development of Moleskine's notebook: the brand has recently opened an exhibition "Cházka", where architects or artists exhibit their notebook.
In August, on the occasion of the Locarno Film Festival, she also unveiled new notebook records reminiscent of a leopard.
Moleskine profits from its success and this cultural aura and diversifies its products: bags, diaries, pencils, laptop cases ... The brand extends to a variety of carriers.
In the autumn of 2006, Modo Modo was bought by SG Capital Europe investment funds, today Syntegra Capital, writing a new side in Moleskine’s history.
Why is there such a high proportion of unemployed disabled workers?
Around 19% of disabled people are looking for work.
Today opens the 15th week for the employment of the handicapped.
Despite the voluntaristic public policy established in 2005, the percentage of unemployed handicapped persons is still twice as high as the average, i.e. 19% compared to 9% for the entire population.
Is it daunting?
"No, underscores Éric Blanchet, CEO of Adapt, which organizes the Weekhond for the Employment of the Disabled."
"Progresses made in 15 years are remarkable."
"At that time, the percentage of unemployed wasn't twice, but three times higher."
"However, there is still much to do."
It must be said that there are numerous obstacles for unemployed disabled people prior to their entry into work.
These are elderly: 37% are more than 50 years old (17% on average).
They have low qualifications: 80% do not have graduates, and out of 2.3 million university students there are only 10 000 disabled.
Finally, long-term unemployment is very widespread: 53% for the handicapped versus 38% on average.
In short, such are the criteria that particularly complicate the return to employment and which represent the "core of the problem," according to Christian Grapin, director-general of Tremplin, which was founded in 1992, to help businesses recruit disabled workers.
So much when it comes to objective brakes.
There remains unspoken, ways of thinking that are certainly evolving, but too slowly.
A study conducted in April by IMS-Entreprendre pour la Cité found that interviewed executives have "a weak level of knowledge about handicapped persons" with "exaggerated notion of people in a mobile chair and psychological or mental deficiencies."
As a result, private businesses with more than 20 employees are still far from achieving the goal of employing 6% of the disabled workers set out in the 1978 law.
The last known numbers, which come from 2008, indicate a state equal to 2.6%.
“We’re around 3% right now,” says Pierre Blanc, director-general of Agefiph, a company responsible for levying fees on businesses that fail to comply.
Evidence is the "fined" number of enterprises, as well as the amounts paid, which fell between 2007 and 2011 from 59,000 of the offending enterprises to 46,000 (and from 606 to 480 million euros).
“The 2005 law, which increased contributions, created strong pressure on businesses, Pierre Blanc explains.”
"But this improvement also demonstrates true awareness, especially in large enterprises."
According to Labor Department, only 59% of businesses with 20 to 49 employees employ disabled workers versus 82% of businesses with 100 to 199 employees.
Gambler: "What happiness"
Before they challenge the French representatives (Bleus), Eden Hazard and Georges Leekens on Tuesday night (20h45) did not hide the pleasure that this friendly match will bring them.
We're not going to go so far as to write that there was an association between them, but it's a symbolic picture.
Sitting next to each other in the grandstand of the Stade de France auditorium, Georges Leekens and Eden Hazard reconciled.
Five months after his striker's angry gesture in the match against Turkey, the Belgian coach gave only words of praise to his player.
“I am very satisfied with Eden and his progress, even with the fact that he loves his country.”
"I want to move forward," assures the French Lille player.
In terms of the difference between his performances in club matches and the matches of the national team, "it's one and the same, it justifies itself."
"I've been in Lille for a whole year, which isn't the case with Belgium."
"I need to clear my mark."
Hazard, spared against Romania on Thursday night because of this match against France, is coming to the stadium in Saint-Denis to finally "shine."
It is impossible to omit the concept of "joy."
Play against Bleus, which he encouraged from the age of 7 and in their victorious campaigns in 1998 and 2000.
"I've always been a fan," he argues.
This necessarily contributes even more to the "special" nature of this meeting.
All the more so, because opposite "are Mathieu (Debuchy), Yohan (Cabaye) and Adil (Rami)", he reminds.
"We crissed immediately."
"To be able to play against them, it's a pleasure!"
Good-hearted Leekens also recalled "pride".
"Pride" to fight "match - holiday".
"Pride" also stated in the quality of "neighbors" that France became a very high-level team a little more than a year after a trauma in the southern African city of Knysna."
"Laurent Blanc has done a tremendous amount of work," he sighed.
Although Belgium will be absent at the next European Championship, the technical footballer would like to see himself in 2014 on a plane with the French towards... Brazil.
"Our little Belgians will become big," he underlined.
"We're building something right now, the players are ambitious, they're having healthy hunger."
"You'll realize it for the next matches." Including that against France.
Portugal very confident
Portuguese coach Paulo Bento starts the barrage against Bosnia Bosnia (22h00) this Wednesday in Lisbon with great optimism.
On the way there, both teams lacked realism.
“At the end of those 90 minutes we will be a happy team and, above all, a happy country.”
Paulo Bento is already seen in Poland and Ukraine.
There is no shadow of doubt for him. The Portuguese national football team, whose helm has been since 2010, will qualify for Euro 2012.
In fact, Portugal is 90 minutes away from happiness.
It remains to play one match and one opponent - Bosnia.
Knowing Friday's result (0-0) in the hell of Bosnian Zenica, it seems clear that nothing has been won.
Portugal certainly had more than an hour superiority, but he wasn't able to score such a rare goal to relieve him.
"It's a good result, which nonetheless contains a risk because we didn't score a goal outside."
But at home at their amazing Stade de la Luz in Lisbon, the Portuguese are convinced that they will advance in front of the audience, who should create a boiling cauldron there, even if the match definitely appears "very balanced," says the optimist, but sharply considering Bento.
“We expect a very balanced match, but we are completely convinced that we can have the upper hand,” said the forty-two-year-old Portuguese coach.
"We still have a 50% chance of being at Euro 2012," relativatized his successor, Carlos Queiroz.
An indecisive Friday's game in Bosnia forces the Portuguese to score a goal and win.
An indecisive match can be enough for the Bosnians, who also need to score a goal.
"It's a good result, which nonetheless contains a risk because we didn't score a goal outside," Bento regrets.
"We will play against a team that has great technical qualities and which has players with great individual qualities," he added.
As in Zenica, the Portuguese will have an interest in putting maximum pressure on the Bosnian team right from the start so that "they won't give them time to think."
A good idea, on the condition that it turns out to be realistic this time.
